const TOPIC_CHECKS = {"MOL-1:guide-1":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-1","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:0","question":"Why do polar groups often increase an organic molecule’s water solubility?","options":["They can make favorable electrostatic or hydrogen-bond interactions with water","They prevent all molecular motion","They must always give the molecule a net negative charge","They make every carbon atom nonpolar"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Water has partial charges, so suitable polar groups interact favorably with its dipoles.","reasons":["Water has partial charges, so suitable polar groups interact favorably with its dipoles.","Solubility concerns intermolecular interactions, not complete arrest of motion.","A neutral polar group can still hydrogen-bond with water.","Adding a polar group does not eliminate its own polarity."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":3}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following is a universal characteristic of water-soluble organic compounds? A) They contain polar groups that can form hydrogen bonds with water. B) They are composed exclusively of carbon and hydrogen C) They are insoluble in aqueous environments D) They contain long hydrocarbon chains E) They are nonpolar and hydrophobic Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Qualified the source’s universal characteristic claim: solubility depends on the whole molecule, not one feature alone.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following is a universal characteristic of water-soluble organic compounds? A) They contain polar groups that can form hydrogen bonds with water. B) They are composed exclusively of carbon and hydrogen C) They are insoluble in aqueous environments D) They contain long hydrocarbon chains E) They are nonpolar and hydrophobic Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-1:guide-1"},{"id":"expanded-application-1-4","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-3","question":"Two alcohols each have one hydroxyl group, but one has a much longer hydrocarbon chain. What is generally expected as the nonpolar chain grows?","options":["Water solubility tends to decrease because nonpolar surface becomes more important","Water solubility must rise because there are more C–H donors","The molecule necessarily gains a full positive charge","The hydroxyl group stops containing oxygen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Solubility reflects the whole molecule. A single polar hydroxyl may not compensate for a large nonpolar region, so increasing hydrocarbon size generally reduces favorable interaction with water.","reasons":["Solubility reflects the whole molecule. A single polar hydroxyl may not compensate for a large nonpolar region, so increasing hydrocarbon size generally reduces favorable interaction with water.","Ordinary C–H groups do not provide a growing set of strong hydrogen-bond donors.","A longer hydrocarbon chain alone does not create an ionic charge.","The hydroxyl retains oxygen; its relative contribution is outweighed rather than chemically erased."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":13}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-1"}],"MOL-1:guide-2":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-1:slides-2026-0-0","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-0","question":"Which statement best explains bent water geometry?","options":["Water polarity depends on electronegativity and a bent molecular shape","Unequal electron sharing gives water partial charges","Water can donate two and accept two hydrogen bonds","A phospholipid combines a polar head with nonpolar tails"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The O–H bonds are polar because oxygen attracts the shared electrons more strongly. Their dipoles do not cancel in a bent molecule. These are partial charges, not complete separation into oxygen and hydrogen ions.","reasons":["Correct. The O–H bonds are polar because oxygen attracts the shared electrons more strongly. Their dipoles do not cancel in a bent molecule. These are partial charges, not complete separation into oxygen and hydrogen ions.","This explains Water polarity. Oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen. Oxygen is partly negative and the hydrogens partly positive. This lets water interact with ions and polar groups. A nonpolar hydrocarbon cannot replace those favorable interactions. For the concept asked here, Water polarity depends on electronegativity and a bent molecular shape.","This explains Hydrogen bonding. The two hydrogen atoms can donate hydrogen bonds, while oxygen has two lone pairs that accept them. These are intermolecular attractions, not the covalent O–H bonds within water. Methyl groups lack suitable polar atoms. For the concept asked here, Water polarity depends on electronegativity and a bent molecular shape.","This explains Amphipathic molecules. Amphipathic means both water-friendly and water-avoiding regions exist in one molecule. In a bilayer, heads face water and tails cluster away from it. The head and tails have different roles; neither region alone describes the whole molecule. For the concept asked here, Water polarity depends on electronegativity and a bent molecular shape."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":9},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":5}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-2"},{"id":"expanded-application-1-1","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:0","question":"A hypothetical water molecule has the usual electronegativity difference but is perfectly linear and symmetric. Which conclusion best explains why real water has a net dipole?","options":["Its bent geometry prevents the two O–H bond dipoles from canceling","Its oxygen carries a full −2 ionic charge in every molecule","Its hydrogens have no electrons","Its O–H covalent bonds are actually intermolecular hydrogen bonds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Real water has polar covalent O–H bonds and a bent geometry. Oxygen attracts shared electrons more strongly, and the bond dipoles do not cancel. Molecular shape matters in addition to bond polarity.","reasons":["Real water has polar covalent O–H bonds and a bent geometry. Oxygen attracts shared electrons more strongly, and the bond dipoles do not cancel. Molecular shape matters in addition to bond polarity.","Partial charge is not a full −2 ionic charge; the molecule is overall electrically neutral.","The electrons are shared unequally, not completely absent from the hydrogen side.","O–H covalent bonds hold one water molecule together; hydrogen bonds describe a different interaction."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":9}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-2"}],"MOL-1:guide-3":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-1:guide-3-1","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:1","question":"Two intact water molecules lose the attraction holding them next to each other. Which bond or interaction has been disrupted?","options":["A peptide bond between the water molecules","A hydrogen bond between the molecules","An O–H covalent bond inside each molecule","The electron-sharing bond holding oxygen to itself"],"answer":1,"reasons":["Peptide bonds join amino-acid residues and are not the interaction between two water molecules.","A hydrogen bond can connect separate water molecules; disrupting it need not break either molecule.","Breaking an O–H covalent bond would change the chemical structure of a water molecule.","The stated event concerns attraction between two molecules, not an oxygen–oxygen covalent structure."],"explanation":"A hydrogen bond can connect separate water molecules; disrupting it need not break either molecule. Distinguish intramolecular covalent structure from intermolecular association. Many weak interactions can have a substantial combined stabilizing effect.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":10}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-1:guide-3"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-1:guide-3-2","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:1","question":"Why can hydrogen bonding contribute substantially to a biological structure even though one hydrogen bond is relatively weak?","options":["Many hydrogen bonds can act together to stabilize the structure","Every hydrogen bond permanently becomes a covalent bond","A hydrogen bond cannot be broken under biological conditions","Hydrogen bonds split water molecules into their constituent atoms"],"answer":0,"reasons":["The combined stabilizing contribution of many weak interactions can be substantial.","A hydrogen bond remains a distinct noncovalent interaction; stabilization does not require conversion into a covalent bond.","Hydrogen bonds are reversible, allowing molecular partners to change.","Breaking an intermolecular hydrogen bond does not split the water molecule into atoms."],"explanation":"The combined stabilizing contribution of many weak interactions can be substantial. Distinguish intramolecular covalent structure from intermolecular association. Many weak interactions can have a substantial combined stabilizing effect.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":10}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-1:guide-3"}],"MOL-1:guide-4":[{"id":"mol-slides-1-application-2","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-1","question":"Which functional group supplies a common hydrogen-bond acceptor?","options":["A C–H bond","A C–C bond","A carbonyl oxygen","A nonpolar hydrocarbon chain"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The carbonyl oxygen has available electron density that can accept a hydrogen bond.","reasons":["A typical C–H bond is not the standard acceptor tested here.","A C–C bond supplies neither the electronegative atom nor the relevant lone pair.","The carbonyl oxygen can accept a hydrogen bond.","A hydrocarbon chain lacks the polar acceptor group."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":19}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-4"},{"id":"expanded-application-1-2","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-1","question":"Which pair correctly assigns a usual hydrogen-bond role to each group?","options":["Carbonyl oxygen: acceptor; amide N–H: donor","Carbonyl carbon: acceptor; alkyl C–H: strong donor","Amide nitrogen: ordinary strong acceptor; oxygen-free alkane: donor","Carbonyl oxygen: donor even when it has no attached hydrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A carbonyl oxygen has available electron pairs and can accept a hydrogen bond. An amide N–H can donate its appropriately bonded hydrogen. The amide nitrogen itself is not a usual acceptor because its electron pair is delocalized.","reasons":["A carbonyl oxygen has available electron pairs and can accept a hydrogen bond. An amide N–H can donate its appropriately bonded hydrogen. The amide nitrogen itself is not a usual acceptor because its electron pair is delocalized.","The electron-poor carbonyl carbon is not the usual acceptor, and ordinary alkyl C–H is not a standard strong donor.","An amide nitrogen lacks the usual available acceptor lone pair, and an alkane lacks a suitable donor group.","Donation requires an appropriate bonded hydrogen; a carbonyl oxygen does not supply one."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":11}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-4"}],"MOL-1:guide-5":[{"id":"expanded-application-1-3","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-2","question":"A sodium ion is placed in water. Which orientation is favored in its first hydration shell?","options":["The oxygen ends of surrounding water molecules face Na+","The hydrogen ends face Na+ because both are positive","Water must lose a hydrogen atom before approaching Na+","No orientation occurs because water has no overall charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ion attracts the opposite partial charge of water: Na+ is positive and the oxygen region is partially negative. This ion–dipole interaction does not require water to become a fully charged ion.","reasons":["The ion attracts the opposite partial charge of water: Na+ is positive and the oxygen region is partially negative. This ion–dipole interaction does not require water to become a fully charged ion.","The hydrogen ends are partially positive and are not the preferred side facing a cation.","Hydration is an interaction of intact water molecules and does not require covalent bond cleavage.","A neutral molecule can still have an oriented dipole and interact with ions."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":12}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-5"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-1:guide-5-1","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:1","question":"Which orientation is favored when water molecules surround a chloride ion, Cl−?","options":["Water molecules must first lose all their hydrogen atoms","Water cannot hydrate an ion unless it forms a peptide bond","The partially positive hydrogen ends point toward Cl−","The partially negative oxygen ends point toward Cl−"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Hydration involves orientation and interaction of intact water molecules, not obligatory removal of their hydrogens.","Hydration of ions does not require peptide bonds.","The anion attracts the partially positive hydrogen side of water through ion–dipole interactions.","The oxygen side is partially negative and is instead favored toward a positive ion such as Na+."],"explanation":"The anion attracts the partially positive hydrogen side of water through ion–dipole interactions. Cations attract the oxygen side of water. Polar uncharged solutes can be hydrated through hydrogen bonding.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":12}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-1:guide-5"}],"MOL-1:guide-6":[{"id":"expanded-application-1-4","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-3","question":"Two alcohols each have one hydroxyl group, but one has a much longer hydrocarbon chain. What is generally expected as the nonpolar chain grows?","options":["Water solubility tends to decrease because nonpolar surface becomes more important","Water solubility must rise because there are more C–H donors","The molecule necessarily gains a full positive charge","The hydroxyl group stops containing oxygen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Solubility reflects the whole molecule. A single polar hydroxyl may not compensate for a large nonpolar region, so increasing hydrocarbon size generally reduces favorable interaction with water.","reasons":["Solubility reflects the whole molecule. A single polar hydroxyl may not compensate for a large nonpolar region, so increasing hydrocarbon size generally reduces favorable interaction with water.","Ordinary C–H groups do not provide a growing set of strong hydrogen-bond donors.","A longer hydrocarbon chain alone does not create an ionic charge.","The hydroxyl retains oxygen; its relative contribution is outweighed rather than chemically erased."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":13}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-6"},{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-1:slides-2026-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-3","question":"Which statement best explains water and nonpolar surfaces?","options":["Increasing a nonpolar hydrocarbon region generally reduces favorable water interactions","Unequal electron sharing gives water partial charges","Water can donate two and accept two hydrogen bonds","A phospholipid combines a polar head with nonpolar tails"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A long carbon–hydrogen region cannot form the same favorable hydrogen bonds as a polar group. Amphipathic molecules combine different regions; labeling the whole molecule from one atom alone can be misleading.","reasons":["Correct. A long carbon–hydrogen region cannot form the same favorable hydrogen bonds as a polar group. Amphipathic molecules combine different regions; labeling the whole molecule from one atom alone can be misleading.","This explains Water polarity. Oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen. Oxygen is partly negative and the hydrogens partly positive. This lets water interact with ions and polar groups. A nonpolar hydrocarbon cannot replace those favorable interactions. For the concept asked here, Increasing a nonpolar hydrocarbon region generally reduces favorable water interactions.","This explains Hydrogen bonding. The two hydrogen atoms can donate hydrogen bonds, while oxygen has two lone pairs that accept them. These are intermolecular attractions, not the covalent O–H bonds within water. Methyl groups lack suitable polar atoms. For the concept asked here, Increasing a nonpolar hydrocarbon region generally reduces favorable water interactions.","This explains Amphipathic molecules. Amphipathic means both water-friendly and water-avoiding regions exist in one molecule. In a bilayer, heads face water and tails cluster away from it. The head and tails have different roles; neither region alone describes the whole molecule. For the concept asked here, Increasing a nonpolar hydrocarbon region generally reduces favorable water interactions."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":13},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":17}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-6"}],"MOL-1:guide-7":[{"id":"expanded-application-1-5","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:2","question":"An amphipathic lipid forms a bilayer in water. Which arrangement is most consistent with its chemistry?","options":["Polar heads face water and nonpolar tails form an interior region","All tails face water while all heads are buried together","Each lipid must be hydrolyzed before assembly","The bilayer is held together only by covalent bonds joining neighboring lipids"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The hydrophobic effect favors shielding nonpolar tails from water while hydrophilic heads interact with the aqueous surroundings. Bilayer assembly therefore does not require creating a covalent polymer from all lipids.","reasons":["The hydrophobic effect favors shielding nonpolar tails from water while hydrophilic heads interact with the aqueous surroundings. Bilayer assembly therefore does not require creating a covalent polymer from all lipids.","Exposing tails and burying heads would oppose their usual solvent preferences.","Hydrolysis is not a requirement for spontaneous amphipathic assembly and can destroy the lipid.","Many noncovalent and solvent-dependent interactions organize membranes rather than a single covalent lipid chain."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":14}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-7"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-25","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:2","question":"Why does a phospholipid’s combination of a polar head and nonpolar tails support a stable bilayer in water?","options":["The bilayer requires peptide bonds between every lipid","Tails form more hydrogen bonds with water than heads","Heads contact water while tails cluster away from water","All parts of a phospholipid are equally hydrophilic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Phospholipids are amphipathic. Their hydrophobic effect drives tails inward while polar heads remain exposed to water.","reasons":["Bilayers assemble mainly through noncovalent interactions rather than peptide cross-links.","Hydrocarbon tails lack the polar groups needed for extensive hydrogen bonding.","This arrangement minimizes unfavorable exposure of the nonpolar tails.","The head and tails have different interactions with water."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":6}],"originalQuestion":"A head (hydrophilic) of a phospholipid in the cell membrane was removed, leaving the tail *hydrophobic) exposed. What is the effect? A) The cell would not be able to import proteins B) No change C) The new interactions would strengthen the cell membrane D) Interruptions in the membrane’s integrity due to interactions between hydrophobic and hydrophilic molecules. Answer: A","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed the ambiguous head-removal question into the underlying membrane-organization concept; original key A is not generally justified.","sourceExcerpt":"A head (hydrophilic) of a phospholipid in the cell membrane was removed, leaving the tail *hydrophobic) exposed. What is the effect? A) The cell would not be able to import proteins B) No change C) The new interactions would strengthen the cell membrane D) Interruptions in the membrane’s integrity due to interactions between hydrophobic and hydrophilic molecules. Answer: A","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":6,"number":25,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-25"}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-7"}],"MOL-1:guide-8":[{"id":"drill-MOL-1-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:3","question":"Which statement best explains ph change?","options":["A tenfold increase in hydrogen ions lowers pH by one","Unequal electron sharing gives water partial charges","Water can donate two and accept two hydrogen bonds","A phospholipid combines a polar head with nonpolar tails"],"answer":0,"explanation":"pH equals minus log10 of hydrogen-ion activity. If hydrogen ions rise from 10^-7 to 10^-6, pH falls from 7 to 6. The logarithmic scale means a one-unit change is a tenfold change, not a ten-percent change.","reasons":["Correct. pH equals minus log10 of hydrogen-ion activity. If hydrogen ions rise from 10^-7 to 10^-6, pH falls from 7 to 6. The logarithmic scale means a one-unit change is a tenfold change, not a ten-percent change.","This explains Water polarity. Oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen. Oxygen is partly negative and the hydrogens partly positive. This lets water interact with ions and polar groups. A nonpolar hydrocarbon cannot replace those favorable interactions. For ph change, use the rule: A tenfold increase in hydrogen ions lowers pH by one.","This explains Hydrogen bonding. The two hydrogen atoms can donate hydrogen bonds, while oxygen has two lone pairs that accept them. These are intermolecular attractions, not the covalent O–H bonds within water. Methyl groups lack suitable polar atoms. For ph change, use the rule: A tenfold increase in hydrogen ions lowers pH by one.","This explains Amphipathic molecules. Amphipathic means both water-friendly and water-avoiding regions exist in one molecule. In a bilayer, heads face water and tails cluster away from it. The head and tails have different roles; neither region alone describes the whole molecule. For ph change, use the rule: A tenfold increase in hydrogen ions lowers pH by one."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":3},{"source":"mol-past","page":4},{"source":"mol-past","page":5}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-8"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-1:guide-8-1","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:3","question":"In the ordinary course approximation, what quantity is used to calculate pH?","options":["The positive base-10 logarithm of hydrogen-ion concentration","The total number of oxygen atoms in a sample","The ratio of sodium ions to chloride ions","The negative base-10 logarithm of hydrogen-ion concentration"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Omitting the negative sign would reverse the relation between acidity and the reported number.","Oxygen-atom count does not specify the hydrogen-ion concentration used in the pH definition.","The sodium/chloride ratio is not the definition of pH.","The standard introductory expression is pH = −log10[H+]."],"explanation":"The standard introductory expression is pH = −log10[H+]. pH = −log10[H+] in the usual course approximation. A tenfold increase in H+ lowers pH by one unit.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":15}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-1:guide-8"}],"MOL-1:guide-9":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-1:slides-2026-4-0","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-4","question":"Which statement best explains acids and bases?","options":["A Brønsted acid donates a proton and a Brønsted base accepts one","Unequal electron sharing gives water partial charges","Water can donate two and accept two hydrogen bonds","A phospholipid combines a polar head with nonpolar tails"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The identity of the conjugate pair follows the transfer of H+. Water can participate on either side in suitable reactions. A base need not contain an OH group in its formula to accept a proton.","reasons":["Correct. The identity of the conjugate pair follows the transfer of H+. Water can participate on either side in suitable reactions. A base need not contain an OH group in its formula to accept a proton.","This explains Water polarity. Oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen. Oxygen is partly negative and the hydrogens partly positive. This lets water interact with ions and polar groups. A nonpolar hydrocarbon cannot replace those favorable interactions. For the concept asked here, A Brønsted acid donates a proton and a Brønsted base accepts one.","This explains Hydrogen bonding. The two hydrogen atoms can donate hydrogen bonds, while oxygen has two lone pairs that accept them. These are intermolecular attractions, not the covalent O–H bonds within water. Methyl groups lack suitable polar atoms. For the concept asked here, A Brønsted acid donates a proton and a Brønsted base accepts one.","This explains Amphipathic molecules. Amphipathic means both water-friendly and water-avoiding regions exist in one molecule. In a bilayer, heads face water and tails cluster away from it. The head and tails have different roles; neither region alone describes the whole molecule. For the concept asked here, A Brønsted acid donates a proton and a Brønsted base accepts one."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":16},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":7}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-9"},{"id":"expanded-application-1-7","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-4","question":"For HA ⇌ H+ + A−, which statement identifies the conjugate base correctly?","options":["A− is the species remaining after HA donates a proton","H+ is the conjugate base because it is positive","HA and A− must differ by several electrons","Any concentrated solution is automatically the conjugate base"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A conjugate acid–base pair differs by one proton. When HA donates H+, the remaining A− can accept a proton and regenerate HA. Concentration and acid/base identity are distinct concepts.","reasons":["A conjugate acid–base pair differs by one proton. When HA donates H+, the remaining A− can accept a proton and regenerate HA. Concentration and acid/base identity are distinct concepts.","H+ is the donated proton, not the remaining conjugate-base species in this equation.","The acid–base transformation shown is proton transfer rather than an arbitrary electron-transfer reaction.","Concentration does not define which member of the conjugate pair is present."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":16}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-9"}],"MOL-1:guide-10":[{"id":"mol-slides-1-application-3","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-5","question":"A solution changes from pH 7 to pH 6. What happens to hydrogen-ion concentration?","options":["It doubles","It rises tenfold","It falls tenfold","It remains unchanged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because pH is a negative base-10 logarithm, a one-unit decrease means a tenfold increase in hydrogen ions.","reasons":["The pH scale is logarithmic, not a twofold scale.","10^-6 divided by 10^-7 equals 10.","A tenfold fall would increase the pH by one.","A change in pH represents a change in hydrogen-ion activity."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":22}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-10"},{"id":"expanded-application-1-6","lectureIds":["MOL-1"],"concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-5","question":"A sample changes from pH 8 to pH 5. What happens to hydrogen-ion concentration?","options":["It increases 1000-fold","It increases threefold","It decreases 1000-fold","It remains unchanged because both pH values are below 10"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The pH falls by three units. Because pH is a negative base-10 logarithm, [H+] increases by 10³, or 1000-fold. Always check direction before calculating the magnitude.","reasons":["The pH falls by three units. Because pH is a negative base-10 logarithm, [H+] increases by 10³, or 1000-fold. Always check direction before calculating the magnitude.","Three pH units are logarithmic, not a threefold arithmetic change.","A fall in pH means an increase, not a decrease, in hydrogen ions.","pH values encode hydrogen-ion activity continuously; being below 10 does not imply equal acidity."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":22}],"topicId":"MOL-1:guide-10"}],"MOL-2:guide-1":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-2:guide-1-1","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:0","question":"Which RNA is the adaptor that connects an mRNA codon to an amino acid during translation?","options":["mRNA","The coding strand of DNA","A plasmid","tRNA"],"answer":3,"reasons":["mRNA carries the coding information; it is not the amino-acid-bearing adaptor.","DNA is the sequence store, not the RNA adaptor carrying amino acids to translation.","A plasmid is a DNA molecule rather than the RNA adaptor used by the ribosome.","A tRNA carries an amino acid and uses its anticodon to recognize a corresponding mRNA codon."],"explanation":"A tRNA carries an amino acid and uses its anticodon to recognize a corresponding mRNA codon. Not every RNA is translated. Transcription and translation involve different polymers and machinery.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":3}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-2:guide-1"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-2:guide-1-2","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:0","question":"Which statement about the range of RNA functions is correct?","options":["Transcription directly assembles an amino-acid chain","Translation uses DNA as its immediate codon-containing template","Some RNAs contribute to structure, catalysis or regulation without being translated","All cellular RNA molecules must be translated into proteins"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Transcription produces RNA; translation assembles the amino-acid chain.","The immediate codon-containing template for translation is mRNA.","The section distinguishes coding mRNA from structural, catalytic and regulatory RNA functions.","Many RNAs, including tRNA and rRNA, function as RNA rather than as translated messages."],"explanation":"The section distinguishes coding mRNA from structural, catalytic and regulatory RNA functions. Not every RNA is translated. Transcription and translation involve different polymers and machinery.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":3}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-2:guide-1"}],"MOL-2:guide-2":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-41","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:0","question":"A molecule contains adenine joined to ribose, with no phosphate. How is it classified?","options":["A pyrimidine nucleoside","A free purine base only","A purine nucleotide","A purine nucleoside (adenosine)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Identify the base, sugar and presence or absence of phosphate before naming a nucleic-acid component.","reasons":["Adenine has a fused two-ring purine structure, so an adenine nucleoside is not a pyrimidine nucleoside.","The ribose makes it a nucleoside rather than a base alone.","A nucleotide requires phosphate, but the described molecule contains only base and ribose.","Adenine is a purine and base+sugar without phosphate is a nucleoside."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":16}],"originalQuestion":"What is the structure indicated by the image? A. Purine B. Purine Nucleotide C. Purine Nucleoside D. Deoxyadenosine E. Pyrimidine Nucleoside Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed the missing structural figure as an explicit adenine–ribose molecule.","sourceExcerpt":"What is the structure indicated by the image? A. Purine B. Purine Nucleotide C. Purine Nucleoside D. Deoxyadenosine E. Pyrimidine Nucleoside Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":16,"number":41,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-41"}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-2"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-2:guide-2-1","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:0","question":"What structural addition converts a nucleoside into a nucleotide?","options":["A ribosomal subunit","Phosphate","A second nitrogenous base","An amino-acid side chain"],"answer":1,"reasons":["Ribosomes are translation machinery, not components added to define a nucleotide.","A nucleoside contains base plus sugar; adding phosphate produces a nucleotide.","The defining distinction is phosphate, not attachment of another base.","Amino-acid side chains belong to protein chemistry and do not define a nucleotide."],"explanation":"A nucleoside contains base plus sugar; adding phosphate produces a nucleotide. Sugar positions use primes; base positions do not. Adenosine is a nucleoside; AMP is a nucleotide.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":6}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-2:guide-2"}],"MOL-2:guide-3":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-142","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:0","question":"A molecule has a base, a ribose with a2′ hydroxyl and a phosphate group. How should it be classified?","options":["Ribonucleotide","Deoxyribonucleotide","Ribonucleoside","Purine base alone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ribose2′ hydroxyl identifies a ribose sugar, and phosphate distinguishes a nucleotide from a nucleoside.","reasons":["The ribose2′ hydroxyl identifies a ribose sugar, and phosphate distinguishes a nucleotide from a nucleoside.","Deoxyribose lacks the 2′ hydroxyl specified here.","A nucleoside lacks the stated phosphate.","The molecule includes both sugar and phosphate, so it is more than a free base."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":13}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following most accurately defines the attached diagram? A) Ribonucleoside B) Deoxyribonucleoside C) Deoxyribonucleotide D) Ribonucleotide E) Purine Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed the missing diagram into an explicit sugar/phosphate description.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following most accurately defines the attached diagram? A) Ribonucleoside B) Deoxyribonucleoside C) Deoxyribonucleotide D) Ribonucleotide E) Purine Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-2:guide-3"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-16-30","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:0","question":"Thymidine, as used in DNA chemistry, is which type of molecule?","options":["A pyrimidine ribonucleotide","A purine deoxyribonucleoside","A pyrimidine deoxyribonucleoside","A purine ribonucleotide"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A nucleoside consists of a base plus sugar. Thymidine ordinarily means thymine linked to deoxyribose.","reasons":["This changes both the sugar class and the phosphate status.","The sugar class is plausible but thymine is a pyrimidine.","Thymine is a pyrimidine and thymidine contains deoxyribose without phosphate.","Thymine is not a purine, and a nucleotide would include phosphate."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":8}],"originalQuestion":"In DNA …., thymidine nucleoside is : A. Purine ribonucleoside B. Pyrimidine ribonucleoside C. Purine deoxyribonucleoside D. Pyrimidine deoxyribonucleoside Ans: D","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"In DNA …., thymidine nucleoside is : A. Purine ribonucleoside B. Pyrimidine ribonucleoside C. Purine deoxyribonucleoside D. Pyrimidine deoxyribonucleoside Ans: D","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":8,"number":30,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-30"}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-3"}],"MOL-2:guide-4":[{"id":"mol-slides-2-application-2","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-1","question":"Which bond connects adjacent phosphate groups in ATP?","options":["A phosphoanhydride bond","A peptide bond","An N-glycosidic bond","A hydrogen bond between bases"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ATP contains phosphate–phosphate phosphoanhydride linkages. The sugar-to-first-phosphate linkage is a phosphoester instead.","reasons":["This is the phosphate–phosphate connection.","Peptide bonds connect amino-acid residues.","The N-glycosidic bond connects a base to its sugar.","Base-pair hydrogen bonds do not join ATP phosphate groups."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":13}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-4"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-16-26","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-1","question":"What is the main structural role of 3′–5′ phosphodiester bonds in DNA?","options":["Covalently joining adjacent nucleotides along the sugar–phosphate backbone","Holding complementary bases together between the two strands","Directly maintaining only DNA tertiary structure","Forming peptide links between histone residues"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DNA’s sequence is embodied in a covalent sugar–phosphate chain; base-pair hydrogen bonds connect the two complementary chains.","reasons":["These bonds connect the 3′ hydroxyl of one sugar with the 5′ phosphate of the next.","Base pairs are joined by hydrogen bonds, not interstrand phosphodiester bonds.","Phosphodiester bonds define the primary covalent backbone, not only higher-order folding.","Histones use peptide bonds in their protein backbones."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":7}],"originalQuestion":"What is the primary function of a phosphodiester bond? Ans: for maintaining the tertiary structure?","adaptationNote":"Corrected the source’s uncertain answer maintaining tertiary structure and supplied full options.","sourceExcerpt":"What is the primary function of a phosphodiester bond? Ans: for maintaining the tertiary structure?","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":7,"number":26,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-26"}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-4"}],"MOL-2:guide-5":[{"id":"expanded-application-2-3","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-2","question":"A DNA strand is 5′-ACGTTA-3′. Which is its complementary strand written 5′→3′?","options":["5′-TAACGT-3′","5′-TGCAAT-3′","5′-ACGTTA-3′","5′-UGCAAU-3′"],"answer":0,"explanation":"First pair the strand with 3′-TGCAAT-5′. To report that complementary strand in the conventional 5′→3′ direction, reverse it to 5′-TAACGT-3′.","reasons":["First pair the strand with 3′-TGCAAT-5′. To report that complementary strand in the conventional 5′→3′ direction, reverse it to 5′-TAACGT-3′.","This is the aligned complementary base order but has been labeled with the wrong direction.","Repeating the original sequence is not generally its complementary strand.","The question asks for DNA, so uracil is inappropriate; the direction is also not the required reverse complement."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":17}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-5"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-9","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:1","question":"How many base-pair hydrogen bonds form when 5′-ATCGTA-3′ pairs fully with its DNA complement?","options":["18","16","14","12"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Count A and T positions as two hydrogen bonds each and G and C positions as three each.","reasons":["This would count all six pairs as GC pairs.","This overcounts the two GC pairs or the four AT pairs.","Four AT pairs contribute 8 bonds and two GC pairs contribute 6, totaling 14.","This would count every pair as an AT pair."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":5}],"originalQuestion":"How many hydrogen bonds will form for the complementary strand of the following sequence? (A-T-C-G-T-A) A.16 B.10 C.14 D. 18 Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"How many hydrogen bonds will form for the complementary strand of the following sequence? (A-T-C-G-T-A) A.16 B.10 C.14 D. 18 Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":5,"number":9,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-9"}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-5"}],"MOL-2:guide-6":[{"id":"mol-past-586010b980d8","source":"mol-past","page":6,"endPage":6,"number":"1","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"question":"An artificial DNA strand is synthesized by scientists in a laboratory, in which all A-T base pairs are replaced by G-C base pairs & vice versa. If G-C was originally 40%, then according to Chargaff’s rule, what will be the G-C content in the artificial strand?","options":["Chargaff’s rule will not be followed, G-C content cannot be determined","Chargaffs rule still applies, and G-C will be 60%","Chargaff's rule still applies, but A will bind with C and T will bind with G","Chargaff’s rule doesn’t apply to DNA"],"answer":1,"sourceNote":"","imageDependent":false,"duplicateKey":"586010b980d8","keyAmbiguous":false,"concept":"MOL-2:1","explanation":"If original GC pairs make up 40%, AT pairs make up 60%. Replacing AT with GC therefore makes the new GC proportion 60%.","reasons":["Changing pair types does not abolish normal complementary pairing.","The original AT fraction becomes the new GC fraction: 60%.","A still pairs with T and G with C; pairing partners are not reversed.","Chargaff relationships apply to complementary double-stranded DNA."],"kind":"Past-paper question · reviewed explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":6}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-6"},{"id":"mol-slides-2-application-1","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-3","question":"A double-stranded DNA sample contains 10% guanine. What percentage is thymine?","options":["10%","20%","40%","80%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cytosine is also 10%, leaving 80% for A and T together; each is 40%.","reasons":["10% is the guanine and cytosine proportion individually.","20% is the combined GC proportion.","Thymine is half of the remaining 80%.","80% is A plus T, not T alone."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":23}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-6"}],"MOL-2:guide-7":[{"id":"expanded-application-2-7","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-4","question":"Which comparison of common DNA conformations is correct?","options":["A- and B-DNA are right-handed; Z-DNA is left-handed","All three are left-handed","Only B-DNA has a covalent backbone","Z-DNA contains amino acids instead of nucleotides"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A- and B-DNA are right-handed conformations, while Z-DNA is left-handed and has a zigzag backbone. They remain DNA polymers despite differing geometry.","reasons":["A- and B-DNA are right-handed conformations, while Z-DNA is left-handed and has a zigzag backbone. They remain DNA polymers despite differing geometry.","The handedness of A and B is opposite to this statement.","All DNA conformations retain covalent sugar–phosphate backbones.","A conformational change does not convert DNA into a protein."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":25}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-7"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-2:guide-7-1","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-4","question":"Why are the major and minor grooves of a DNA helix useful to DNA-binding proteins?","options":["They expose base-specific chemical patterns that proteins can recognize","They convert the phosphodiester backbone into a polypeptide chain","They make every DNA helix permanently left-handed","They require the entire duplex to be covalently cut before recognition"],"answer":0,"reasons":["The grooves provide access to chemical patterns associated with the base sequence.","Grooves do not change the DNA backbone into a protein backbone.","B-DNA and A-DNA are right-handed; groove access does not impose Z-DNA handedness.","Groove recognition does not inherently require covalent cleavage of the duplex."],"explanation":"The grooves provide access to chemical patterns associated with the base sequence. A and B are right-handed. Z is left-handed.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":24}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-2:guide-7"}],"MOL-2:guide-8":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-33","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:3","question":"Under comparable conditions, which DNA sample would generally have a higher melting temperature?","options":["A duplex with more A–U pairs","A sample with no complementary pairing","A sample with fewer bases but an unspecified sequence","A duplex with a higher proportion of G–C base pairs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GC-rich DNA generally melts at a higher temperature than AT-rich DNA under similar conditions; salt, length and sequence also matter.","reasons":["U is characteristic of RNA; DNA normally pairs A with T.","Loss of duplex pairing does not raise the melting temperature of a conventional duplex.","Length alone without sequence and conditions does not establish a higher melting temperature.","GC content generally raises duplex stability through base pairing and stacking contributions."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":7}],"originalQuestion":"A DNA sample isolated from a thermophilic organism is found to have a higher melting temperature (Tm) compared to DNA from a non-thermophilic organism. A) Increased number of phosphodiester bonds B) Higher proportion of G–C base pairs with more hydrogen bonds C) Increased A–U bonds D) Greater hydrophobic interactions in the DNA backbone Answer: B","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"A DNA sample isolated from a thermophilic organism is found to have a higher melting temperature (Tm) compared to DNA from a non-thermophilic organism. A) Increased number of phosphodiester bonds B) Higher proportion of G–C base pairs with more hydrogen bonds C) Increased A–U bonds D) Greater hydrophobic interactions in the DNA backbone Answer: B","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":7,"number":33,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-33"}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-8"},{"id":"mol-slides-2-application-3","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-5","question":"A DNA sample is heated until its strands separate. Which change is expected?","options":["Absorbance near 260 nm rises","The sugar–phosphate backbone must be hydrolyzed","The GC percentage necessarily falls","Every thymine becomes uracil"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Duplex melting produces a hyperchromic increase in absorbance without ordinarily cutting the covalent backbone.","reasons":["This is the hyperchromic effect described in the lecture.","Strand separation does not require backbone hydrolysis.","Heating does not by itself rewrite the base composition.","Denaturation does not generally convert thymine into uracil."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":27}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-8"}],"MOL-2:guide-9":[{"id":"expanded-application-2-6","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:2","question":"A nucleosome core contains two copies each of H2A, H2B, H3 and H4. Where is H1 primarily associated in this comparison?","options":["With linker DNA outside the core octamer","As a ninth identical copy of H3 in the core","Only in bacterial ribosomes","As the RNA template of telomerase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"H1 is the linker-associated histone. It helps organize DNA beyond the core octamer, which already contains eight molecules from the four stated histone classes.","reasons":["H1 is the linker-associated histone. It helps organize DNA beyond the core octamer, which already contains eight molecules from the four stated histone classes.","H1 is not an extra H3 molecule and is not counted as part of the eight core histones.","A histone is not a bacterial ribosomal component in this chromatin example.","Telomerase uses an RNA template, not histone H1 as its template."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":28}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-9"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-16-14","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:2","question":"What forms the core of a nucleosome?","options":["DNA wrapped around a histone octamer","DNA wrapped around one ribosome","A histone octamer made only of H1","RNA wrapped around mitochondrial ATP synthase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Nucleosomes organize eukaryotic DNA around a basic histone core. H1 associates with linker DNA.","reasons":["The octamer has two copies each of H2A, H2B, H3 and H4.","Ribosomes translate RNA; they are not the nucleosome core.","H1 is a linker histone rather than a core-octamer component.","This is unrelated to nuclear chromatin packaging."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":4}],"originalQuestion":"What is a nucleosome? Ans: DNA wrapped around histone octamer","adaptationNote":"Added complete alternatives.","sourceExcerpt":"What is a nucleosome? Ans: DNA wrapped around histone octamer","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":4,"number":14,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-14"}],"topicId":"MOL-2:guide-9"}],"MOL-2:guide-10":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-2:guide-10-1","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:1","question":"A small DNA molecule replicates independently of a bacterium’s main chromosome and carries an antibiotic-resistance gene. What is it?","options":["An individual histone subunit","An aminoacyl-tRNA","A plasmid","A ribosomal RNA molecule"],"answer":2,"reasons":["A histone is a protein and cannot be the described DNA molecule.","An aminoacyl-tRNA is a charged translation adaptor, not the DNA construct described.","Plasmids are independently replicating DNA molecules and can carry traits such as antibiotic resistance.","rRNA is an RNA component of the ribosome, not independently replicating DNA carrying a resistance gene."],"explanation":"Plasmids are independently replicating DNA molecules and can carry traits such as antibiotic resistance. Plasmids have their own replication origin. A selectable marker helps identify cells carrying the construct.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":30}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-2:guide-10"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-2:guide-10-2","lectureIds":["MOL-2"],"concept":"MOL-2:1","question":"Why does a cloning plasmid commonly include a selectable marker?","options":["To replace the need for a plasmid replication origin","To help identify cells carrying the construct","To remove every intron from any inserted genomic gene automatically","To make the plasmid become a ribosome"],"answer":1,"reasons":["The replication origin and selectable marker perform distinct functions.","A selectable marker permits identification or selection of cells that carry the construct.","A marker does not automatically supply bacterial processing of eukaryotic introns.","The plasmid remains DNA; a marker does not turn it into translation machinery."],"explanation":"A selectable marker permits identification or selection of cells that carry the construct. Plasmids have their own replication origin. A selectable marker helps identify cells carrying the construct.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":30}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-2:guide-10"}],"MOL-3:guide-1":[{"id":"mol-slides-3-application-1","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-0","question":"After one semiconservative replication round, which composition describes each daughter DNA duplex?","options":["Two newly made strands","Two parental strands","One parental and one new strand","One DNA and one permanent RNA strand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each original strand serves as a template for a new partner.","reasons":["This would leave the parental duplex together rather than distributing its strands.","Replication does not create a daughter duplex consisting solely of the two old strands.","One old and one new strand is the semiconservative model.","An RNA primer is temporary; it is not the permanent complementary strand of the daughter chromosome."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":6}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-1"},{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-3:slides-2026-1-0","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-1","question":"Which statement best explains replication origins?","options":["Multiple origins allow long eukaryotic chromosomes to be copied efficiently","DNA polymerase extends a strand in the 5′ to 3′ direction","Proofreading removes a wrong terminal nucleotide using 3′ to 5′ exonuclease activity","Helicase unwinds; primase primes; ligase seals nicks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Replication bubbles can grow from many start sites and later merge. Fork speed and number of initiation sites are different variables. A long chromosome need not wait for one fork to traverse its whole length.","reasons":["Correct. Replication bubbles can grow from many start sites and later merge. Fork speed and number of initiation sites are different variables. A long chromosome need not wait for one fork to traverse its whole length.","This explains DNA synthesis direction. A polymerase adds a nucleotide to a pre-existing 3′ hydroxyl. It reads the template in the opposite direction. Leading and lagging strands both grow 5′ to 3′; the lagging strand is discontinuous because the templates are antiparallel. For the concept asked here, Multiple origins allow long eukaryotic chromosomes to be copied efficiently.","This explains Proofreading. Proofreading reverses away from the growing 3′ end to remove an error. It is different from 5′ to 3′ nucleotide addition and from removal of RNA primers. Match the direction to the function. For the concept asked here, Multiple origins allow long eukaryotic chromosomes to be copied efficiently.","This explains Replication fork roles. Helicase separates the strands, primase creates a starting primer and polymerase extends it. Topoisomerases relieve torsional stress. Ligase joins adjacent DNA fragments by sealing the backbone rather than synthesizing an entire fragment. For the concept asked here, Multiple origins allow long eukaryotic chromosomes to be copied efficiently."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":8},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":63}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-1"}],"MOL-3:guide-2":[{"id":"expanded-application-3-3","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-2","question":"A replication fork advances but positive torsional stress builds ahead of it. Which enzyme function most directly relieves that stress?","options":["Transient DNA cleavage and resealing by a topoisomerase","Joining an amino acid to tRNA","Adding a poly-A tail","Recognizing a stop codon"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Topoisomerases change DNA topology through controlled transient strand breaks and resealing. This relieves twisting generated by helix opening and differs from helicase’s direct separation of paired strands.","reasons":["Topoisomerases change DNA topology through controlled transient strand breaks and resealing. This relieves twisting generated by helix opening and differs from helicase’s direct separation of paired strands.","tRNA charging supports translation and does not relieve DNA torsion.","Polyadenylation processes RNA rather than altering DNA topology.","Stop-codon recognition terminates translation, not replication stress."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":8}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-2"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-21","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:2","question":"At a replication fork, what is the direct function of DNA helicase?","options":["Adding the 5′ cap to an RNA transcript","Joining two completed Okazaki fragments","Extending the telomere using an RNA template","Separating the parental DNA strands"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Helicase disrupts base-pairing interactions to expose single-stranded templates.","reasons":["This is an RNA-processing reaction.","Ligase seals nicks rather than unwinding the parental duplex.","Extending a chromosome end using an RNA template is telomerase’s reaction, not helicase unwinding.","Helicase disrupts base-pairing interactions to expose single-stranded templates."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":23}],"originalQuestion":"Which one of the following is the site of action of helicase? A) A B) B C) C D) D Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Replaced an unlabeled fork diagram with the named enzyme-role question.","sourceExcerpt":"Which one of the following is the site of action of helicase? A) A B) B C) C D) D Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-3:guide-2"}],"MOL-3:guide-3":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-49","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-5","question":"A nucleotide analogue lacking a 3′ hydroxyl is incorporated at the end of a DNA strand. What is the direct effect?","options":["Further chain extension stops at that end","Only lagging-strand extension is affected","It creates a peptide bond to bypass the missing hydroxyl","DNA synthesis necessarily continues unchanged"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DNA synthesis depends on attack by the growing strand’s 3′ hydroxyl on the incoming nucleotide phosphate.","reasons":["The next nucleotide needs the terminal 3′ hydroxyl to form a phosphodiester linkage.","The same extension chemistry applies to either strand.","DNA polymerase forms phosphodiester bonds, not peptide bonds.","The required reactive group is absent."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":11}],"originalQuestion":"A nucleotide used during replication is missing the 3′ hydroxyl (–OH) group. What will be the effect on DNA replication? A) Replication continues normally B) Only the lagging strand is affected C) DNA polymerase slows down but continues D) DNA elongation stops Answer: D","adaptationNote":"Specified incorporation at the growing strand end, which is required for chain termination.","sourceExcerpt":"A nucleotide used during replication is missing the 3′ hydroxyl (–OH) group. What will be the effect on DNA replication? A) Replication continues normally B) Only the lagging strand is affected C) DNA polymerase slows down but continues D) DNA elongation stops Answer: D","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":11,"number":49,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-49"}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-3"},{"id":"expanded-application-3-1","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:0","question":"A DNA polymerase can add a nucleotide only when which reactive group is available at the primer terminus?","options":["A 3′ hydroxyl group","A free 2′ hydroxyl on every residue","A side-chain thiol","A 5′ cap of 7-methylguanosine"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The primer’s terminal 3′ hydroxyl attacks the incoming nucleotide phosphate, extending DNA 5′→3′. A normal DNA sugar lacks the RNA-like 2′ hydroxyl but retains the needed 3′ hydroxyl.","reasons":["The primer’s terminal 3′ hydroxyl attacks the incoming nucleotide phosphate, extending DNA 5′→3′. A normal DNA sugar lacks the RNA-like 2′ hydroxyl but retains the needed 3′ hydroxyl.","Normal deoxyribose has no 2′ hydroxyl; that group is not the required chain-extension nucleophile.","Thiol chemistry belongs to different molecular reactions such as disulfide formation.","A 7-methylguanosine cap is an mRNA-processing feature, not a DNA primer requirement."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":10}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-3"}],"MOL-3:guide-4":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-47","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-3","question":"Why would a complete loss of DNA primase impair synthesis of both leading and lagging strands?","options":["Primase makes the protein ribosome","Only the lagging strand ever needs a primer","Primase is the enzyme that seals every nick","Both require a primer to supply a starting 3′ hydroxyl"],"answer":3,"explanation":"DNA polymerases extend existing nucleic-acid ends. Primase can create the RNA starts needed on both strands.","reasons":["Primase synthesizes RNA primers for DNA replication.","DNA polymerase also needs a starting primer on the leading strand.","DNA ligase seals nicks after synthesis and processing.","The leading strand needs an initial primer and each Okazaki fragment needs a primer."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":10}],"originalQuestion":"A mutation occurs in the gene encoding DNA primase, making it non-functional. Which of the following processes would be affected? A) Only leading strand synthesis B) Only lagging strand synthesis C) Lagging strand synthesis only, but replication can still continue D) Both leading and lagging strand synthesis would stop Answer: D","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"A mutation occurs in the gene encoding DNA primase, making it non-functional. Which of the following processes would be affected? A) Only leading strand synthesis B) Only lagging strand synthesis C) Lagging strand synthesis only, but replication can still continue D) Both leading and lagging strand synthesis would stop Answer: D","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":10,"number":47,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-47"}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-4"},{"id":"expanded-application-3-2","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-3","question":"At one replication fork, why are repeated RNA primers needed on the lagging strand?","options":["DNA polymerase still extends each fragment 5′→3′ while the fork opens in the opposite overall direction","Lagging DNA is synthesized 3′→5′","Primase seals completed DNA nicks","The lagging template has no bases"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Antiparallel templates and one direction of DNA synthesis create discontinuous lagging-strand synthesis. Each Okazaki fragment needs its own primer, but each fragment still grows 5′→3′.","reasons":["Antiparallel templates and one direction of DNA synthesis create discontinuous lagging-strand synthesis. Each Okazaki fragment needs its own primer, but each fragment still grows 5′→3′.","The lagging strand does not reverse polymerase chemistry to synthesize 3′→5′.","Ligase seals nicks; primase supplies starting RNA.","The lagging template contains the base sequence being copied."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":12}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-4"}],"MOL-3:guide-5":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-3:guide-5-1","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-3","question":"Adjacent DNA fragments have been filled with DNA but a single nick remains in the backbone. Which activity is required next?","options":["Helicase adds the missing DNA nucleotides","Primase converts every base into RNA","A glycosylase synthesizes an entire chromosome","DNA ligase seals the nick"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Helicase separates strands; polymerase, rather than helicase, performs gap filling.","Primase makes primers; the final replicated backbone should not be converted into RNA.","A glycosylase removes selected damaged bases and does not synthesize a chromosome.","Ligase forms the missing phosphodiester linkage once suitable DNA ends are adjacent."],"explanation":"Ligase forms the missing phosphodiester linkage once suitable DNA ends are adjacent. Gap filling and nick sealing are distinct tasks. Final DNA should not retain the ordinary replication primers.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":14}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-3:guide-5"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-3:guide-5-2","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-3","question":"Which distinction correctly separates polymerase from ligase during completion of DNA replication?","options":["Both activities leave the RNA primers as permanent parts of the final DNA","Neither activity contributes to DNA backbone continuity","Polymerase fills nucleotide gaps; ligase seals remaining backbone nicks","Ligase fills a long nucleotide gap; polymerase only seals a nick"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Ordinary replication RNA primers must be removed and replaced with DNA.","Their combined activities are central to achieving continuous DNA after primer removal.","Nucleotide addition and nick sealing are separate steps in producing a continuous DNA backbone.","The functions have been reversed: polymerase adds nucleotides and ligase seals suitable adjacent ends."],"explanation":"Nucleotide addition and nick sealing are separate steps in producing a continuous DNA backbone. Gap filling and nick sealing are distinct tasks. Final DNA should not retain the ordinary replication primers.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":14}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-3:guide-5"}],"MOL-3:guide-6":[{"id":"expanded-application-3-4","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:1","question":"A polymerase retains DNA synthesis but loses 3′→5′ exonuclease activity. What is the most direct expected consequence?","options":["More replication errors escape terminal proofreading","DNA must now be synthesized 3′→5′","Every RNA primer is synthesized twice as fast","All telomeres become circular immediately"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Loss of proofreading reduces removal of incorrect terminal nucleotides and therefore lowers copying fidelity. The polymerase’s forward 5′→3′ synthesis direction need not change.","reasons":["Loss of proofreading reduces removal of incorrect terminal nucleotides and therefore lowers copying fidelity. The polymerase’s forward 5′→3′ synthesis direction need not change.","Synthesis direction and proofreading-removal direction describe distinct activities.","Primase activity is not directly increased by removing a polymerase proofreading function.","Telomere structure does not instantly become circular because proofreading is lost."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":18}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-6"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-16","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:1","question":"What activity removes a mismatched nucleotide from the newly synthesized DNA3′ end during polymerase proofreading?","options":["RNA helicase activity","5′→3′ exonuclease activity as the proofreading direction","3′→5′ exonuclease activity","Peptidyl transferase activity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Proofreading backs up from the growing end to remove an incorrectly incorporated terminal nucleotide.","reasons":["Unwinding RNA is not DNA-polymerase proofreading.","That direction can participate in other processing but is not the terminal proofreading direction.","Proofreading backs up from the growing end to remove an incorrectly incorporated terminal nucleotide.","Peptidyl transferase forms peptide bonds in translation."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":14}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following enzymes has 3’→ 5' exonuclease activity? A) DNA polymerase B) RNA polymerase C) DNA topoisomerase D) DNA helicase Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Specified proofreading-capable polymerases because not every DNA polymerase has this activity.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following enzymes has 3’→ 5' exonuclease activity? A) DNA polymerase B) RNA polymerase C) DNA topoisomerase D) DNA helicase Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-3:guide-6"}],"MOL-3:guide-7":[{"id":"expanded-application-3-6","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-4","question":"Which feature explains why telomerase is called a reverse transcriptase?","options":["It makes DNA using an RNA template contained in the enzyme complex","It converts every chromosome into RNA","It synthesizes protein from a DNA template","It removes all terminal DNA repeats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reverse transcription is DNA synthesis directed by RNA information. Telomerase carries an RNA template and extends terminal DNA repeats, helping address the end-replication problem.","reasons":["Reverse transcription is DNA synthesis directed by RNA information. Telomerase carries an RNA template and extends terminal DNA repeats, helping address the end-replication problem.","Its product is DNA, not conversion of a chromosome into RNA.","Protein synthesis uses ribosomes and an mRNA template, not telomerase.","Telomerase extends rather than simply deletes terminal repeats."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":22}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-7"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-18","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-4","question":"Besides its RNA template, telomerase requires which catalytic component?","options":["A DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","An RNA-dependent DNA polymerase (reverse transcriptase)","An RNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Only a DNA ligase"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It synthesizes DNA using an internal RNA template.","reasons":["That synthesizes RNA from DNA rather than telomeric DNA from RNA.","It synthesizes DNA using an internal RNA template.","That would synthesize RNA, not the required telomeric DNA.","Ligase can seal nicks but cannot perform telomerase’s templated extension."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":18}],"originalQuestion":"Telomerase is the specialized enzyme complex that allows for replication of the telomeric ends of the chromosomes. In addition to an RNA that is complementary to the telomeric repeat sequence, what other component is required of this complex for its normal activity? A) RNA-dependent RNA polymerase B) DNA-dependent DNA polymerase C) RNA-dependent DNA polymerase D) DNA-dependent RNA polymerase E) DNA helicase Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Telomerase is the specialized enzyme complex that allows for replication of the telomeric ends of the chromosomes. In addition to an RNA that is complementary to the telomeric repeat sequence, what other component is required of this complex for its normal activity? A) RNA-dependent RNA polymerase B) DNA-dependent DNA polymerase C) RNA-dependent DNA polymerase D) DNA-dependent RNA polymerase E) DNA helicase Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-3:guide-7"}],"MOL-3:guide-8":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-3:guide-8-1","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:3","question":"A mispaired base escapes the replication machinery. Which repair system is specifically described as correcting this copying error?","options":["Mismatch repair","The ribosomal release-factor system","RNA editing of apolipoprotein B","Protein glycosylation"],"answer":0,"reasons":["Mismatch repair removes an error-containing segment from newly synthesized DNA and permits correct resynthesis.","Release factors participate in translation termination, not repair of mispaired DNA bases.","Apolipoprotein RNA editing changes an RNA transcript and is not DNA mismatch repair.","Glycosylation modifies proteins rather than correcting DNA base mismatches."],"explanation":"Mismatch repair removes an error-containing segment from newly synthesized DNA and permits correct resynthesis. Mismatch repair addresses copying errors. Base excision starts by removing a damaged base.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":30}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-3:guide-8"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-3:guide-8-2","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:3","question":"What is the first characteristic step of base excision repair described in this topic?","options":["A ribosome removes an entire amino-acid chain","An RNA polymerase adds a poly-A tail to DNA","DNA ligase first synthesizes a long replacement nucleotide strand","A DNA glycosylase removes a damaged base, leaving an abasic site"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Amino-acid chains are protein substrates and are not the damaged DNA base targeted here.","Polyadenylation is RNA processing and is not the initiating event of base excision repair.","Ligase seals the final nick; it does not synthesize a long replacement strand as the first step.","A glycosylase removes the damaged base; later enzymes process the backbone and restore DNA."],"explanation":"A glycosylase removes the damaged base; later enzymes process the backbone and restore DNA. Mismatch repair addresses copying errors. Base excision starts by removing a damaged base.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":30}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-3:guide-8"}],"MOL-3:guide-9":[{"id":"expanded-application-3-5","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:3","question":"A repair pathway removes a short DNA segment surrounding a bulky UV photoproduct and then fills the gap. Which pathway fits best?","options":["Nucleotide excision repair","tRNA aminoacylation","Polyadenylation","Direct peptide-bond hydrolysis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Nucleotide excision repair removes a patch containing bulky helix-distorting damage. DNA polymerase copies the intact partner strand and ligase seals the repair.","reasons":["Nucleotide excision repair removes a patch containing bulky helix-distorting damage. DNA polymerase copies the intact partner strand and ligase seals the repair.","Aminoacylation attaches amino acids to tRNA in translation.","Polyadenylation adds adenosines to RNA and does not excise a DNA lesion.","Peptide hydrolysis degrades protein rather than repairing DNA."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":33}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-9"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-3","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:3","question":"A child with marked photosensitivity and early skin lesions has xeroderma pigmentosum. Which repair defect best explains the condition?","options":["Inability to make all RNA primers","Inability of ribosomes to terminate translation","Impaired nucleotide excision repair of UV-induced pyrimidine photoproducts","Failure to remove every viral genome"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nucleotide excision repair protects against UV-induced bulky DNA lesions. Its failure increases sun sensitivity and skin-cancer risk.","reasons":["Primase loss would impair replication, not specifically produce the characteristic UV-repair disorder.","This is not the characteristic xeroderma pigmentosum mechanism.","This pathway removes bulky UV lesions such as pyrimidine dimers.","The defining defect is DNA lesion repair rather than universal antiviral genome destruction."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":2}],"originalQuestion":"A 10 year old present to the emergency room with many skin lesions and oversensitivity to light, the disease is said to be caused by an error in dna repair which of the following is not properly repaired. A. The integrity of double-stranded DNA B. Viral DNA C. Removal of pyrimidine dimers D. Single-strand breaks E. DNA cross-links Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"A 10 year old present to the emergency room with many skin lesions and oversensitivity to light, the disease is said to be caused by an error in dna repair which of the following is not properly repaired. A. The integrity of double-stranded DNA B. Viral DNA C. Removal of pyrimidine dimers D. Single-strand breaks E. DNA cross-links Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":2,"number":3,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-3"}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-9"}],"MOL-3:guide-10":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-19","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-2","question":"A drug prevents DNA topoisomerase from relieving torsional stress during replication. Which enzyme function has been blocked?","options":["Separating base pairs by helicase motion only","Joining amino acids into a polypeptide","Changing DNA topology through transient strand breaks","Adding ribonucleotides to mRNA"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Topoisomerases manage DNA topology while forks and transcription complexes move along DNA.","reasons":["Helicase unwinds the fork; it is distinct from topoisomerase strand-passage chemistry.","The ribosome catalyzes peptide synthesis.","Topoisomerases cut and reseal one or both strands to alter supercoiling or disentangle DNA.","RNA polymerase catalyzes transcription."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":8}],"originalQuestion":"A woman was diagnosed with cancer. She was given a drug to help in treating the cancer. The drug included an enzyme that is responsible for inhibiting supercoiling. Which enzyme is inhibited? A. Helicase B. DNA Topoisomerase C. DNA polymerase D. RNA polymerase Answer: B","adaptationNote":"Repaired the contradictory wording drug included an enzyme responsible for inhibiting supercoiling.","sourceExcerpt":"A woman was diagnosed with cancer. She was given a drug to help in treating the cancer. The drug included an enzyme that is responsible for inhibiting supercoiling. Which enzyme is inhibited? A. Helicase B. DNA Topoisomerase C. DNA polymerase D. RNA polymerase Answer: B","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":8,"number":19,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-19"}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-10"},{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-3:slides-2026-5-0","lectureIds":["MOL-3"],"concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-5","question":"Which statement best explains nucleoside-analogue reasoning?","options":["An incorporated analogue lacking an available 3′ hydroxyl can terminate further DNA extension","DNA polymerase extends a strand in the 5′ to 3′ direction","Proofreading removes a wrong terminal nucleotide using 3′ to 5′ exonuclease activity","Helicase unwinds; primase primes; ligase seals nicks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"AZT illustrates the 3′-OH requirement. Activation and selectivity differ among drugs, so not every analogue works identically. The central chemistry is loss of the functional group needed to form the next backbone linkage.","reasons":["Correct. AZT illustrates the 3′-OH requirement. Activation and selectivity differ among drugs, so not every analogue works identically. The central chemistry is loss of the functional group needed to form the next backbone linkage.","This explains DNA synthesis direction. A polymerase adds a nucleotide to a pre-existing 3′ hydroxyl. It reads the template in the opposite direction. Leading and lagging strands both grow 5′ to 3′; the lagging strand is discontinuous because the templates are antiparallel. For the concept asked here, An incorporated analogue lacking an available 3′ hydroxyl can terminate further DNA extension.","This explains Proofreading. Proofreading reverses away from the growing 3′ end to remove an error. It is different from 5′ to 3′ nucleotide addition and from removal of RNA primers. Match the direction to the function. For the concept asked here, An incorporated analogue lacking an available 3′ hydroxyl can terminate further DNA extension.","This explains Replication fork roles. Helicase separates the strands, primase creates a starting primer and polymerase extends it. Topoisomerases relieve torsional stress. Ligase joins adjacent DNA fragments by sealing the backbone rather than synthesizing an entire fragment. For the concept asked here, An incorporated analogue lacking an available 3′ hydroxyl can terminate further DNA extension."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":34},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":88}],"topicId":"MOL-3:guide-10"}],"MOL-4:guide-1":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-4:guide-1-1","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-1","question":"Why can a commonly single-stranded RNA molecule nevertheless form stems, loops and compact structures?","options":["RNA must first lose all its bases to fold","Every RNA molecule must become double-stranded DNA","The presence of uracil prevents all base pairing","Different regions of the same RNA can base-pair internally"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Bases are needed for the internal pairing that helps organize RNA structure.","RNA folding does not require conversion of the molecule into DNA.","Uracil can participate in base pairing; its presence does not prevent RNA secondary structure.","Internal complementary regions permit base-paired stems and loops within an RNA molecule."],"explanation":"Internal complementary regions permit base-paired stems and loops within an RNA molecule. RNA can form stems and loops. Only a subset of RNAs are translated.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":4}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-4:guide-1"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-4:guide-1-2","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-1","question":"Which RNA is particularly associated with ribosome structure and key catalytic functions?","options":["A free amino acid","A cholesterol molecule","rRNA","A DNA promoter"],"answer":2,"reasons":["A free amino acid is a translation substrate, not a ribosomal RNA molecule.","Cholesterol is a lipid and is not the RNA component described.","Ribosomal RNA contributes to the ribosome’s structure and catalytic functions.","A promoter is a DNA regulatory region rather than the structural/catalytic ribosomal RNA."],"explanation":"Ribosomal RNA contributes to the ribosome’s structure and catalytic functions. RNA can form stems and loops. Only a subset of RNAs are translated.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":4}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-4:guide-1"}],"MOL-4:guide-2":[{"id":"drill-MOL-4-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:3","question":"Which statement best explains rna versus dna chemistry?","options":["The 2′ hydroxyl makes RNA more susceptible to alkaline hydrolysis","RNA polymerase II transcribes most protein-coding nuclear genes","General transcription factors assemble at a core promoter to recruit polymerase II","RNA is complementary to the template and matches the coding strand except U replaces T"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RNA contains ribose rather than deoxyribose. The extra hydroxyl affects chemical stability. RNA can contain purines, pyrimidines and base-paired regions, so its instability is not explained by an inability to pair bases.","reasons":["Correct. RNA contains ribose rather than deoxyribose. The extra hydroxyl affects chemical stability. RNA can contain purines, pyrimidines and base-paired regions, so its instability is not explained by an inability to pair bases.","This explains RNA polymerase II. Its product is a pre-mRNA that requires processing. Polymerase I makes the large rRNA precursor, while polymerase III makes tRNAs and 5S rRNA. Ribosomal protein genes are still protein-coding genes and use polymerase II. For rna versus dna chemistry, use the rule: The 2′ hydroxyl makes RNA more susceptible to alkaline hydrolysis.","This explains Transcription initiation. TBP, part of TFIID, recognizes a TATA element where present. Promoter damage can reduce initiation. A bacterial sigma factor is not a human general transcription factor. Not every human promoter contains a TATA box. For rna versus dna chemistry, use the rule: The 2′ hydroxyl makes RNA more susceptible to alkaline hydrolysis.","This explains Template and coding strands. For coding DNA 5′-ATG-3′, the RNA sequence is 5′-AUG-3′. The template must be read antiparallel. Always write strand direction before matching a sequence. For rna versus dna chemistry, use the rule: The 2′ hydroxyl makes RNA more susceptible to alkaline hydrolysis."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":25},{"source":"mol-past","page":26},{"source":"mol-past","page":27}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-2"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-30","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:3","question":"What chemical feature makes RNA generally more susceptible than DNA to alkaline backbone hydrolysis?","options":["The ribose 2′ hydroxyl","RNA contains only pyrimidines","RNA can never form double helices","RNA has no phosphodiester bonds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RNA’s 2′ hydroxyl enables base-catalyzed backbone cleavage. DNA’s 2′ carbon lacks that hydroxyl.","reasons":["It can participate in intramolecular attack on a nearby phosphodiester bond.","RNA contains both purines and pyrimidines.","RNA can form base-paired helical structures.","RNA does have a phosphodiester backbone; the key difference is its ribose2′ hydroxyl."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":7}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following best explains why RNA is not the primary nucleotide sugar used for information storage? A) The -OH group on its 2’ end makes it susceptible to hydrolysis B) It contains only pyrimidines C) It has uracil instead of thymine D) Inability to form double helices Answer: A","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following best explains why RNA is not the primary nucleotide sugar used for information storage? A) The -OH group on its 2’ end makes it susceptible to hydrolysis B) It contains only pyrimidines C) It has uracil instead of thymine D) Inability to form double helices Answer: A","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":7,"number":30,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-30"}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-2"}],"MOL-4:guide-3":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-26","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:0","question":"Where is RNA polymeraseI transcription of the major rRNA precursor concentrated in eukaryotic cells?","options":["The mitochondrial matrix for all nuclear rRNAs","The nucleolus","The cytosolic proteasome","The rough-ER lumen"],"answer":1,"explanation":"RNA polymeraseI transcribes the precursor for 18S,5.8S and28S rRNAs in the nucleolus.","reasons":["Mitochondria have a separate transcription system.","RNA polymeraseI transcribes the precursor for 18S,5.8S and28S rRNAs in the nucleolus.","The proteasome degrades proteins rather than transcribes rRNA.","Nuclear rRNA transcription does not occur in the ER lumen."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":27}],"originalQuestion":"Transcription of genes by RNA polymerase I takes place in which of the following? A) A B) B C) C D) D E) E Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Replaced missing cell-image labels with named compartments.","sourceExcerpt":"Transcription of genes by RNA polymerase I takes place in which of the following? A) A B) B C) C D) D E) E Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-4:guide-3"},{"id":"expanded-application-4-3","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:0","question":"Which transcript is most directly assigned to eukaryotic RNA polymerase III?","options":["5S ribosomal RNA","Most protein-coding pre-mRNAs","The entire large rRNA precursor made by Pol I","A polypeptide chain"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RNA polymerase III makes tRNAs and 5S rRNA among its products. The 5S exception prevents overgeneralizing that all rRNA comes from polymerase I.","reasons":["RNA polymerase III makes tRNAs and 5S rRNA among its products. The 5S exception prevents overgeneralizing that all rRNA comes from polymerase I.","Most protein-coding nuclear pre-mRNAs are Pol II products.","The large rRNA precursor is mainly a Pol I product rather than Pol III.","RNA polymerases make RNA, not polypeptides."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":9}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-3"}],"MOL-4:guide-4":[{"id":"mol-slides-4-application-2","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-1","question":"What distinguishes initiation of transcription from ordinary DNA-polymerase extension?","options":["RNA polymerase requires no pre-existing primer","RNA polymerase does not need a template","RNA is synthesized 3′→5′","DNA polymerase starts without a 3′-OH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RNA polymerase can start a new chain de novo, whereas DNA polymerase extends an existing 3′ hydroxyl.","reasons":["Primer-independent initiation is the relevant contrast.","RNA polymerase still uses a template for ordinary transcription.","RNA chain growth is 5′→3′.","DNA polymerase needs an appropriate starting 3′ hydroxyl."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":14}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-4"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-4:guide-4-1","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:3","question":"During transcription elongation, which direction of RNA growth is correct?","options":["3′→5′, by adding ribonucleotides at the 5′ end","N terminus→C terminus, using amino acids","The RNA grows equally from both ends at once","5′→3′, by adding ribonucleotides at the 3′ end"],"answer":3,"reasons":["This reverses the synthesis direction; the template is read 3′→5′ while RNA grows 5′→3′.","N-to-C growth describes a polypeptide, not an RNA transcript.","The described transcription mechanism extends the RNA at one 3′ end.","RNA polymerase adds substrates to the 3′ end, so the new RNA grows 5′→3′."],"explanation":"RNA polymerase adds substrates to the 3′ end, so the new RNA grows 5′→3′. RNA grows 5′→3′. A transcription bubble exposes only a local DNA segment.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":13}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-4:guide-4"}],"MOL-4:guide-5":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-4:slides-2026-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-2","question":"Which statement best explains promoter elements?","options":["Promoter sequences position transcription machinery near an initiation site","RNA polymerase II transcribes most protein-coding nuclear genes","General transcription factors assemble at a core promoter to recruit polymerase II","RNA is complementary to the template and matches the coding strand except U replaces T"],"answer":0,"explanation":"TATA, CAAT, GC and initiator elements are not interchangeable labels. The supplied deck notes that TATA-less promoters can use other initiation features. Do not assume one consensus motif occurs in every human promoter.","reasons":["Correct. TATA, CAAT, GC and initiator elements are not interchangeable labels. The supplied deck notes that TATA-less promoters can use other initiation features. Do not assume one consensus motif occurs in every human promoter.","This explains RNA polymerase II. Its product is a pre-mRNA that requires processing. Polymerase I makes the large rRNA precursor, while polymerase III makes tRNAs and 5S rRNA. Ribosomal protein genes are still protein-coding genes and use polymerase II. For the concept asked here, Promoter sequences position transcription machinery near an initiation site.","This explains Transcription initiation. TBP, part of TFIID, recognizes a TATA element where present. Promoter damage can reduce initiation. A bacterial sigma factor is not a human general transcription factor. Not every human promoter contains a TATA box. For the concept asked here, Promoter sequences position transcription machinery near an initiation site.","This explains Template and coding strands. For coding DNA 5′-ATG-3′, the RNA sequence is 5′-AUG-3′. The template must be read antiparallel. Always write strand direction before matching a sequence. For the concept asked here, Promoter sequences position transcription machinery near an initiation site."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":17}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-5"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-4:guide-5-1","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-2","question":"What does position +1 ordinarily identify in transcription numbering?","options":["The final upstream promoter base in every gene","The last nucleotide removed during polyadenylation","The first transcribed nucleotide","The first amino acid of every translated protein"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Upstream promoter bases have negative positions rather than universally defining +1.","Position +1 identifies transcription initiation rather than the later cleavage/polyadenylation site.","The first transcribed DNA position is designated +1; upstream positions receive negative numbers.","Transcription numbering is distinct from the translation start and amino-acid numbering."],"explanation":"The first transcribed DNA position is designated +1; upstream positions receive negative numbers. +1 is the transcription start site. Upstream positions lie before +1 in the transcriptional direction.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":16}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-4:guide-5"}],"MOL-4:guide-6":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-18","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:1","question":"Which statement correctly describes nuclear transcription initiation in humans?","options":["Bacterial sigma factor is required at every human promoter","Enhancers never influence human transcription","General transcription factors help assemble a preinitiation complex","All nuclear mRNA transcription occurs in the cytoplasm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"RNA polymerase II works with general transcription factors and gene-specific regulatory factors.","reasons":["Sigma factors are associated with bacterial RNA polymerase.","Enhancers are important cis-regulatory elements in eukaryotes.","Factors such as TFIID assist recruitment and positioning of RNA polymerase II.","Nuclear genes are transcribed in the nucleus."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":8}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following is true for transcription in humans? A. Transcription factor IID helps in formation of a preinitiation complex B. Sigma factor helps begin transcription C. Transcription occurs in the cytoplasm D. Enhancers are not involved in the regulation of transcription Answer: A","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following is true for transcription in humans? A. Transcription factor IID helps in formation of a preinitiation complex B. Sigma factor helps begin transcription C. Transcription occurs in the cytoplasm D. Enhancers are not involved in the regulation of transcription Answer: A","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":8,"number":18,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-18"}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-6"},{"id":"expanded-application-4-4","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-2","question":"A mutation at a promoter reduces recruitment of initiation factors while the coding region remains intact. Which step is most directly impaired?","options":["Transcription initiation","Peptidyl transfer on a ribosome","Disulfide reduction","GAG sulfation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Promoter elements help assemble and position transcription machinery. An intact coding region cannot compensate if initiation becomes inefficient.","reasons":["Promoter elements help assemble and position transcription machinery. An intact coding region cannot compensate if initiation becomes inefficient.","Peptidyl transfer occurs later during translation and is not the direct promoter-recruitment step.","Disulfide reduction changes protein covalent crosslinks, unrelated to promoter assembly.","GAG sulfation modifies carbohydrates, not transcription-initiation complexes."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":17}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-6"}],"MOL-4:guide-7":[{"id":"drill-MOL-4-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:2","question":"Which statement best explains template and coding strands?","options":["RNA is complementary to the template and matches the coding strand except U replaces T","RNA polymerase II transcribes most protein-coding nuclear genes","General transcription factors assemble at a core promoter to recruit polymerase II","The 2′ hydroxyl makes RNA more susceptible to alkaline hydrolysis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For coding DNA 5′-ATG-3′, the RNA sequence is 5′-AUG-3′. The template must be read antiparallel. Always write strand direction before matching a sequence.","reasons":["Correct. For coding DNA 5′-ATG-3′, the RNA sequence is 5′-AUG-3′. The template must be read antiparallel. Always write strand direction before matching a sequence.","This explains RNA polymerase II. Its product is a pre-mRNA that requires processing. Polymerase I makes the large rRNA precursor, while polymerase III makes tRNAs and 5S rRNA. Ribosomal protein genes are still protein-coding genes and use polymerase II. For template and coding strands, use the rule: RNA is complementary to the template and matches the coding strand except U replaces T.","This explains Transcription initiation. TBP, part of TFIID, recognizes a TATA element where present. Promoter damage can reduce initiation. A bacterial sigma factor is not a human general transcription factor. Not every human promoter contains a TATA box. For template and coding strands, use the rule: RNA is complementary to the template and matches the coding strand except U replaces T.","This explains RNA versus DNA chemistry. RNA contains ribose rather than deoxyribose. The extra hydroxyl affects chemical stability. RNA can contain purines, pyrimidines and base-paired regions, so its instability is not explained by an inability to pair bases. For template and coding strands, use the rule: RNA is complementary to the template and matches the coding strand except U replaces T."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":25},{"source":"mol-past","page":26},{"source":"mol-past","page":27}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-7"},{"id":"expanded-application-4-1","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:2","question":"The coding DNA strand is 5′-ATTCGA-3′. What is the RNA sequence?","options":["5′-AUUCGA-3′","5′-UAAGCU-3′","5′-TCGAAT-3′","5′-ATTCGA-3′"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RNA matches the coding strand when both are written 5′→3′, with uracil replacing thymine. Complementing the coding strand would confuse it with the template.","reasons":["RNA matches the coding strand when both are written 5′→3′, with uracil replacing thymine. Complementing the coding strand would confuse it with the template.","This complements the coding sequence rather than matching it in the required direction.","This is a DNA reverse-complement-style sequence, not the RNA matching the coding strand.","RNA uses uracil rather than thymine; this option has copied the DNA sequence without replacing T with U."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":19}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-7"}],"MOL-4:guide-8":[{"id":"expanded-application-4-5","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:1","question":"A bacterial RNA polymerase core enzyme is catalytically active but cannot efficiently recognize its usual promoters. Which associated factor is most directly missing?","options":["Sigma factor","Histone H1","Poly-A-binding protein","Mannose-6-phosphate receptor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sigma factor helps bacterial core RNA polymerase recognize promoters and initiate at appropriate sites. Catalytic elongation activity and promoter recognition can therefore be experimentally separated.","reasons":["Sigma factor helps bacterial core RNA polymerase recognize promoters and initiate at appropriate sites. Catalytic elongation activity and promoter recognition can therefore be experimentally separated.","H1 is associated with eukaryotic chromatin linker DNA rather than bacterial promoter recognition.","Poly-A-binding protein acts on RNA tails, not bacterial core-polymerase promoter selection.","M6P receptor sorts lysosomal enzyme cargo in the secretory pathway."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":27}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-8"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-4:guide-8-1","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:1","question":"A bacterial sigma factor is present but the RNA-polymerase core has lost its catalytic activity. Which statement is most accurate?","options":["Sigma alone normally synthesizes the complete RNA without the core","Sigma normally removes introns from the final RNA","The catalytic role is performed by a DNA ligase instead of RNA polymerase","Promoter recognition assistance cannot by itself replace the core’s RNA-synthesis activity"],"answer":3,"reasons":["The section separates promoter-recognition assistance from the core’s catalytic synthesis function.","Sigma is not the spliceosome and does not perform ordinary eukaryotic intron removal.","DNA ligase seals DNA nicks; it does not replace the catalytic RNA-polymerase core.","Sigma helps recognition and initiation positioning, while the polymerase core performs RNA polymerization."],"explanation":"Sigma helps recognition and initiation positioning, while the polymerase core performs RNA polymerization. Sigma helps bacterial promoter recognition. The core enzyme performs RNA polymerization.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":27}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-4:guide-8"}],"MOL-4:guide-9":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-4:slides-2026-4-0","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-4","question":"Which statement best explains polycistronic messages?","options":["A bacterial mRNA can encode several different proteins","RNA polymerase II transcribes most protein-coding nuclear genes","General transcription factors assemble at a core promoter to recruit polymerase II","RNA is complementary to the template and matches the coding strand except U replaces T"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This organization differs from the usual monocistronic eukaryotic protein-coding message. Several ribosomes on one mRNA are a polysome, which is a separate concept and does not require several different genes on that mRNA.","reasons":["Correct. This organization differs from the usual monocistronic eukaryotic protein-coding message. Several ribosomes on one mRNA are a polysome, which is a separate concept and does not require several different genes on that mRNA.","This explains RNA polymerase II. Its product is a pre-mRNA that requires processing. Polymerase I makes the large rRNA precursor, while polymerase III makes tRNAs and 5S rRNA. Ribosomal protein genes are still protein-coding genes and use polymerase II. For the concept asked here, A bacterial mRNA can encode several different proteins.","This explains Transcription initiation. TBP, part of TFIID, recognizes a TATA element where present. Promoter damage can reduce initiation. A bacterial sigma factor is not a human general transcription factor. Not every human promoter contains a TATA box. For the concept asked here, A bacterial mRNA can encode several different proteins.","This explains Template and coding strands. For coding DNA 5′-ATG-3′, the RNA sequence is 5′-AUG-3′. The template must be read antiparallel. Always write strand direction before matching a sequence. For the concept asked here, A bacterial mRNA can encode several different proteins."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":25}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-9"},{"id":"expanded-application-4-6","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-4","question":"One bacterial mRNA contains three coding regions that produce three separate proteins. Which description is correct?","options":["The mRNA is polycistronic","The message is a three-subunit ribosome","The RNA must be translated into one obligatory fusion protein","Each protein must come from a different chromosome"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Polycistronic means one transcript carries several coding regions. Separate initiation and termination signals can permit separate protein products from that one RNA.","reasons":["Polycistronic means one transcript carries several coding regions. Separate initiation and termination signals can permit separate protein products from that one RNA.","A message containing coding regions is not itself a ribosome.","Multiple coding regions do not require a single fused product.","The same transcript can encode products without each originating on a separate chromosome."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":26}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-9"}],"MOL-4:guide-10":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-35","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-5","question":"Rifampin inhibits which enzyme in susceptible bacteria?","options":["DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","RNA-dependent DNA polymerase","RNA-dependent RNA polymerase","DNA-dependent DNA polymerase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It blocks bacterial transcription by acting on bacterial RNA polymerase.","reasons":["It blocks bacterial transcription by acting on bacterial RNA polymerase.","This is reverse transcriptase rather than the bacterial transcription target.","This enzyme uses RNA as template and is not the defining rifampin target.","That enzyme performs DNA replication."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":33}],"originalQuestion":"Rifampin inhibits which one of the following enzymes? A) DNA-dependent DNA polymerase B) DNA-dependent RNA polymerase C) RNA-dependent DNA polymerase D) RNA-dependent RNA polymerase E) Reverse transcriptase Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Rifampin inhibits which one of the following enzymes? A) DNA-dependent DNA polymerase B) DNA-dependent RNA polymerase C) RNA-dependent DNA polymerase D) RNA-dependent RNA polymerase E) Reverse transcriptase Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-4:guide-10"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-31","lectureIds":["MOL-4"],"concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-5","question":"Alpha-amanitin from poisonous mushrooms strongly inhibits which enzyme at low concentrations?","options":["Eukaryotic RNA polymeraseII","RNA polymeraseI","Bacterial DNA gyrase","DNA ligase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PolymeraseII inhibition impairs nuclear mRNA synthesis.","reasons":["PolymeraseII inhibition impairs nuclear mRNA synthesis.","PolymeraseI is relatively resistant compared with polymeraseII.","That enzyme is a target of fluoroquinolones, not alpha-amanitin’s defining target.","Ligase seals DNA nicks and is not the principal alpha-amanitin target."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":30},{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":5},{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":11}],"originalQuestion":"19. The illustration below is a schematic diagram of the DNA sequence of a gene including the exons in black (A-D) and the introns in white(1-3). When the mRNA is first made ( mRNA precursor) which exons and/or introns are included? A) All the exons (A-D) and all the introns (1-3) B) All the introns (1-3) but no exons C) All the exons(A-D) but no introns D) Only the first and last exon ( A and D) E) Can’t say because each mRNA molecule may include different exons Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Completed the source recall into an unambiguous inhibitor-target question.","sourceExcerpt":"19. The illustration below is a schematic diagram of the DNA sequence of a gene including the exons in black (A-D) and the introns in white(1-3). When the mRNA is first made ( mRNA precursor) which exons and/or introns are included? A) All the exons (A-D) and all the introns (1-3) B) All the introns (1-3) but no exons C) All the exons(A-D) but no introns D) Only the first and last exon ( A and D) E) Can’t say because each mRNA molecule may include different exons Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":5,"number":18,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-18"},{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":11,"number":51,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-51"}],"topicId":"MOL-4:guide-10"}],"MOL-5:guide-1":[{"id":"drill-MOL-5-1-0","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:1","question":"Which statement best explains splicing?","options":["Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing","A 7-methylguanosine cap is added to the 5′ end","Cleavage near a polyadenylation signal precedes addition of a 3′ poly-A tail","RNA editing changes the RNA sequence after transcription"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Splice-site changes can produce abnormal mature RNA even when transcription proceeds. Alternative splicing selects different exon combinations and can increase protein diversity. A mutation far inside an intron may have no effect, but intronic regulatory sequences can matter.","reasons":["Correct. Splice-site changes can produce abnormal mature RNA even when transcription proceeds. Alternative splicing selects different exon combinations and can increase protein diversity. A mutation far inside an intron may have no effect, but intronic regulatory sequences can matter.","This explains mRNA capping. The cap supports protection, export and translation initiation. It is not a poly-A tail and is not attached to the 3′ end. Think of the cap as an early processing mark recognized by protein machinery. For splicing, use the rule: Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing.","This explains Polyadenylation. The AAUAAA signal helps recruit processing machinery. A disrupted signal can impair cleavage and tail addition. The tail is added after transcriptional cleavage, not copied from a long run of template thymidines. For splicing, use the rule: Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing.","This explains RNA editing. A classic comparison is tissue-specific apoB RNA editing, which can introduce an earlier stop. Editing changes bases; alternative splicing changes exon combinations. Both affect RNA products without requiring a different genomic sequence in each tissue. For splicing, use the rule: Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":39},{"source":"mol-past","page":40},{"source":"mol-past","page":41}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-1"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-29","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:1","question":"Before splicing, a newly transcribed eukaryotic pre-mRNA has which arrangement corresponding to exon–intron–exon genomic sequence?","options":["No RNA until all introns disappear from DNA","Exon–intron–exon","Exon–exon only","Intron only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The intron is transcribed into pre-mRNA and removed during splicing.","reasons":["Introns remain in genomic DNA while processing removes them from RNA.","The intron is transcribed into pre-mRNA and removed during splicing.","This describes the spliced product for the stated region.","Pre-mRNA contains transcribed exons as well as introns; splicing later removes the intron."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":29}],"originalQuestion":"What is the eukaryotic mRNA precursor of this strand: Exon-intron-Exon? A) Intron - Exon B) Exon - Exon C) Intron D) Exon - intron - Exon Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"What is the eukaryotic mRNA precursor of this strand: Exon-intron-Exon? A) Intron - Exon B) Exon - Exon C) Intron D) Exon - intron - Exon Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-5:guide-1"}],"MOL-5:guide-2":[{"id":"expanded-application-5-1","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:0","question":"Which feature is unusual about the linkage between the mRNA 5′ cap and the transcript?","options":["It is a 5′–5′ triphosphate linkage","It is a peptide bond to methionine","It is an ordinary 3′–5′ bond between two unmodified adenines","It joins the cap to the poly-A tail directly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 7-methylguanosine cap uses a 5′–5′ triphosphate connection, unlike the ordinary 3′–5′ backbone. This specialized end supports stability, export and translation initiation.","reasons":["The 7-methylguanosine cap uses a 5′–5′ triphosphate connection, unlike the ordinary 3′–5′ backbone. This specialized end supports stability, export and translation initiation.","Peptide bonds are protein links and do not form the cap attachment.","The cap contains modified guanosine and an unusual linkage, not two ordinary adenines.","The cap is at the 5′ end; the poly-A tail is at the opposite end."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":8}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-2"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-40","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:0","question":"What chemical structure is added to the 5′ end of most eukaryotic mRNAs?","options":["A methylated adenine attached by a peptide bond","A histone octamer","A3′ poly-A tail at the 5′ end","A7-methylguanosine cap linked through a5′–5′ triphosphate bridge"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The unusual cap protects the RNA and participates in processing, export and translation initiation.","reasons":["The conventional cap contains methylguanosine and a phosphate linkage.","Histones package DNA rather than form the mRNA cap.","Poly-A is added at the 3′ end and is distinct from the cap.","The unusual cap protects the RNA and participates in processing, export and translation initiation."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":41}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following is true about the cap at the 5’ end? A) It has a methylated guanine B) It has a methylated adenine Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Completed a two-option source question and supplied the cap-linkage detail.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following is true about the cap at the 5’ end? A) It has a methylated guanine B) It has a methylated adenine Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-5:guide-2"}],"MOL-5:guide-3":[{"id":"expanded-application-5-2","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:2","question":"A normal polyadenylation signal is present. Which sequence of events produces the usual 3′ poly-A tail?","options":["RNA cleavage followed by addition of adenosines by poly-A polymerase","Translation of a polylysine segment followed by DNA synthesis","Removal of the 5′ cap and ligation of guanosines at 3′","Copying a long mandatory DNA poly-T tract without RNA cleavage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The transcript is cleaved downstream of the recognition signal, creating a new 3′ end to which poly-A polymerase adds adenosines. The tail is not simply copied from one long template sequence.","reasons":["The transcript is cleaved downstream of the recognition signal, creating a new 3′ end to which poly-A polymerase adds adenosines. The tail is not simply copied from one long template sequence.","A protein sequence does not create the RNA tail.","Cap removal and guanosine addition do not describe normal polyadenylation.","A long genomic poly-T run is not the required template for each tail adenosine."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":9}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-3"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-11","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:2","question":"Changing an mRNA polyadenylation signal from AAUAAA to AAUAAG is most likely to cause what?","options":["A longer cap at the 3′ end","Less efficient cleavage and poly-A addition","Guaranteed recruitment of more polyadenylation enzymes","Automatic correction by RNA editing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AAUAAA helps identify where pre-mRNA is cleaved before poly-A polymerase extends the new 3′ end.","reasons":["The cap is a 5′ modification and is distinct from 3′ polyadenylation.","The signal recruits the 3′-end processing machinery, so mutation can reduce normal cleavage and polyadenylation.","A signal-disrupting mutation generally reduces correct recognition rather than necessarily enhancing it.","RNA editing does not generally repair a defective polyadenylation signal."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":3}],"originalQuestion":"A mutation changes mRNA polyadenylation signal sequence from 5′-AAUAAA-3′ to 5′-AAUAAG-3′. What effect will this have on post transcriptional processing? A) RNA editing will correct the defective sequence B) Poly-A tail synthesis is reduced because endonuclease cleavage can’t occur efficiently C) Increased recruitment of polyadenylation enzymes D) A more stable mRNA is produced Answer: B","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"A mutation changes mRNA polyadenylation signal sequence from 5′-AAUAAA-3′ to 5′-AAUAAG-3′. What effect will this have on post transcriptional processing? A) RNA editing will correct the defective sequence B) Poly-A tail synthesis is reduced because endonuclease cleavage can’t occur efficiently C) Increased recruitment of polyadenylation enzymes D) A more stable mRNA is produced Answer: B","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":3,"number":11,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-11"}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-3"}],"MOL-5:guide-4":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-5:slides-2026-1-0","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-1","question":"Which statement best explains spliceosome assembly?","options":["U1 recognizes the 5′ splice site and U2 recognizes the branch-point region","A 7-methylguanosine cap is added to the 5′ end","Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing","Cleavage near a polyadenylation signal precedes addition of a 3′ poly-A tail"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The spliceosome includes U1, U2, U4, U5 and U6 snRNPs. These are RNA–protein particles. Individual snRNPs are components of the larger machine rather than exact synonyms for the entire assembled spliceosome.","reasons":["Correct. The spliceosome includes U1, U2, U4, U5 and U6 snRNPs. These are RNA–protein particles. Individual snRNPs are components of the larger machine rather than exact synonyms for the entire assembled spliceosome.","This explains mRNA capping. The cap supports protection, export and translation initiation. It is not a poly-A tail and is not attached to the 3′ end. Think of the cap as an early processing mark recognized by protein machinery. For the concept asked here, U1 recognizes the 5′ splice site and U2 recognizes the branch-point region.","This explains Splicing. Splice-site changes can produce abnormal mature RNA even when transcription proceeds. Alternative splicing selects different exon combinations and can increase protein diversity. A mutation far inside an intron may have no effect, but intronic regulatory sequences can matter. For the concept asked here, U1 recognizes the 5′ splice site and U2 recognizes the branch-point region.","This explains Polyadenylation. The AAUAAA signal helps recruit processing machinery. A disrupted signal can impair cleavage and tail addition. The tail is added after transcriptional cleavage, not copied from a long run of template thymidines. For the concept asked here, U1 recognizes the 5′ splice site and U2 recognizes the branch-point region."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":13},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":111}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-4"},{"id":"mol-slides-5-application-1","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-1","question":"Which pairing of spliceosomal recognition roles is correct?","options":["U1 at the 5′ splice site and U2 at the branch point","U1 at the poly-A tail and U2 at a stop codon","Ribosomes at both splice boundaries","Proteasomes at the branch point"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The supplied deck identifies U1 and U2 as recognizing the 5′ splice site and branch-point region in spliceosome assembly.","reasons":["This matches the recognition roles described.","These are not the listed U1/U2 targets.","Ribosomes translate mRNA rather than assembling the nuclear spliceosome.","Proteasomes degrade proteins; they are not the branch-point recognition particle."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":13}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-4"}],"MOL-5:guide-5":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-5:slides-2026-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-2","question":"Which statement best explains lariat chemistry?","options":["The branch-point adenosine helps form an intron lariat through transesterification","A 7-methylguanosine cap is added to the 5′ end","Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing","Cleavage near a polyadenylation signal precedes addition of a 3′ poly-A tail"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Two transesterification steps excise the intron and join the exons. ATP supports assembly and remodeling of the machinery; it is not used simply to supply the bond energy for each transesterification. Read the GU–AG boundaries and branch point together.","reasons":["Correct. Two transesterification steps excise the intron and join the exons. ATP supports assembly and remodeling of the machinery; it is not used simply to supply the bond energy for each transesterification. Read the GU–AG boundaries and branch point together.","This explains mRNA capping. The cap supports protection, export and translation initiation. It is not a poly-A tail and is not attached to the 3′ end. Think of the cap as an early processing mark recognized by protein machinery. For the concept asked here, The branch-point adenosine helps form an intron lariat through transesterification.","This explains Splicing. Splice-site changes can produce abnormal mature RNA even when transcription proceeds. Alternative splicing selects different exon combinations and can increase protein diversity. A mutation far inside an intron may have no effect, but intronic regulatory sequences can matter. For the concept asked here, The branch-point adenosine helps form an intron lariat through transesterification.","This explains Polyadenylation. The AAUAAA signal helps recruit processing machinery. A disrupted signal can impair cleavage and tail addition. The tail is added after transcriptional cleavage, not copied from a long run of template thymidines. For the concept asked here, The branch-point adenosine helps form an intron lariat through transesterification."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":13},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":111}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-5"},{"id":"expanded-application-5-3","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-2","question":"Which hydroxyl begins formation of the intron lariat in major spliceosome splicing?","options":["The 2′-OH of branch-point adenosine","The 3′-OH of the final poly-A tail","A serine side-chain hydroxyl in hemoglobin","The 5′-OH of the DNA coding strand"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The branch-point A 2′ hydroxyl attacks the 5′ splice-site phosphate, forming the unusual 2′–5′ lariat linkage. The released exon 3′-OH is used in the second reaction.","reasons":["The branch-point A 2′ hydroxyl attacks the 5′ splice-site phosphate, forming the unusual 2′–5′ lariat linkage. The released exon 3′-OH is used in the second reaction.","The poly-A tail does not initiate the intron branch reaction.","Splicing chemistry concerns RNA hydroxyls rather than a hemoglobin residue.","The DNA coding strand is not the RNA substrate undergoing spliceosome bond exchange."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":13}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-5"}],"MOL-5:guide-6":[{"id":"expanded-application-5-4","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:1","question":"Two cell types produce mRNAs with different exon combinations from the same gene. Which mechanism best explains this?","options":["Alternative splicing","A different genetic code in each cell","Mandatory deletion of the unused genomic exons","Conversion of every intron into protein"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alternative splicing selects different exon connections from a pre-mRNA. The genomic gene can remain the same while tissue-specific processing produces different RNA isoforms.","reasons":["Alternative splicing selects different exon connections from a pre-mRNA. The genomic gene can remain the same while tissue-specific processing produces different RNA isoforms.","The ordinary codon table need not change to produce splice isoforms.","RNA processing does not require physically deleting the unchosen exons from the genome.","Introns are ordinarily removed from the relevant mature transcript rather than all translated."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":14}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-6"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-38","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:1","question":"How can one gene generate several mature mRNAs with different exon combinations?","options":["DNA transposition as the obligatory processing step","Identical splicing with no alternative choices","Alternative splicing","rRNA processing alone"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Different combinations of exons can be joined from the same pre-mRNA, producing distinct protein isoforms.","reasons":["DNA movement is not the routine mechanism that joins different exon combinations.","Identical exon choices cannot explain different exon combinations.","Different combinations of exons can be joined from the same pre-mRNA, producing distinct protein isoforms.","Ribosomal RNA processing does not select exons from a protein-coding pre-mRNA."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":39}],"originalQuestion":"The completion of the human genome project demonstrated that the human genome codes for approximately 25000 genes. However, these limited numbers of genes encode several hundreds of thousands of different proteins. Which of the following processes provides the majority of the additional diversity of the protein products from these genes? A) DNA transposition B) RNA editing C) mRNA splicing D) rRNA processing E) tRNA modification Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"The completion of the human genome project demonstrated that the human genome codes for approximately 25000 genes. However, these limited numbers of genes encode several hundreds of thousands of different proteins. Which of the following processes provides the majority of the additional diversity of the protein products from these genes? A) DNA transposition B) RNA editing C) mRNA splicing D) rRNA processing E) tRNA modification Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-5:guide-6"}],"MOL-5:guide-7":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-5:slides-2026-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-3","question":"Which statement best explains silent changes can alter splicing?","options":["A synonymous coding change can still cause disease by activating an abnormal splice site","A 7-methylguanosine cap is added to the 5′ end","Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing","Cleavage near a polyadenylation signal precedes addition of a 3′ poly-A tail"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The progeria example changes RNA processing even though the codon change is synonymous in the usual reading frame. Predicting a variant’s effect requires considering RNA processing, not only the amino-acid table.","reasons":["Correct. The progeria example changes RNA processing even though the codon change is synonymous in the usual reading frame. Predicting a variant’s effect requires considering RNA processing, not only the amino-acid table.","This explains mRNA capping. The cap supports protection, export and translation initiation. It is not a poly-A tail and is not attached to the 3′ end. Think of the cap as an early processing mark recognized by protein machinery. For the concept asked here, A synonymous coding change can still cause disease by activating an abnormal splice site.","This explains Splicing. Splice-site changes can produce abnormal mature RNA even when transcription proceeds. Alternative splicing selects different exon combinations and can increase protein diversity. A mutation far inside an intron may have no effect, but intronic regulatory sequences can matter. For the concept asked here, A synonymous coding change can still cause disease by activating an abnormal splice site.","This explains Polyadenylation. The AAUAAA signal helps recruit processing machinery. A disrupted signal can impair cleavage and tail addition. The tail is added after transcriptional cleavage, not copied from a long run of template thymidines. For the concept asked here, A synonymous coding change can still cause disease by activating an abnormal splice site."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":22},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":120}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-7"},{"id":"expanded-application-5-5","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-3","question":"A synonymous LMNA change activates an abnormal splice site. Why can the variant still be harmful?","options":["It can alter processing and the final protein despite retaining the original codon meaning","Synonymous always means no biological effect","It must change the entire DNA molecule into RNA","It converts all exons into stop codons"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A synonymous description concerns the immediate codon’s amino-acid meaning. The same nucleotide can also participate in RNA-processing signals; altered splicing can produce an abnormal protein product.","reasons":["A synonymous description concerns the immediate codon’s amino-acid meaning. The same nucleotide can also participate in RNA-processing signals; altered splicing can produce an abnormal protein product.","Synonymous changes can affect splice regulation, RNA stability or translation and are not automatically neutral.","Transcription copies information without converting the entire DNA molecule into RNA.","One splice-regulatory variant does not need to change every exon into stop signals."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":21}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-7"}],"MOL-5:guide-8":[{"id":"drill-MOL-5-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:3","question":"Which statement best explains rna editing?","options":["RNA editing changes the RNA sequence after transcription","A 7-methylguanosine cap is added to the 5′ end","Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing","Cleavage near a polyadenylation signal precedes addition of a 3′ poly-A tail"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A classic comparison is tissue-specific apoB RNA editing, which can introduce an earlier stop. Editing changes bases; alternative splicing changes exon combinations. Both affect RNA products without requiring a different genomic sequence in each tissue.","reasons":["Correct. A classic comparison is tissue-specific apoB RNA editing, which can introduce an earlier stop. Editing changes bases; alternative splicing changes exon combinations. Both affect RNA products without requiring a different genomic sequence in each tissue.","This explains mRNA capping. The cap supports protection, export and translation initiation. It is not a poly-A tail and is not attached to the 3′ end. Think of the cap as an early processing mark recognized by protein machinery. For rna editing, use the rule: RNA editing changes the RNA sequence after transcription.","This explains Splicing. Splice-site changes can produce abnormal mature RNA even when transcription proceeds. Alternative splicing selects different exon combinations and can increase protein diversity. A mutation far inside an intron may have no effect, but intronic regulatory sequences can matter. For rna editing, use the rule: RNA editing changes the RNA sequence after transcription.","This explains Polyadenylation. The AAUAAA signal helps recruit processing machinery. A disrupted signal can impair cleavage and tail addition. The tail is added after transcriptional cleavage, not copied from a long run of template thymidines. For rna editing, use the rule: RNA editing changes the RNA sequence after transcription."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":39},{"source":"mol-past","page":40},{"source":"mol-past","page":41}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-8"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-5:guide-8-1","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:3","question":"Which example most directly illustrates RNA editing rather than ordinary intron splicing?","options":["An intron is removed and the adjoining exons are joined","The DNA template is copied into a new pre-mRNA","A peptide is hydrolyzed by a protease","A selected RNA nucleotide is changed after transcription without the same change in DNA"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Removing introns and joining exons describes splicing rather than the editing distinction asked here.","Producing the RNA from DNA is transcription, preceding the RNA-editing event.","Proteolysis acts on protein rather than selectively changing RNA nucleotide sequence.","RNA editing changes selected nucleotide identity or content after transcription and need not reflect an identical DNA change."],"explanation":"RNA editing changes selected nucleotide identity or content after transcription and need not reflect an identical DNA change. Splicing changes segment connections. Editing changes selected RNA residues or sequence content.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":23}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-5:guide-8"}],"MOL-5:guide-9":[{"id":"expanded-application-5-6","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-4","question":"Intestinal editing changes an apoB RNA codon into a stop signal through C-to-U deamination. What is the direct consequence?","options":["A shorter protein can be made without the same stop mutation in DNA","The ribosome changes U back to C before translation","All apoB introns must be retained","The edited RNA necessarily encodes a longer protein"],"answer":0,"explanation":"C-to-U editing can turn a sense codon into a stop codon, producing apoB-48 in the teaching example. The change occurs in RNA and need not be a corresponding genomic stop mutation.","reasons":["C-to-U editing can turn a sense codon into a stop codon, producing apoB-48 in the teaching example. The change occurs in RNA and need not be a corresponding genomic stop mutation.","The editing outcome can be read during translation rather than automatically reversed by the ribosome.","This is a base-editing mechanism, not obligatory retention of all introns.","A premature termination signal shortens the translated product rather than extending it."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":27}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-9"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-41","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:3","question":"Failure of intestinal C-to-U editing of apolipoproteinB mRNA would most directly prevent production of which normal shorter product?","options":["The DNA polymerase primer","ApoB-100 in the liver","ApoB-48","Every ribosomal RNA"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Editing changes a glutamine codon to a stop codon in intestinal apoB RNA, producing the shorter ApoB-48 form.","reasons":["This protein-coding RNA editing event is separate from DNA-primer production.","The longer liver product is normally made without this intestinal editing event.","Editing changes a glutamine codon to a stop codon in intestinal apoB RNA, producing the shorter ApoB-48 form.","ApoB editing does not define global rRNA synthesis."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":42}],"originalQuestion":"A patient participating in a metabolic study has normal mRNA sizes for the apoB gene in all tissues. However, the apoB protein is 100-kDa in both the liver and intestine, whereas, in healthy individuals, it is 100-kDa in the liver and 48-kDa in the intestine. This suggests a deficiency in which of the following processes? A) Transcription factor phosphorylation B) RNA splicing C) Transcription initiation D) DNA amplification E) RNA editing Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Corrected source shorthand100-kDa/48-kDa:100 and48 in ApoB names refer to relative sequence lengths, not those literal molecular masses.","sourceExcerpt":"A patient participating in a metabolic study has normal mRNA sizes for the apoB gene in all tissues. However, the apoB protein is 100-kDa in both the liver and intestine, whereas, in healthy individuals, it is 100-kDa in the liver and 48-kDa in the intestine. This suggests a deficiency in which of the following processes? A) Transcription factor phosphorylation B) RNA splicing C) Transcription initiation D) DNA amplification E) RNA editing Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-5:guide-9"}],"MOL-5:guide-10":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-5:slides-2026-5-0","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-5","question":"Which statement best explains species-specific editing?","options":["Guide-RNA-directed uridine insertion and deletion in kinetoplastid mitochondria is not the human editing pathway","A 7-methylguanosine cap is added to the 5′ end","Introns are removed and exons are joined in pre-mRNA processing","Cleavage near a polyadenylation signal precedes addition of a 3′ poly-A tail"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The deck separates this pathway from mammalian deamination editing. Do not transfer an example from one organism to every human cell merely because both are called RNA editing.","reasons":["Correct. The deck separates this pathway from mammalian deamination editing. Do not transfer an example from one organism to every human cell merely because both are called RNA editing.","This explains mRNA capping. The cap supports protection, export and translation initiation. It is not a poly-A tail and is not attached to the 3′ end. Think of the cap as an early processing mark recognized by protein machinery. For the concept asked here, Guide-RNA-directed uridine insertion and deletion in kinetoplastid mitochondria is not the human editing pathway.","This explains Splicing. Splice-site changes can produce abnormal mature RNA even when transcription proceeds. Alternative splicing selects different exon combinations and can increase protein diversity. A mutation far inside an intron may have no effect, but intronic regulatory sequences can matter. For the concept asked here, Guide-RNA-directed uridine insertion and deletion in kinetoplastid mitochondria is not the human editing pathway.","This explains Polyadenylation. The AAUAAA signal helps recruit processing machinery. A disrupted signal can impair cleavage and tail addition. The tail is added after transcriptional cleavage, not copied from a long run of template thymidines. For the concept asked here, Guide-RNA-directed uridine insertion and deletion in kinetoplastid mitochondria is not the human editing pathway."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":26},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":123}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-10"},{"id":"expanded-application-5-7","lectureIds":["MOL-5"],"concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-5","question":"Which process belongs to the kinetoplastid mitochondrial example rather than the standard human ADAR pathway?","options":["Guide-RNA-directed uridine insertion and deletion","Adenosine deamination to inosine","Addition of a 7-methylguanosine cap","Recognition of an AUG by a ribosome"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The kinetoplastid example uses guide RNAs to direct U insertion and deletion. Human ADAR editing instead deaminates adenosine to inosine, so the species context is essential.","reasons":["The kinetoplastid example uses guide RNAs to direct U insertion and deletion. Human ADAR editing instead deaminates adenosine to inosine, so the species context is essential.","A-to-I conversion describes the mammalian ADAR mechanism in this comparison.","Capping is end processing and is not the guide-RNA insertion/deletion pathway.","Start-codon recognition is translation initiation rather than RNA editing."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":25}],"topicId":"MOL-5:guide-10"}],"MOL-6:guide-1":[{"id":"expanded-application-6-1","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:1","question":"Which statement correctly uses the term degenerate for the standard genetic code?","options":["Several codons can specify the same amino acid","Each codon randomly specifies several different amino acids","Every codon specifies a stop signal","Only one codon is used in all proteins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Degeneracy means multiple codons can encode the same amino acid. It does not make an individual ordinary codon ambiguous about its specified amino acid.","reasons":["Degeneracy means multiple codons can encode the same amino acid. It does not make an individual ordinary codon ambiguous about its specified amino acid.","Random multiple meanings for a codon would be ambiguity, not ordinary code degeneracy.","Only three standard codons are stops; most are sense codons.","Protein sequences require many different codons and amino-acid assignments."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":4}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-1"},{"id":"drill-MOL-6-1-0","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:1","question":"Which statement best explains genetic code?","options":["AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start","The A site accepts aminoacyl-tRNA, the P site holds peptidyl-tRNA and the E site releases tRNA","A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid","The peptide grows from its amino terminus toward its carboxyl terminus"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The RNA codon is read 5′ to 3′ and the anticodon pairs antiparallel. UAA, UAG and UGA are stop signals recognized by release factors, not ordinary tRNAs. Code degeneracy allows different codons to specify the same amino acid.","reasons":["Correct. The RNA codon is read 5′ to 3′ and the anticodon pairs antiparallel. UAA, UAG and UGA are stop signals recognized by release factors, not ordinary tRNAs. Code degeneracy allows different codons to specify the same amino acid.","This explains Ribosomal sites. During elongation, the growing peptide is transferred to the amino acid on the A-site tRNA and translocation advances the complex. The initiator tRNA initially occupies the P site. Blocking translocation stalls elongation. For genetic code, use the rule: AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start.","This explains Mutation consequences. UAC to UAA converts a tyrosine codon to a stop. A silent change preserves the encoded amino acid. An insertion or deletion shifts the reading frame only when its length is not a multiple of three within the coding region. For genetic code, use the rule: AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start.","This explains Protein elongation. The ribosome reads mRNA from 5′ to 3′. tRNA charging uses aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases; peptide-bond formation occurs in the ribosome. Charging, decoding and peptide transfer are separate steps. For genetic code, use the rule: AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":46},{"source":"mol-past","page":47},{"source":"mol-past","page":48}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-1"}],"MOL-6:guide-2":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-38","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-2","question":"How can degeneracy of the genetic code reduce the effect of some point mutations?","options":["Every codon must be read multiple times before peptide transfer","Different codons can encode the same amino acid","Only one codon exists for each amino acid","Every altered codon is automatically repaired by tRNA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because the code is degenerate, some substitutions preserve the amino acid. Degeneracy does not make all mutations harmless.","reasons":["Degeneracy concerns mapping of codons to amino acids, not obligatory repeated reading.","A nucleotide substitution can be synonymous, especially at some third codon positions.","Most amino acids have more than one codon.","tRNA decoding is not a general mRNA repair system."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":9}],"originalQuestion":"A biology student learns that there are more tRNA molecules than amino acids. The question mentioned degeneracy, what is the benefit of all this? A) Codons are reread multiple times to ensure perfect matching. B) tRNA acts like a buffer, allowing some mutations to be tolerated C) Only one tRNA exists per amino acid, guaranteeing accuracy. D) Mutations in mRNA are automatically repaired during translation. Answer: B","adaptationNote":"Replaced vague tRNA acts like a buffer wording with the synonymous-codon mechanism.","sourceExcerpt":"A biology student learns that there are more tRNA molecules than amino acids. The question mentioned degeneracy, what is the benefit of all this? A) Codons are reread multiple times to ensure perfect matching. B) tRNA acts like a buffer, allowing some mutations to be tolerated C) Only one tRNA exists per amino acid, guaranteeing accuracy. D) Mutations in mRNA are automatically repaired during translation. Answer: B","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":9,"number":38,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-38"}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-2"},{"id":"drill-MOL-6-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:2","question":"Which statement best explains mutation consequences?","options":["A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid","The A site accepts aminoacyl-tRNA, the P site holds peptidyl-tRNA and the E site releases tRNA","AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start","The peptide grows from its amino terminus toward its carboxyl terminus"],"answer":0,"explanation":"UAC to UAA converts a tyrosine codon to a stop. A silent change preserves the encoded amino acid. An insertion or deletion shifts the reading frame only when its length is not a multiple of three within the coding region.","reasons":["Correct. UAC to UAA converts a tyrosine codon to a stop. A silent change preserves the encoded amino acid. An insertion or deletion shifts the reading frame only when its length is not a multiple of three within the coding region.","This explains Ribosomal sites. During elongation, the growing peptide is transferred to the amino acid on the A-site tRNA and translocation advances the complex. The initiator tRNA initially occupies the P site. Blocking translocation stalls elongation. For mutation consequences, use the rule: A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid.","This explains Genetic code. The RNA codon is read 5′ to 3′ and the anticodon pairs antiparallel. UAA, UAG and UGA are stop signals recognized by release factors, not ordinary tRNAs. Code degeneracy allows different codons to specify the same amino acid. For mutation consequences, use the rule: A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid.","This explains Protein elongation. The ribosome reads mRNA from 5′ to 3′. tRNA charging uses aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases; peptide-bond formation occurs in the ribosome. Charging, decoding and peptide transfer are separate steps. For mutation consequences, use the rule: A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":46},{"source":"mol-past","page":47},{"source":"mol-past","page":48}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-2"}],"MOL-6:guide-3":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-6:slides-2026-0-0","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-0","question":"Which statement best explains reading-frame changes?","options":["A coding insertion or deletion shifts the frame when its length is not a multiple of three","The A site accepts aminoacyl-tRNA, the P site holds peptidyl-tRNA and the E site releases tRNA","AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start","A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A three-base deletion can remove one amino acid without shifting all later codons. The ΔF508 example therefore differs from a one- or two-base frameshift. State whether the change is in the coding sequence before applying the rule.","reasons":["Correct. A three-base deletion can remove one amino acid without shifting all later codons. The ΔF508 example therefore differs from a one- or two-base frameshift. State whether the change is in the coding sequence before applying the rule.","This explains Ribosomal sites. During elongation, the growing peptide is transferred to the amino acid on the A-site tRNA and translocation advances the complex. The initiator tRNA initially occupies the P site. Blocking translocation stalls elongation. For the concept asked here, A coding insertion or deletion shifts the frame when its length is not a multiple of three.","This explains Genetic code. The RNA codon is read 5′ to 3′ and the anticodon pairs antiparallel. UAA, UAG and UGA are stop signals recognized by release factors, not ordinary tRNAs. Code degeneracy allows different codons to specify the same amino acid. For the concept asked here, A coding insertion or deletion shifts the frame when its length is not a multiple of three.","This explains Mutation consequences. UAC to UAA converts a tyrosine codon to a stop. A silent change preserves the encoded amino acid. An insertion or deletion shifts the reading frame only when its length is not a multiple of three within the coding region. For the concept asked here, A coding insertion or deletion shifts the frame when its length is not a multiple of three."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":6},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":135}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-3"},{"id":"mol-slides-6-application-1","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-0","question":"A coding sequence loses exactly three bases and one amino acid, while downstream reading remains aligned. Which description fits?","options":["A frameshift is inevitable","An in-frame deletion","A new stop codon is required","A change only in a poly-A tail"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A three-base coding deletion can remove one residue without shifting all later codons, as in the ΔF508 example.","reasons":["A frameshift follows changes not divisible by three in the coding frame.","An in-frame deletion preserves downstream codon grouping.","The deletion need not introduce a stop codon.","The change described is in a coding sequence, not only a tail."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":13}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-3"}],"MOL-6:guide-4":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-49","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-1","question":"At which part of tRNA is an amino acid attached during charging?","options":["The terminal adenosine of the 3′-CCA end","The5′ phosphate as a peptide bond","The anticodon loop directly","A random internal base without enzyme specificity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases esterify the amino acid to the terminal ribose of the 3′ end.","reasons":["Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases esterify the amino acid to the terminal ribose of the 3′ end.","The amino acid is ester-linked at the 3′ terminal ribose.","The anticodon recognizes mRNA; it is not the amino-acid attachment end.","Synthetases recognize specific tRNAs and amino acids."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":54}],"originalQuestion":"At which labelled site the amino acid binds in tRNA molecule? A) A B) B C) C D) D Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed the missing labeled tRNA figure as a named-site question.","sourceExcerpt":"At which labelled site the amino acid binds in tRNA molecule? A) A B) B C) C D) D Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-6:guide-4"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-28","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:1","question":"A tRNA anticodon is 3′-UAC-5′. Which mRNA codon does it pair with, and which amino acid does that codon specify?","options":["5′-CAU-3′, histidine","5′-AUG-3′, no amino acid because it is a start signal","5′-AUG-3′, methionine","5′-ATG-3′, methionine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Anticodon and codon are antiparallel. AUG pairs with UAC and encodes methionine.","reasons":["CAU is not the antiparallel complement of the stated anticodon.","AUG can establish initiation and also specifies methionine.","Antiparallel complementary pairing gives AUG, a methionine codon.","ATG is DNA notation; RNA uses U instead of T."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":6}],"originalQuestion":"A tRNA with anticodon 3’ UAC 5’ binds to mRNA. What is the corresponding codon and the amino acid it carries? A) 5’ ATG 3’ - methionine B) 5’ CAU 3’ - methionine C) 5’ AUG 3’ - start codon (no amino acid) D) 5’ AUG’ 3’ - methionine E) 5’ CAU 3’ - histidine Answer: D","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"A tRNA with anticodon 3’ UAC 5’ binds to mRNA. What is the corresponding codon and the amino acid it carries? A) 5’ ATG 3’ - methionine B) 5’ CAU 3’ - methionine C) 5’ AUG 3’ - start codon (no amino acid) D) 5’ AUG’ 3’ - methionine E) 5’ CAU 3’ - histidine Answer: D","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":6,"number":28,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-28"}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-4"}],"MOL-6:guide-5":[{"id":"mol-slides-6-application-2","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-1","question":"Which enzyme attaches an amino acid to the correct tRNA?","options":["RNA polymerase II","Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase","DNA topoisomerase","Peptidyl-tRNA release factor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases charge tRNAs with the appropriate amino acids using ATP.","reasons":["Polymerase II transcribes RNA rather than charging tRNA.","This enzyme matches the amino acid to its adapter.","Topoisomerase changes DNA topology.","A termination factor releases the peptide rather than charging tRNA."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":18}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-5"},{"id":"expanded-application-6-3","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-1","question":"A tRNA is correctly paired to a codon but carries an incorrectly attached amino acid. Which prior quality-control step most directly failed?","options":["Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase selection or editing","DNA ligase nick sealing","M6P receptor recognition","RNA polymerase I promoter binding"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Synthetases match amino acids to the appropriate tRNAs and often proofread charging. The ribosome largely uses codon–anticodon pairing and cannot simply infer the correct attached amino acid from its identity alone.","reasons":["Synthetases match amino acids to the appropriate tRNAs and often proofread charging. The ribosome largely uses codon–anticodon pairing and cannot simply infer the correct attached amino acid from its identity alone.","DNA ligase acts on DNA backbone nicks, not tRNA charging.","M6P recognition sorts lysosomal protein cargo.","Pol I transcription does not directly attach amino acids to tRNAs."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":18}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-5"}],"MOL-6:guide-6":[{"id":"expanded-application-6-4","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-2","question":"The wobble position is which pairing relationship?","options":["The third codon base with the first anticodon base","The first codon base with the first anticodon base","Any base in DNA with any amino acid","A peptide bond with a promoter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Codon and anticodon pair antiparallel. The first anticodon position pairs with the third codon position, where selected nonstandard pairings can permit recognition of synonymous codons.","reasons":["Codon and anticodon pair antiparallel. The first anticodon position pairs with the third codon position, where selected nonstandard pairings can permit recognition of synonymous codons.","Antiparallel alignment places the first codon base opposite the third anticodon base.","Wobble remains a constrained RNA base-pairing phenomenon, not arbitrary recognition across molecular types.","Peptide bonds and promoters do not pair in decoding."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":17}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-6"},{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-6:slides-2026-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-2","question":"Which statement best explains wobble pairing?","options":["Wobble permits selected nonstandard pairings at the third codon position","The A site accepts aminoacyl-tRNA, the P site holds peptidyl-tRNA and the E site releases tRNA","AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start","A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The first anticodon position pairs with the third codon position because binding is antiparallel. Wobble contributes to decoding degeneracy; it is not random pairing at every codon base.","reasons":["Correct. The first anticodon position pairs with the third codon position because binding is antiparallel. Wobble contributes to decoding degeneracy; it is not random pairing at every codon base.","This explains Ribosomal sites. During elongation, the growing peptide is transferred to the amino acid on the A-site tRNA and translocation advances the complex. The initiator tRNA initially occupies the P site. Blocking translocation stalls elongation. For the concept asked here, Wobble permits selected nonstandard pairings at the third codon position.","This explains Genetic code. The RNA codon is read 5′ to 3′ and the anticodon pairs antiparallel. UAA, UAG and UGA are stop signals recognized by release factors, not ordinary tRNAs. Code degeneracy allows different codons to specify the same amino acid. For the concept asked here, Wobble permits selected nonstandard pairings at the third codon position.","This explains Mutation consequences. UAC to UAA converts a tyrosine codon to a stop. A silent change preserves the encoded amino acid. An insertion or deletion shifts the reading frame only when its length is not a multiple of three within the coding region. For the concept asked here, Wobble permits selected nonstandard pairings at the third codon position."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":17},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":146}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-6"}],"MOL-6:guide-7":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-32","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-3","question":"A drug specifically blocks bacterial small-subunit binding to the Shine–Dalgarno region of an mRNA. Which site is directly affected?","options":["A 3′ eukaryotic poly-A tail","A nuclear splice junction","A 5′ poly-A tail on every bacterial message","The 5′ untranslated ribosome-binding region"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A mere absence of a cap does not define a unique antibiotic mechanism. This reconstruction states the bacterial target explicitly.","reasons":["That is not the bacterial Shine–Dalgarno binding site.","The target is bacterial translation initiation rather than nuclear splicing.","This does not describe the normal bacterial initiation element.","The bacterial ribosome-binding sequence positions the start codon during initiation."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":13}],"originalQuestion":"An antibiotic administered only works on prokaryotic mRNA, which of the following is the site of action? A. 3’ uncapped region B. 5’ uncapped region C. 3’ polyA tail D. 5’ polyA tail Answer: B","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed the underdetermined uncapped-region antibiotic question around a specified Shine–Dalgarno target.","sourceExcerpt":"An antibiotic administered only works on prokaryotic mRNA, which of the following is the site of action? A. 3’ uncapped region B. 5’ uncapped region C. 3’ polyA tail D. 5’ polyA tail Answer: B","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":13,"number":32,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-32"}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-7"},{"id":"expanded-application-6-5","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:0","question":"Which tRNA enters the P site directly during normal initiation rather than the usual A-site entry during elongation?","options":["The initiator tRNA","Every incoming elongator tRNA","Only an uncharged exiting tRNA","A tRNA carrying a stop amino acid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The initiator tRNA is the classic P-site entry exception. During ordinary elongation, incoming aminoacyl-tRNAs enter A, the peptidyl-tRNA occupies P and uncharged tRNA leaves through E.","reasons":["The initiator tRNA is the classic P-site entry exception. During ordinary elongation, incoming aminoacyl-tRNAs enter A, the peptidyl-tRNA occupies P and uncharged tRNA leaves through E.","Ordinary elongator tRNAs enter A before translocation.","An uncharged exiting tRNA is associated with E rather than the special initiation position.","Stop codons are recognized by release factors rather than tRNAs carrying a stop amino acid."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":22}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-7"}],"MOL-6:guide-8":[{"id":"drill-MOL-6-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:3","question":"Which statement best explains protein elongation?","options":["The peptide grows from its amino terminus toward its carboxyl terminus","The A site accepts aminoacyl-tRNA, the P site holds peptidyl-tRNA and the E site releases tRNA","AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start","A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ribosome reads mRNA from 5′ to 3′. tRNA charging uses aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases; peptide-bond formation occurs in the ribosome. Charging, decoding and peptide transfer are separate steps.","reasons":["Correct. The ribosome reads mRNA from 5′ to 3′. tRNA charging uses aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases; peptide-bond formation occurs in the ribosome. Charging, decoding and peptide transfer are separate steps.","This explains Ribosomal sites. During elongation, the growing peptide is transferred to the amino acid on the A-site tRNA and translocation advances the complex. The initiator tRNA initially occupies the P site. Blocking translocation stalls elongation. For protein elongation, use the rule: The peptide grows from its amino terminus toward its carboxyl terminus.","This explains Genetic code. The RNA codon is read 5′ to 3′ and the anticodon pairs antiparallel. UAA, UAG and UGA are stop signals recognized by release factors, not ordinary tRNAs. Code degeneracy allows different codons to specify the same amino acid. For protein elongation, use the rule: The peptide grows from its amino terminus toward its carboxyl terminus.","This explains Mutation consequences. UAC to UAA converts a tyrosine codon to a stop. A silent change preserves the encoded amino acid. An insertion or deletion shifts the reading frame only when its length is not a multiple of three within the coding region. For protein elongation, use the rule: The peptide grows from its amino terminus toward its carboxyl terminus."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":46},{"source":"mol-past","page":47},{"source":"mol-past","page":48}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-8"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-44","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:0","question":"Immediately before an elongation peptide-transfer reaction, which site holds the tRNA carrying the growing peptide?","options":["E site","A separate T site","A site before the incoming aminoacyl-tRNA arrives","P site"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Peptide transfer moves the growing chain from P-site tRNA onto the A-site aminoacyl-tRNA. The peptidyl-tRNA is briefly in A until translocation.","reasons":["E is the exit site for deacylated tRNA.","A/P/E are the standard ribosomal tRNA-binding sites.","The A site accepts the incoming aminoacyl-tRNA at this stage.","Before transfer, peptidyl-tRNA occupies P while a new aminoacyl-tRNA is in A."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":10},{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":3}],"originalQuestion":"A molecular biologist is studying the process of translation using purified ribosomes and tRNAs. During the experiment, she observes that one tRNA is holding the growing peptide chain while another tRNA carrying a single amino acid enters the adjacent site to continue elongation. She wants to identify the ribosomal site where the peptidyl tRNA is located. Which of the following sites does it occupy? A) A site B) E site C) P site D) T site E) Release factor Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Specified the stage because peptidyl-tRNA is transiently in A after peptide transfer; the original wording was stage-ambiguous.","sourceExcerpt":"A molecular biologist is studying the process of translation using purified ribosomes and tRNAs. During the experiment, she observes that one tRNA is holding the growing peptide chain while another tRNA carrying a single amino acid enters the adjacent site to continue elongation. She wants to identify the ribosomal site where the peptidyl tRNA is located. Which of the following sites does it occupy? A) A site B) E site C) P site D) T site E) Release factor Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":3,"number":6,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-6"},{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":10,"number":44,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-44"}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-8"}],"MOL-6:guide-9":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-50","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:1","question":"How is a normal stop codon such as UAG recognized during translation termination?","options":["By a standard tRNA carrying a stop amino acid","By the 5′ methylguanosine cap entering the A site","By a DNA ligase at the ribosome","By a protein release factor rather than an ordinary aminoacyl-tRNA"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Release factors recognize stop codons and promote hydrolysis of the peptidyl-tRNA linkage.","reasons":["There is no standard stop amino acid or ordinary aminoacyl-tRNA for termination.","The cap is an initiation-related feature and does not act as the stop-codon decoder.","DNA ligase seals DNA nicks rather than recognizes mRNA stops.","Release factors recognize stop codons and promote hydrolysis of the peptidyl-tRNA linkage."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":54}],"originalQuestion":"They wanted the anticodon of the stop codon (UAG) A) CAU B) TAU C) CTA D) CAT Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Repaired the source request for an anticodon of UAG: normal termination uses release factors, not a standard stop tRNA.","sourceExcerpt":"They wanted the anticodon of the stop codon (UAG) A) CAU B) TAU C) CTA D) CAT Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-6:guide-9"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-6:guide-9-1","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:2","question":"Several ribosomes translate different positions on the same monocistronic mRNA at once. What does this arrangement represent?","options":["A requirement for a special tRNA carrying a stop amino acid","Conversion of the mRNA into a DNA chromosome","A polysome producing multiple copies of the encoded chain","Proof that the message must encode several different proteins"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Release factors recognize stop codons; there is no ordinary stop-amino-acid tRNA requirement.","Translation does not convert the mRNA into a DNA chromosome.","A polysome is multiple ribosomes on one message and can occur on a monocistronic mRNA.","Multiple ribosomes do not by themselves establish multiple coding regions or polycistronic organization."],"explanation":"A polysome is multiple ribosomes on one message and can occur on a monocistronic mRNA. Release factors recognize stop signals. Polysomes can occur on a monocistronic mRNA.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":26}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-6:guide-9"}],"MOL-6:guide-10":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-6:slides-2026-5-0","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-5","question":"Which statement best explains protein processing after translation?","options":["Hydroxylation, glycosylation, phosphorylation and proteolytic trimming modify different chemical features","The A site accepts aminoacyl-tRNA, the P site holds peptidyl-tRNA and the E site releases tRNA","AUG specifies methionine and usually establishes the translation start","A nonsense mutation creates a stop codon; a missense mutation changes an amino acid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Collagen hydroxylation depends on vitamin C; a precursor can be activated by cleavage. Modifying a protein product differs from changing the RNA codon that originally encoded it.","reasons":["Correct. Collagen hydroxylation depends on vitamin C; a precursor can be activated by cleavage. Modifying a protein product differs from changing the RNA codon that originally encoded it.","This explains Ribosomal sites. During elongation, the growing peptide is transferred to the amino acid on the A-site tRNA and translocation advances the complex. The initiator tRNA initially occupies the P site. Blocking translocation stalls elongation. For the concept asked here, Hydroxylation, glycosylation, phosphorylation and proteolytic trimming modify different chemical features.","This explains Genetic code. The RNA codon is read 5′ to 3′ and the anticodon pairs antiparallel. UAA, UAG and UGA are stop signals recognized by release factors, not ordinary tRNAs. Code degeneracy allows different codons to specify the same amino acid. For the concept asked here, Hydroxylation, glycosylation, phosphorylation and proteolytic trimming modify different chemical features.","This explains Mutation consequences. UAC to UAA converts a tyrosine codon to a stop. A silent change preserves the encoded amino acid. An insertion or deletion shifts the reading frame only when its length is not a multiple of three within the coding region. For the concept asked here, Hydroxylation, glycosylation, phosphorylation and proteolytic trimming modify different chemical features."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":33},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":157}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-10"},{"id":"expanded-application-6-7","lectureIds":["MOL-6"],"concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-5","question":"Which residue modification is especially important in the collagen example?","options":["Hydroxylation of selected proline and lysine residues","Conversion of every glycine into tryptophan","Attachment of a poly-A tail to the peptide","Replacement of all peptide bonds with disulfides"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Collagen maturation includes hydroxylation of selected proline and lysine residues. This is a covalent protein modification distinct from transcription, RNA processing and ordinary peptide-bond formation.","reasons":["Collagen maturation includes hydroxylation of selected proline and lysine residues. This is a covalent protein modification distinct from transcription, RNA processing and ordinary peptide-bond formation.","The collagen mechanism does not replace every glycine with an aromatic residue.","Poly-A tails are RNA features, not peptide-tail modifications.","Disulfides connect cysteine side chains and do not replace the peptide backbone."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":33}],"topicId":"MOL-6:guide-10"}],"MOL-7:guide-1":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-7:slides-2026-0-0","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-0","question":"Which statement best explains differential expression?","options":["Cells with essentially the same genome can express different sets of genes","Histone acetylation generally favors accessible chromatin and transcription","Enhancers are cis-acting DNA elements; transcription factors are trans-acting products","microRNAs guide silencing machinery to target RNAs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cell specialization depends on regulation rather than every cell having a wholly different list of genes. The lecture’s mixed-cell example uses different expression markers to distinguish cellular states.","reasons":["Correct. Cell specialization depends on regulation rather than every cell having a wholly different list of genes. The lecture’s mixed-cell example uses different expression markers to distinguish cellular states.","This explains Chromatin accessibility. Acetylation changes lysine charge interactions and recruits regulatory proteins. HDACs remove acetyl groups. DNA methylation at promoter CpG sites is commonly associated with repression, but regulatory effects depend on genomic context. For the concept asked here, Cells with essentially the same genome can express different sets of genes.","This explains Cis versus trans regulation. A cis element acts as a DNA sequence on the same molecule. A trans-acting protein can diffuse and bind many DNA molecules. An enhancer is not itself a diffusible protein. For the concept asked here, Cells with essentially the same genome can express different sets of genes.","This explains microRNA regulation. Drosha and Dicer participate in canonical microRNA processing. A mature microRNA guides RISC, often reducing translation or RNA stability. Animal microRNAs usually do not require complete target complementarity. For the concept asked here, Cells with essentially the same genome can express different sets of genes."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":4},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":168}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-1"},{"id":"expanded-application-7-1","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-0","question":"Two differentiated cell types share essentially the same genome but make different protein sets. Which explanation fits?","options":["They regulate expression of different gene subsets","One type must lack every gene it does not express","The genetic code is completely different in the two cell types","Proteins must be translated directly from DNA without RNA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Differential gene expression allows cells with similar genomic information to specialize. Regulation can act at chromatin, transcription, RNA turnover, translation and protein turnover.","reasons":["Differential gene expression allows cells with similar genomic information to specialize. Regulation can act at chromatin, transcription, RNA turnover, translation and protein turnover.","Genes can remain present while silent; wholesale deletion is not required for ordinary differentiation.","Cells generally use the same code while choosing different genes and expression levels.","Cytosolic protein synthesis uses mRNA rather than translating DNA directly."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":4}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-1"}],"MOL-7:guide-2":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-40","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:0","question":"Which description best fits constitutive heterochromatin compared with typical euchromatin?","options":["Always the most actively transcribed chromatin","A structure present identically in bacteria as canonical human nucleosomes","The site where ribosomes translate cytosolic mRNA","More condensed and usually less transcriptionally accessible"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Heterochromatin is generally condensed; it may be enriched near the nuclear periphery, centromeres or telomeres depending on type.","reasons":["Euchromatin is generally more accessible.","Typical bacterial chromosome organization differs from eukaryotic nucleosomal chromatin.","Chromatin concerns nuclear DNA packaging, not cytosolic translation.","Compact chromatin limits access of transcription machinery."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":16}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following is true for heterochromatin? A. Found in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes B. Found at the peripheral side of the nucleus C. Makes up 80% of human genome D. Are easily transcribed E. Need a lot of ATP to be transcribed Answer: B","adaptationNote":"Replaced location-only and questionable numerical alternatives with the central chromatin distinction.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following is true for heterochromatin? A. Found in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes B. Found at the peripheral side of the nucleus C. Makes up 80% of human genome D. Are easily transcribed E. Need a lot of ATP to be transcribed Answer: B","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":16,"number":40,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-40"}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-2"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-7:guide-2-1","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:0","question":"An inactive X chromosome is an example of which chromatin category?","options":["A universal state of all actively transcribed genes","Facultative heterochromatin","Constitutively open euchromatin in every cell","The bacterial ribosome"],"answer":1,"reasons":["The topic describes regulated repression, not a universal state of all active genes.","An inactive X illustrates heterochromatin established in a regulated cellular or developmental context.","The inactive X is a compact, repressed example rather than a universally open region.","The example concerns nuclear chromatin, not bacterial translation machinery."],"explanation":"An inactive X illustrates heterochromatin established in a regulated cellular or developmental context. Euchromatin generally permits greater access. Facultative heterochromatin depends on developmental or cellular context.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":6}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-7:guide-2"}],"MOL-7:guide-3":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-50","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-2","question":"Which statement best defines an epigenetic change?","options":["The destruction of every chromosome before division","Only the replacement of one amino acid in a protein","A change in gene-regulatory state that can persist without a change in DNA base sequence","Any substitution in the DNA coding sequence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Epigenetics concerns persistent regulatory states without an underlying sequence change. Not every transient change in expression is necessarily heritable.","reasons":["Epigenetic regulation does not require chromosome destruction.","That describes a protein-sequence consequence, not the definition of epigenetics.","Chromatin marks or related mechanisms can maintain expression states independently of sequence changes.","A base substitution is a genetic sequence change."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":11}],"originalQuestion":"There was a question about the definition epigenetics","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed from the source’s recalled topic-only epigenetics question and supplied full options.","sourceExcerpt":"There was a question about the definition epigenetics","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":11,"number":50,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-50"}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-3"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-51","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:0","question":"Which modification is often associated with transcriptional repression at a mammalian promoter?","options":["DNA cytosine methylation at relevant CpG sites","Addition of a poly-A tail to a histone lysine","Replacement of nucleosomes with ribosomes","Histone acetylation as a universal repression signal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Promoter CpG methylation can recruit repression-associated machinery and reduce transcription.","reasons":["Promoter CpG methylation can recruit repression-associated machinery and reduce transcription.","Poly-A is an RNA feature, not a histone-lysine modification.","Ribosomes are not chromatin modifications.","Histone acetylation generally supports accessible chromatin."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":56}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following modifications is most commonly associated with repression of a gene? A) Histone acetylation of a lysine residue B) Nucleotide demethylation of a guanine C) Nucleotide methylation of cytosine D) Histone ubiquitination of arginine E) Histone deubiquitination of lysine Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Qualified the locus-dependent effect of DNA methylation.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following modifications is most commonly associated with repression of a gene? A) Histone acetylation of a lysine residue B) Nucleotide demethylation of a guanine C) Nucleotide methylation of cytosine D) Histone ubiquitination of arginine E) Histone deubiquitination of lysine Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-7:guide-3"}],"MOL-7:guide-4":[{"id":"mol-slides-7-application-1","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-1","question":"Which statement about histone methylation is most accurate?","options":["It always represses every gene","Its association can differ with the residue and methylation mark","It changes every cytosine to thymine","It is identical to histone acetylation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The lecture contrasts marks associated with activation, such as H3K4 methylation, with repressive associations such as H3K9 methylation.","reasons":["This ignores context and the mark’s location.","Residue and mark context are essential.","Histone methylation is a protein modification, not this DNA base conversion.","Acetyl and methyl groups are different modifications with different regulatory contexts."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":11}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-4"},{"id":"expanded-application-7-3","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-1","question":"Why is “all histone methylation represses transcription” an unreliable rule?","options":["The effect depends on the modified residue and the methylation context","Methylation never occurs on histones","Histones contain no amino acids","Methylation always converts a chromosome into mRNA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Different histone methyl marks are associated with different chromatin states. Position and context determine whether a mark is linked to activation or repression.","reasons":["Different histone methyl marks are associated with different chromatin states. Position and context determine whether a mark is linked to activation or repression.","Histone methylation is a real regulatory modification.","Histones are proteins and therefore are made from amino acids.","Methylation changes a chemical mark rather than converting DNA into a transcript."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":11}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-4"}],"MOL-7:guide-5":[{"id":"expanded-application-7-4","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:1","question":"Which pair correctly separates a cis element from a trans-acting product?","options":["Enhancer DNA; transcriptional activator protein","Activator protein; enhancer DNA","RNA polymerase protein; promoter DNA","Histone protein; silencer DNA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Enhancers are regulatory DNA sequences linked to their target DNA molecule. Activators are diffusible products that bind appropriate elements and affect transcription, so they are trans-acting.","reasons":["Enhancers are regulatory DNA sequences linked to their target DNA molecule. Activators are diffusible products that bind appropriate elements and affect transcription, so they are trans-acting.","This reverses the categories for the stated pair.","RNA polymerase is a diffusible protein, whereas the promoter is cis DNA.","The protein belongs to the trans-product side and the silencer is cis DNA."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":13}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-5"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-42","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:1","question":"Which is a cis-acting DNA element that can regulate a gene from a nearby or distant position?","options":["Enhancer","MicroRNA","General transcription-factor protein","Transcription-factor protein"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cis elements are DNA sequences, whereas trans factors are diffusible products. Enhancers can act over substantial genomic distances.","reasons":["An enhancer is a DNA sequence on the same DNA molecule as its regulated target.","A microRNA is an RNA product acting in trans, not a cis DNA element.","General factors are diffusible proteins, not regulatory DNA sequences.","The protein is a trans-acting product that can bind regulatory DNA."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":9}],"originalQuestion":"Which regulatory DNA element acts in cis and can function close to or far away from the gene it regulates? A) Transcription factors (trans-acting proteins) B) Enhancers C) miRNA (trans-acting) D) General transcription machinery (trans-acting) Answer: B (Lowkey not sure about this question)","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Which regulatory DNA element acts in cis and can function close to or far away from the gene it regulates? A) Transcription factors (trans-acting proteins) B) Enhancers C) miRNA (trans-acting) D) General transcription machinery (trans-acting) Answer: B (Lowkey not sure about this question)","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":9,"number":42,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-42"}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-5"}],"MOL-7:guide-6":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-7:guide-6-1","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-2","question":"How can an enhancer located far from a promoter along the DNA sequence influence that promoter?","options":["Linear distance makes physical interaction permanently impossible","The promoter must lose its DNA backbone before any regulatory contact","DNA looping can bring their associated complexes close in three-dimensional space","The enhancer must first be translated into an RNA-polymerase protein"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Sequence distance and three-dimensional proximity are different quantities.","Regulatory looping does not require destroying the promoter backbone.","Looping permits spatial proximity even when the regulatory sites are distant along the linear sequence.","An enhancer is a DNA element; its regulatory contact does not require translation into polymerase."],"explanation":"Looping permits spatial proximity even when the regulatory sites are distant along the linear sequence. Regulatory proteins can have separable functional domains. Distance in the linear DNA sequence differs from three-dimensional proximity.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":15}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-7:guide-6"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-7:guide-6-2","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-2","question":"An activator retains its DNA-binding part but loses a region needed to recruit transcription machinery. Why can its regulatory activity fall?","options":["All activators function only as membrane transporters","DNA recognition and productive protein interactions can depend on distinct functional regions","DNA binding alone guarantees full activation regardless of every other region","Every short DNA motif is itself an entire folded protein domain"],"answer":1,"reasons":["The described activator is a transcriptional regulatory protein, not a membrane-transport category.","The topic describes separable functions for DNA binding, protein interactions and signal responses.","Binding the DNA does not guarantee the contacts needed to promote transcription.","A sequence motif and a complete folded protein domain are not equivalent concepts."],"explanation":"The topic describes separable functions for DNA binding, protein interactions and signal responses. Regulatory proteins can have separable functional domains. Distance in the linear DNA sequence differs from three-dimensional proximity.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":15}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-7:guide-6"}],"MOL-7:guide-7":[{"id":"expanded-application-7-5","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-3","question":"A liver cell raises PEPCK transcription after glucagon signaling through cAMP. Which mediator belongs in the pathway?","options":["PKA-dependent activation of CREB","Direct insertion of glucagon into the DNA backbone","M6P receptor binding to the promoter","Ligase joining glucagon to a histone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The lecture pathway connects glucagon receptor signaling to cAMP, PKA and CREB, which regulates transcription through response elements. The hormone is not itself a DNA sequence or a covalent promoter component.","reasons":["The lecture pathway connects glucagon receptor signaling to cAMP, PKA and CREB, which regulates transcription through response elements. The hormone is not itself a DNA sequence or a covalent promoter component.","A hormone does not insert into the DNA backbone to become a regulatory base.","M6P receptors sort protein cargo rather than recognize a gluconeogenic promoter.","DNA ligase does not attach glucagon to histones as the signaling mechanism."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":20}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-7"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-36","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-3","question":"Which sequence is a standard signaling route from glucagon to increased transcription of selected hepatic genes?","options":["Receptor signaling → cAMP → PKA → CREB","Glucagon universally acts only through protein kinase B","Glucagon is incorporated into DNA as a base","Glucagon enters the nucleus and directly binds GRE"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Glucagon receptor signaling can activate cAMP-dependent protein kinase and CREB; cortisol–GRE signaling is a different mechanism.","reasons":["This route can promote transcription through cAMP-response elements.","The canonical hepatic glucagon pathway involves cAMP and PKA.","A peptide hormone is not a DNA nucleotide.","GREs are bound by glucocorticoid receptor complexes, not directly by glucagon."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":14}],"originalQuestion":"Glucagon is released in a low blood glucose level state. Which of the following is the correct mechanism of how glucagon works? A. Binds to intracellular receptor B. Increase activity of protein kinase B, which activates CREB C. Increase activity of protein kinase A D. Bind to GRE via DNA binding domain Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Glucagon is released in a low blood glucose level state. Which of the following is the correct mechanism of how glucagon works? A. Binds to intracellular receptor B. Increase activity of protein kinase B, which activates CREB C. Increase activity of protein kinase A D. Bind to GRE via DNA binding domain Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":14,"number":36,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-36"}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-7"}],"MOL-7:guide-8":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-39","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:3","question":"How can a cortisol-activated glucocorticoid receptor increase PEPCK gene transcription?","options":["The receptor complex binds a glucocorticoid response element (GRE)","Cortisol must activate the glucagon cAMP–PKA pathway in every case","Cortisol itself base-pairs with CRE sequences","Cortisol is incorporated as a nucleotide in PEPCK mRNA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A hormone-bound glucocorticoid receptor regulates selected genes through GRE-containing regulatory regions.","reasons":["The receptor functions as a ligand-regulated transcription factor at appropriate DNA response elements.","Glucocorticoid receptor signaling is distinct from the glucagon pathway.","Cortisol is not a nucleic acid and does not base-pair with DNA.","Steroid hormones are not RNA nucleotide building blocks."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":9}],"originalQuestion":"How does cortisol increase expression of gluconeogenic genes such as PEPCK? A) By binding to CRE B) By binding to GRE C) By activating adenylate cyclase D) By phosphorylating CREB Answer: B","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"How does cortisol increase expression of gluconeogenic genes such as PEPCK? A) By binding to CRE B) By binding to GRE C) By activating adenylate cyclase D) By phosphorylating CREB Answer: B","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":9,"number":39,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-39"}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-8"},{"id":"drill-MOL-7-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:3","question":"Which statement best explains steroid response elements?","options":["A glucocorticoid receptor complex can regulate genes through glucocorticoid response elements","Histone acetylation generally favors accessible chromatin and transcription","Enhancers are cis-acting DNA elements; transcription factors are trans-acting products","microRNAs guide silencing machinery to target RNAs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ligand-bound receptor acts as a transcriptional regulator. GRE and CRE are different response elements; cAMP signaling through CREB should not be substituted for the direct glucocorticoid-receptor mechanism.","reasons":["Correct. The ligand-bound receptor acts as a transcriptional regulator. GRE and CRE are different response elements; cAMP signaling through CREB should not be substituted for the direct glucocorticoid-receptor mechanism.","This explains Chromatin accessibility. Acetylation changes lysine charge interactions and recruits regulatory proteins. HDACs remove acetyl groups. DNA methylation at promoter CpG sites is commonly associated with repression, but regulatory effects depend on genomic context. For steroid response elements, use the rule: A glucocorticoid receptor complex can regulate genes through glucocorticoid response elements.","This explains Cis versus trans regulation. A cis element acts as a DNA sequence on the same molecule. A trans-acting protein can diffuse and bind many DNA molecules. An enhancer is not itself a diffusible protein. For steroid response elements, use the rule: A glucocorticoid receptor complex can regulate genes through glucocorticoid response elements.","This explains microRNA regulation. Drosha and Dicer participate in canonical microRNA processing. A mature microRNA guides RISC, often reducing translation or RNA stability. Animal microRNAs usually do not require complete target complementarity. For steroid response elements, use the rule: A glucocorticoid receptor complex can regulate genes through glucocorticoid response elements."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":55},{"source":"mol-past","page":56},{"source":"mol-past","page":57}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-8"}],"MOL-7:guide-9":[{"id":"drill-MOL-7-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:2","question":"Which statement best explains microrna regulation?","options":["microRNAs guide silencing machinery to target RNAs","Histone acetylation generally favors accessible chromatin and transcription","Enhancers are cis-acting DNA elements; transcription factors are trans-acting products","A glucocorticoid receptor complex can regulate genes through glucocorticoid response elements"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Drosha and Dicer participate in canonical microRNA processing. A mature microRNA guides RISC, often reducing translation or RNA stability. Animal microRNAs usually do not require complete target complementarity.","reasons":["Correct. Drosha and Dicer participate in canonical microRNA processing. A mature microRNA guides RISC, often reducing translation or RNA stability. Animal microRNAs usually do not require complete target complementarity.","This explains Chromatin accessibility. Acetylation changes lysine charge interactions and recruits regulatory proteins. HDACs remove acetyl groups. DNA methylation at promoter CpG sites is commonly associated with repression, but regulatory effects depend on genomic context. For microrna regulation, use the rule: microRNAs guide silencing machinery to target RNAs.","This explains Cis versus trans regulation. A cis element acts as a DNA sequence on the same molecule. A trans-acting protein can diffuse and bind many DNA molecules. An enhancer is not itself a diffusible protein. For microrna regulation, use the rule: microRNAs guide silencing machinery to target RNAs.","This explains Steroid response elements. The ligand-bound receptor acts as a transcriptional regulator. GRE and CRE are different response elements; cAMP signaling through CREB should not be substituted for the direct glucocorticoid-receptor mechanism. For microrna regulation, use the rule: microRNAs guide silencing machinery to target RNAs."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":55},{"source":"mol-past","page":56},{"source":"mol-past","page":57}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-9"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-54","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:2","question":"Which feature is typical of siRNA-guided cleavage of a target mRNA?","options":["Extensive complementarity between guide and target","Obligatory production by cytoplasmic Drosha","Delivery of amino acids to the ribosome","A requirement that the target contain no complementary bases"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Near-perfect pairing can direct Argonaute-mediated target cleavage.","reasons":["Near-perfect pairing can direct Argonaute-mediated target cleavage.","Canonical Drosha action is associated with nuclear miRNA processing, not this universal siRNA requirement.","That is tRNA’s role rather than siRNA’s.","Guide recognition depends on complementarity."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":57}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following is correct about small interfering RNA (siRNA)? A) siRNA is derived from microRNA (miRNA) B) siRNA targets multiple mRNAs C) siRNA requires complete complementarity to mRNA D) siRNA is processed by the Drosha enzyme in the cytoplasm E) siRNA binds Argonaute protein in the nucleus Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Qualified complete complementarity to extensive/near-perfect pairing for the canonical cleavage mechanism.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following is correct about small interfering RNA (siRNA)? A) siRNA is derived from microRNA (miRNA) B) siRNA targets multiple mRNAs C) siRNA requires complete complementarity to mRNA D) siRNA is processed by the Drosha enzyme in the cytoplasm E) siRNA binds Argonaute protein in the nucleus Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-7:guide-9"}],"MOL-7:guide-10":[{"id":"expanded-application-7-6","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-4","question":"Ferritin mRNA is present, but its translation is low when cellular iron is low. What explains this?","options":["An iron-regulatory protein binds a 5′-UTR IRE and blocks efficient initiation","The ferritin coding sequence must have been deleted","All ferritin mRNA is converted into DNA","Iron directly supplies the peptide bonds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Low iron permits IRP binding to the ferritin 5′ IRE and restrains translation. This regulation allows protein abundance to change without requiring a matching change in mRNA amount.","reasons":["Low iron permits IRP binding to the ferritin 5′ IRE and restrains translation. This regulation allows protein abundance to change without requiring a matching change in mRNA amount.","The stated intact mRNA and reversible iron dependence support translational control rather than mandatory gene deletion.","Ferritin regulation does not require conversion of mRNA back into DNA.","Ribosomal chemistry makes peptide bonds; iron is not a structural substitute for them."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":25}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-10"},{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-7:slides-2026-5-0","lectureIds":["MOL-7"],"concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-5","question":"Which statement best explains expression measurements?","options":["Western blot measures a protein signal, whereas Northern blot measures an RNA signal","Histone acetylation generally favors accessible chromatin and transcription","Enhancers are cis-acting DNA elements; transcription factors are trans-acting products","microRNAs guide silencing machinery to target RNAs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"qPCR-based RNA measurement requires reverse transcription of RNA to a DNA template. RNA-seq assesses transcript populations and in-situ hybridization retains spatial information. Choose the technique that answers the stated molecular and location question.","reasons":["Correct. qPCR-based RNA measurement requires reverse transcription of RNA to a DNA template. RNA-seq assesses transcript populations and in-situ hybridization retains spatial information. Choose the technique that answers the stated molecular and location question.","This explains Chromatin accessibility. Acetylation changes lysine charge interactions and recruits regulatory proteins. HDACs remove acetyl groups. DNA methylation at promoter CpG sites is commonly associated with repression, but regulatory effects depend on genomic context. For the concept asked here, Western blot measures a protein signal, whereas Northern blot measures an RNA signal.","This explains Cis versus trans regulation. A cis element acts as a DNA sequence on the same molecule. A trans-acting protein can diffuse and bind many DNA molecules. An enhancer is not itself a diffusible protein. For the concept asked here, Western blot measures a protein signal, whereas Northern blot measures an RNA signal.","This explains microRNA regulation. Drosha and Dicer participate in canonical microRNA processing. A mature microRNA guides RISC, often reducing translation or RNA stability. Animal microRNAs usually do not require complete target complementarity. For the concept asked here, Western blot measures a protein signal, whereas Northern blot measures an RNA signal."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":26},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":195}],"topicId":"MOL-7:guide-10"}],"MOL-8:guide-1":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-8:slides-2026-0-0","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-0","question":"Which statement best explains common amino-acid structure?","options":["The alpha carbon connects an amino group, a carboxyl group, hydrogen and the variable R group","Aspartate and glutamate are usually negatively charged near physiological pH","Serine, threonine and tyrosine are common phosphorylation sites","Nonpolar side chains often pack inside soluble globular proteins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The R group distinguishes amino-acid chemistry. At physiological pH, the amino and carboxyl groups are commonly ionized. A structural drawing without charges should not be treated as the only state in solution.","reasons":["Correct. The R group distinguishes amino-acid chemistry. At physiological pH, the amino and carboxyl groups are commonly ionized. A structural drawing without charges should not be treated as the only state in solution.","This explains Amino-acid side chains. Lysine and arginine are usually positive, while histidine can change protonation near relevant physiological ranges. A polar uncharged residue such as asparagine can hydrogen bond without carrying the same ionic charge as aspartate. For the concept asked here, The alpha carbon connects an amino group, a carboxyl group, hydrogen and the variable R group.","This explains Common protein modifications. Their hydroxyl groups can carry phosphate. N-linked glycosylation attaches to asparagine, while many O-linked glycans attach to serine or threonine. Do not confuse glycosylation with phosphorylation. For the concept asked here, The alpha carbon connects an amino group, a carboxyl group, hydrogen and the variable R group.","This explains Hydrophobic packing. Hydrophobic residues minimize exposure to water. Charged residues are often surface-exposed. This is a general folding principle, not an absolute rule for every protein, especially membrane proteins. For the concept asked here, The alpha carbon connects an amino group, a carboxyl group, hydrogen and the variable R group."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":3},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":207}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-1"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-8:guide-1-1","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-0","question":"Which feature differs among the general alpha-amino-acid structures and determines many distinctive properties?","options":["A variable number of complete ribosomes","The replacement of every amino group with a DNA base","The R side chain","The identity of a mandatory second alpha carbon"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Ribosomes are not substituents that distinguish individual amino acids.","Amino-acid diversity does not arise by replacing amino groups with DNA bases.","The R group varies while the general alpha-amino-acid framework is shared.","The shared framework has the alpha carbon; variation is primarily in the attached side chain."],"explanation":"The R group varies while the general alpha-amino-acid framework is shared. The R group varies; the general backbone is shared. Ionization state depends on pH.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":3}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-8:guide-1"}],"MOL-8:guide-2":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-8:slides-2026-1-0","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-1","question":"Which statement best explains zwitterions?","options":["A zwitterion carries positive and negative groups even when its net charge is zero","Aspartate and glutamate are usually negatively charged near physiological pH","Serine, threonine and tyrosine are common phosphorylation sites","Nonpolar side chains often pack inside soluble globular proteins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Zero net charge does not mean that every functional group is uncharged. Net charge is the algebraic sum of all ionizable groups in their current protonation states.","reasons":["Correct. Zero net charge does not mean that every functional group is uncharged. Net charge is the algebraic sum of all ionizable groups in their current protonation states.","This explains Amino-acid side chains. Lysine and arginine are usually positive, while histidine can change protonation near relevant physiological ranges. A polar uncharged residue such as asparagine can hydrogen bond without carrying the same ionic charge as aspartate. For the concept asked here, A zwitterion carries positive and negative groups even when its net charge is zero.","This explains Common protein modifications. Their hydroxyl groups can carry phosphate. N-linked glycosylation attaches to asparagine, while many O-linked glycans attach to serine or threonine. Do not confuse glycosylation with phosphorylation. For the concept asked here, A zwitterion carries positive and negative groups even when its net charge is zero.","This explains Hydrophobic packing. Hydrophobic residues minimize exposure to water. Charged residues are often surface-exposed. This is a general folding principle, not an absolute rule for every protein, especially membrane proteins. For the concept asked here, A zwitterion carries positive and negative groups even when its net charge is zero."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":4},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":208}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-2"},{"id":"mol-slides-8-application-2","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-1","question":"A molecule has both NH3+ and COO− groups and a net charge of zero. Which description is appropriate?","options":["It has no charged functional groups","It is a zwitterion","It must have lost its amino group","It contains only nonpolar bonds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Opposite charges can sum to zero while remaining present on separate groups.","reasons":["Zero net charge does not eliminate the individual charges.","This is the definition of a zwitterionic state.","The positively charged amino group is explicitly present.","The stated charged groups are not exclusively nonpolar."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":4}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-2"}],"MOL-8:guide-3":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-8:slides-2026-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-2","question":"Which statement best explains chirality?","options":["Glycine is achiral because its alpha carbon has two hydrogens","Aspartate and glutamate are usually negatively charged near physiological pH","Serine, threonine and tyrosine are common phosphorylation sites","Nonpolar side chains often pack inside soluble globular proteins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Most protein amino acids have an asymmetric alpha carbon and use the L configuration. D/L labels describe configuration, not a general rule that all L amino acids rotate polarized light in the same direction.","reasons":["Correct. Most protein amino acids have an asymmetric alpha carbon and use the L configuration. D/L labels describe configuration, not a general rule that all L amino acids rotate polarized light in the same direction.","This explains Amino-acid side chains. Lysine and arginine are usually positive, while histidine can change protonation near relevant physiological ranges. A polar uncharged residue such as asparagine can hydrogen bond without carrying the same ionic charge as aspartate. For the concept asked here, Glycine is achiral because its alpha carbon has two hydrogens.","This explains Common protein modifications. Their hydroxyl groups can carry phosphate. N-linked glycosylation attaches to asparagine, while many O-linked glycans attach to serine or threonine. Do not confuse glycosylation with phosphorylation. For the concept asked here, Glycine is achiral because its alpha carbon has two hydrogens.","This explains Hydrophobic packing. Hydrophobic residues minimize exposure to water. Charged residues are often surface-exposed. This is a general folding principle, not an absolute rule for every protein, especially membrane proteins. For the concept asked here, Glycine is achiral because its alpha carbon has two hydrogens."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":5},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":209}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-3"},{"id":"mol-slides-8-application-1","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-2","question":"Why is glycine achiral at its alpha carbon?","options":["It has two hydrogens attached there","Its side chain is an aromatic ring","It contains a disulfide bridge","It has no amino group"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An asymmetric center needs four different substituents. Glycine has hydrogen as its R group, giving two identical hydrogens.","reasons":["Two identical substituents prevent the usual alpha-carbon chirality.","Glycine has no aromatic side chain.","A disulfide bond is not the reason for glycine’s lack of chirality.","Glycine still has an amino group."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":5}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-3"}],"MOL-8:guide-4":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-70","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-4","question":"Which amino acids are branched-chain amino acids?","options":["Aspartate and glutamate","Valine, leucine and isoleucine","Cysteine and methionine","Tryptophan, tyrosine and phenylalanine"],"answer":1,"explanation":"These three have branched aliphatic side chains.","reasons":["Aspartate and glutamate are acidic amino acids with carboxylate side chains, not the branched-chain set.","These three have branched aliphatic side chains.","These are sulfur-containing amino acids.","Tryptophan, tyrosine and phenylalanine have aromatic rings rather than the branched aliphatic chains being tested."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":73}],"originalQuestion":"There was some long question about branched chain amino acid A) Valine-Leusine-Isoleucine B) Tryptophan-tyrosine- phenylalanine Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed the vague recalled branched-chain question.","sourceExcerpt":"There was some long question about branched chain amino acid A) Valine-Leusine-Isoleucine B) Tryptophan-tyrosine- phenylalanine Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-8:guide-4"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-64","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:2","question":"Which tripeptide consists entirely of nonpolar side chains?","options":["Lys–Asp–His","Ala–Leu–Val","Asn–Gln–Glu","Ser–Thr–Ile"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Alanine, leucine and valine all have nonpolar hydrocarbon side chains.","reasons":["These include charged or ionizable polar side chains.","Alanine, leucine and valine all have nonpolar hydrocarbon side chains.","Asparagine and glutamine are polar, while glutamate is acidic.","Serine and threonine contain hydroxyl groups."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":66}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following is composed exclusively from hydrophobic amino acids? A) Serine-Threonine-Isoleucine B) Alanine- Leucine- Valine C) Lysine- Aspartate - Histidine Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Added a fourth option.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following is composed exclusively from hydrophobic amino acids? A) Serine-Threonine-Isoleucine B) Alanine- Leucine- Valine C) Lysine- Aspartate - Histidine Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-8:guide-4"}],"MOL-8:guide-5":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-8:guide-5-1","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-5","question":"Which pair contains amide side chains rather than the corresponding acidic carboxylate side chains?","options":["Aspartate and glutamate","Lysine and arginine","Alanine and valine","Asparagine and glutamine"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Asp and Glu contain carboxylate side chains in their usual physiological state.","Lys and Arg are basic side-chain examples, not the amide pair.","Ala and Val are nonpolar examples rather than the amide-containing pair.","Asn and Gln contain amides and are distinguished here from acidic Asp and Glu."],"explanation":"Asn and Gln contain amides and are distinguished here from acidic Asp and Glu. Asn/Gln have amide side chains. Asp/Glu have carboxylate side chains.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":10}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-8:guide-5"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-8:guide-5-2","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-5","question":"Which statement correctly interprets the term “polar uncharged side chain”?","options":["The side chain cannot form a hydrogen bond","The residue must be chemically identical to glutamate","The side chain is usually uncharged in the stated range even though a free amino acid can have charged backbone groups","The entire free amino acid can never carry any charge at any pH"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Many polar uncharged side chains support hydrogen bonding.","Amide-containing Asn/Gln must be distinguished from acidic Glu/Asp rather than equated with them.","The classification refers to the side chain’s usual state, not the absence of charged amino/carboxyl groups on a free amino acid.","Ionization depends on pH, and backbone groups commonly carry charges."],"explanation":"The classification refers to the side chain’s usual state, not the absence of charged amino/carboxyl groups on a free amino acid. Asn/Gln have amide side chains. Asp/Glu have carboxylate side chains.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":10}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-8:guide-5"}],"MOL-8:guide-6":[{"id":"expanded-application-8-2","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:0","question":"Which pair ordinarily contributes negatively charged side chains near pH 7.4?","options":["Aspartate and glutamate","Lysine and arginine","Asparagine and glutamine","Valine and leucine"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Aspartate and glutamate have side-chain carboxyl groups that are largely deprotonated near physiological pH. Their amide counterparts Asn and Gln do not carry the same ordinary negative side-chain charge.","reasons":["Aspartate and glutamate have side-chain carboxyl groups that are largely deprotonated near physiological pH. Their amide counterparts Asn and Gln do not carry the same ordinary negative side-chain charge.","Lysine and arginine usually carry positive side-chain charge near this pH.","Asparagine and glutamine have neutral amide side chains rather than carboxylates.","Valine and leucine have nonpolar hydrocarbon side chains, not ionizable carboxylates."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":11}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-6"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-16-1","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:0","question":"Which listed amino-acid side chain is ordinarily positively charged near physiological pH?","options":["Glutamine","Leucine","Asparagine","Arginine"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Arginine is a basic amino acid. Distinguish an ionizable side chain from the charged alpha-amino and alpha-carboxyl groups shared by free amino acids.","reasons":["Its amide side chain is ordinarily uncharged near physiological pH.","Leucine has a nonpolar hydrocarbon side chain with no physiological acid/base group.","Its amide side chain is polar but ordinarily uncharged.","Its guanidinium group has a high pKa and is predominantly protonated near pH 7.4."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":2}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following can be ionized under physiological pH? A. Arginine B. Leucine C. Asparagine D. Glutamine E. Threonine Ans: A","adaptationNote":"Specified side-chain charge because all free amino acids have ionizable terminal groups.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following can be ionized under physiological pH? A. Arginine B. Leucine C. Asparagine D. Glutamine E. Threonine Ans: A","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":2,"number":1,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-1"}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-6"}],"MOL-8:guide-7":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-8:slides-2026-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-3","question":"Which statement best explains special side chains?","options":["Glycine is small and flexible, while proline constrains the backbone","Aspartate and glutamate are usually negatively charged near physiological pH","Serine, threonine and tyrosine are common phosphorylation sites","Nonpolar side chains often pack inside soluble globular proteins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"These properties help explain their structural roles. Proline’s ring restricts backbone geometry, so it cannot be treated as interchangeable with every other nonpolar residue in an alpha helix.","reasons":["Correct. These properties help explain their structural roles. Proline’s ring restricts backbone geometry, so it cannot be treated as interchangeable with every other nonpolar residue in an alpha helix.","This explains Amino-acid side chains. Lysine and arginine are usually positive, while histidine can change protonation near relevant physiological ranges. A polar uncharged residue such as asparagine can hydrogen bond without carrying the same ionic charge as aspartate. For the concept asked here, Glycine is small and flexible, while proline constrains the backbone.","This explains Common protein modifications. Their hydroxyl groups can carry phosphate. N-linked glycosylation attaches to asparagine, while many O-linked glycans attach to serine or threonine. Do not confuse glycosylation with phosphorylation. For the concept asked here, Glycine is small and flexible, while proline constrains the backbone.","This explains Hydrophobic packing. Hydrophobic residues minimize exposure to water. Charged residues are often surface-exposed. This is a general folding principle, not an absolute rule for every protein, especially membrane proteins. For the concept asked here, Glycine is small and flexible, while proline constrains the backbone."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":12},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":214}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-7"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-68","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-3","question":"What makes proline chemically unusual among the standard protein amino acids?","options":["Its side chain closes a ring to the backbone nitrogen","It has a thiol that forms disulfide bonds","It has no alpha carbon","Its side chain forms a ring with the carboxyl carbon instead"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This constrains backbone geometry and leaves an internal proline peptide nitrogen without the usual N–H donor.","reasons":["This constrains backbone geometry and leaves an internal proline peptide nitrogen without the usual N–H donor.","That is cysteine chemistry, not proline.","Proline has an alpha carbon like the other standard amino acids; its ring connection to nitrogen is the unusual feature.","The characteristic ring closes to the amino nitrogen."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":71}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following options is the best fit for the amino acid proline? A) Hydrophilic and polar B) Nitrogen of amide in ring C) Carbon of carboxyl in ring Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Clarified the source’s imprecise nitrogen of amide in ring wording.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following options is the best fit for the amino acid proline? A) Hydrophilic and polar B) Nitrogen of amide in ring C) Carbon of carboxyl in ring Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-8:guide-7"}],"MOL-8:guide-8":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-midterm-sulfur","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-4","question":"Which two standard amino acids contain sulfur?","options":["Cysteine and methionine","Aspartate and glutamate","Serine and threonine","Phenylalanine and tyrosine"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The presence of sulfur does not make both residues equivalent: cysteine can form disulfides while methionine usually cannot.","reasons":["Cysteine has a thiol and methionine a thioether.","Their characteristic side-chain groups are carboxylates.","Both have hydroxyl groups but no sulfur.","Their aromatic rings do not contain sulfur."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-midterm","page":35}],"originalQuestion":"Which amino acids below contain sulphur? Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Added complete options to a recalled short answer.","sourceExcerpt":"Which amino acids below contain sulphur? Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-8:guide-8"},{"id":"expanded-application-8-4","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:1","question":"Which residue belongs to both the aromatic group and the common hydroxyl phosphorylation sites?","options":["Tyrosine","Phenylalanine","Methionine","Aspartate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tyrosine has an aromatic ring and a hydroxyl group that can undergo phosphorylation. Phenylalanine is aromatic but lacks the hydroxyl, while serine and threonine have hydroxyls without an aromatic ring.","reasons":["Tyrosine has an aromatic ring and a hydroxyl group that can undergo phosphorylation. Phenylalanine is aromatic but lacks the hydroxyl, while serine and threonine have hydroxyls without an aromatic ring.","Phenylalanine has no tyrosine-like phenolic hydroxyl.","Methionine has a thioether side chain, not an aromatic hydroxyl.","Aspartate has an acidic carboxylate side chain, not the aromatic hydroxyl specified."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":16}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-8"}],"MOL-8:guide-9":[{"id":"drill-MOL-8-1-0","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:1","question":"Which statement best explains common protein modifications?","options":["Serine, threonine and tyrosine are common phosphorylation sites","Aspartate and glutamate are usually negatively charged near physiological pH","Nonpolar side chains often pack inside soluble globular proteins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Their hydroxyl groups can carry phosphate. N-linked glycosylation attaches to asparagine, while many O-linked glycans attach to serine or threonine. Do not confuse glycosylation with phosphorylation.","reasons":["Correct. Their hydroxyl groups can carry phosphate. N-linked glycosylation attaches to asparagine, while many O-linked glycans attach to serine or threonine. Do not confuse glycosylation with phosphorylation.","This explains Amino-acid side chains. Lysine and arginine are usually positive, while histidine can change protonation near relevant physiological ranges. A polar uncharged residue such as asparagine can hydrogen bond without carrying the same ionic charge as aspartate. For common protein modifications, use the rule: Serine, threonine and tyrosine are common phosphorylation sites.","This explains Hydrophobic packing. Hydrophobic residues minimize exposure to water. Charged residues are often surface-exposed. This is a general folding principle, not an absolute rule for every protein, especially membrane proteins. For common protein modifications, use the rule: Serine, threonine and tyrosine are common phosphorylation sites."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":64},{"source":"mol-past","page":65},{"source":"mol-past","page":66}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-9"},{"id":"mol-past-a4d6e1263571","source":"mol-past","page":64,"endPage":64,"number":"1","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"question":"Which post-translational modification occurs to Asparagine?","options":["N-linked glycosylation","O-linked glycosylation","Poly A tail","N-linked acetylization"],"answer":0,"sourceNote":"","imageDependent":false,"duplicateKey":"a4d6e1263571","keyAmbiguous":false,"concept":"MOL-8:1","explanation":"N-linked glycosylation attaches carbohydrate to the amide nitrogen of an asparagine side chain in an appropriate sequence context.","reasons":["Asparagine is the residue used for N-linked glycosylation.","O-linked glycosylation commonly uses hydroxyl groups such as serine or threonine.","A poly-A tail is an RNA processing feature rather than an amino-acid modification.","This option does not name the glycosylation process asked about."],"kind":"Past-paper question · reviewed explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":64}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-9"}],"MOL-8:guide-10":[{"id":"expanded-application-8-7","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-5","question":"For Gly–Leu–Asp–Ser–Arg–Tyr–Met, which property is supported directly by the sequence?","options":["It has acidic and basic side chains and common phosphorylation sites","Every residue supplies a separate free alpha-amino group","Methionine guarantees an intrachain disulfide","The peptide has no polar groups"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Asp provides an acidic side chain, Arg a basic one and Ser/Tyr common hydroxyl phosphorylation sites. Exact net charge requires pH and pKa information, while internal backbone groups are engaged in peptide bonds.","reasons":["Asp provides an acidic side chain, Arg a basic one and Ser/Tyr common hydroxyl phosphorylation sites. Exact net charge requires pH and pKa information, while internal backbone groups are engaged in peptide bonds.","Only the N terminus is an ordinary free alpha-amino group; internal ones are amidated.","A disulfide requires cysteine thiols, absent from this sequence.","Asp, Ser, Arg and Tyr provide polar or charged chemistry, so the peptide is not wholly nonpolar."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":19}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-10"},{"id":"mol-slides-8-application-3","lectureIds":["MOL-8"],"concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-5","question":"For Gly–Leu–Asp–Ser–Arg–Tyr–Met, which statement is correct?","options":["It contains two sulfur-containing residues","It contains only nonpolar side chains","It contains both an acidic and a basic side chain","It has no potential hydroxyl phosphorylation sites"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Asp contributes an acidic side chain and Arg a basic one. Ser and Tyr also have hydroxyl groups, while Met is the sole sulfur-containing residue in this sequence.","reasons":["Only Met among these listed residues contains sulfur.","Asp, Arg, Ser and Tyr prevent this description.","Asp and Arg provide the acidic/basic pair.","Ser and Tyr are common hydroxyl phosphorylation targets."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":19}],"topicId":"MOL-8:guide-10"}],"MOL-9:guide-1":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-9:guide-1-1","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:0","question":"Which comparison correctly distinguishes pH from pKa?","options":["pH and pKa are always the same numerical property","pKa counts amino-acid residues, while pH counts chromosomes","Adding acid changes every acid’s chemical identity into a different pKa","pH describes solution acidity; pKa describes a particular acid–base equilibrium"],"answer":3,"reasons":["They are equal only in particular conditions, such as equal conjugate acid/base in the usual buffer relation.","Neither quantity is a count of residues or chromosomes.","Changing solution acidity is not the same as replacing the underlying ionizable chemical group.","pH belongs to the solution, whereas pKa characterizes the dissociation equilibrium of an ionizable group under stated conditions."],"explanation":"pH belongs to the solution, whereas pKa characterizes the dissociation equilibrium of an ionizable group under stated conditions. pH measures the solution’s acidity. pKa describes an acid–base equilibrium.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":4}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-9:guide-1"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-9:guide-1-2","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:0","question":"Under comparable conditions, which acid is stronger?","options":["Whichever acid is placed in the larger container","Both must have identical strength whenever solution pH changes","The acid with the lower pKa","The acid with the higher pKa, in every ordinary comparison"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Container size by itself does not define the dissociation tendency of an acid.","Changing solution pH does not make all distinct acids identical in strength.","Because pKa is −log10 Ka, a lower pKa corresponds to a larger dissociation constant and stronger acid in the comparison.","This reverses the relationship between pKa and acid strength."],"explanation":"Because pKa is −log10 Ka, a lower pKa corresponds to a larger dissociation constant and stronger acid in the comparison. pH measures the solution’s acidity. pKa describes an acid–base equilibrium.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":4}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-9:guide-1"}],"MOL-9:guide-2":[{"id":"mol-slides-9-application-1","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-0","question":"A weak-acid buffer has pKa 6.4 and [A−]/[HA] = 10. What is its pH?","options":["5.4","6.4","7.4","10.0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Henderson–Hasselbalch gives pH = 6.4 + log10(10) = 7.4.","reasons":["This would result from a base-to-acid ratio of 0.1.","This would result from equal base and acid.","Adding the logarithmic term of one gives 7.4.","The ratio is not itself the pH."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":7}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-2"},{"id":"expanded-application-9-1","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:0","question":"A buffer has pKa 6.2 and [A−]/[HA]=10. What is its approximate pH?","options":["7.2","5.2","6.2","62"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Henderson–Hasselbalch gives 6.2+log10(10)=7.2. More conjugate base than acid raises pH above the pKa.","reasons":["Henderson–Hasselbalch gives 6.2+log10(10)=7.2. More conjugate base than acid raises pH above the pKa.","5.2 would correspond to an acid-dominated ratio of 0.1 rather than 10.","pH=pKa requires equal base and acid concentrations.","The logarithmic expression is not multiplication of pKa by the concentration ratio."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":6}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-2"}],"MOL-9:guide-3":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-9:slides-2026-0-0","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-0","question":"Which statement best explains protonation from ph and pka?","options":["When pH exceeds pKa, the deprotonated form predominates","pH equals pKa plus log10 of the base-to-acid ratio","A buffer works best near its pKa","The pI is the pH where the molecule has zero average net charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At pH equal to pKa, the two forms are equal. Below pKa, the protonated form predominates. This is a ratio rule: it does not mean a whole molecule with several groups has only one possible charge.","reasons":["Correct. At pH equal to pKa, the two forms are equal. Below pKa, the protonated form predominates. This is a ratio rule: it does not mean a whole molecule with several groups has only one possible charge.","This explains Henderson–Hasselbalch. When base equals acid, the logarithmic term is zero and pH equals pKa. A base-to-acid ratio of 0.1 gives pH one unit below pKa; a ratio of 10 gives one unit above. For the concept asked here, When pH exceeds pKa, the deprotonated form predominates.","This explains Buffer choice. Both protonated and deprotonated forms must be available to absorb added base or acid. A useful working range is roughly pKa plus or minus one. Choose the closest suitable pKa rather than merely the widest listed range. For the concept asked here, When pH exceeds pKa, the deprotonated form predominates.","This explains Isoelectric point. Below pI, an amino acid tends to be more positively charged; above pI, more negatively charged. Zero net charge does not mean there are no charged groups. For simple neutral side chains, use the two pKa values flanking the neutral species. For the concept asked here, When pH exceeds pKa, the deprotonated form predominates."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":7},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":243}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-3"},{"id":"expanded-application-9-2","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-0","question":"A carboxyl group has pKa 4.0 and is in a solution at pH 7.0. Which form predominates?","options":["Deprotonated COO−","Protonated COOH","A permanent positive carboxyl ion","A peptide bond formed automatically with water"],"answer":0,"explanation":"pH is above pKa, so the deprotonated form predominates. For a carboxyl group this means COO−. Protonation state and charge must be linked to the specific group being considered.","reasons":["pH is above pKa, so the deprotonated form predominates. For a carboxyl group this means COO−. Protonation state and charge must be linked to the specific group being considered.","COOH predominates below rather than well above its pKa.","Deprotonation of a carboxyl group gives negative, not positive, charge.","An acid–base equilibrium does not automatically form a peptide bond."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":7}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-3"}],"MOL-9:guide-4":[{"id":"expanded-application-9-3","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:1","question":"Two buffers have the same pKa and base/acid ratio, but one has 100 times greater total concentration. Which statement is best?","options":["They can start at the same pH, but the concentrated one generally has greater capacity","The concentrated one must have pH 100 units higher","The dilute one always neutralizes unlimited acid","Their capacities must be identical because their pH is identical"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ratio controls starting pH in Henderson–Hasselbalch, while total amounts influence how much acid/base can be consumed. Equal pH therefore does not imply equal buffer capacity.","reasons":["The ratio controls starting pH in Henderson–Hasselbalch, while total amounts influence how much acid/base can be consumed. Equal pH therefore does not imply equal buffer capacity.","pH is logarithmic and is not raised by multiplying the total concentration at an unchanged ratio.","All buffers have finite amounts of acid/base components and therefore finite capacity.","Capacity depends on available buffer components, not pH alone."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":8}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-4"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-9:guide-4-1","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:0","question":"When a small amount of strong acid is added to a useful HA/A− buffer, which component chiefly consumes the added H+?","options":["HA, forming more A− without consuming H+","The covalent backbone of every container molecule","Only the sodium atoms, regardless of buffer composition","A−, forming more HA"],"answer":3,"reasons":["HA supplies a proton when buffering added base; it does not describe the stated uptake of added H+.","Ordinary buffering is acid–base interconversion, not indiscriminate destruction of molecular backbones.","Buffer action depends on its acid/base components rather than an arbitrary sodium-only rule.","The conjugate base accepts added H+, shifting the acid/base balance toward HA and limiting the pH change."],"explanation":"The conjugate base accepts added H+, shifting the acid/base balance toward HA and limiting the pH change. Added acid consumes A−. Added base consumes HA.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":8}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-9:guide-4"}],"MOL-9:guide-5":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-9:slides-2026-1-0","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-1","question":"Which statement best explains titration plateaus?","options":["Each ionizable group contributes a pKa and a buffering region","pH equals pKa plus log10 of the base-to-acid ratio","A buffer works best near its pKa","The pI is the pH where the molecule has zero average net charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Glycine has two main ionizable groups, while some side chains add further pKa values. Near each pKa, the acid and conjugate-base forms can buffer added acid or base. The pI and pKa answer different questions.","reasons":["Correct. Glycine has two main ionizable groups, while some side chains add further pKa values. Near each pKa, the acid and conjugate-base forms can buffer added acid or base. The pI and pKa answer different questions.","This explains Henderson–Hasselbalch. When base equals acid, the logarithmic term is zero and pH equals pKa. A base-to-acid ratio of 0.1 gives pH one unit below pKa; a ratio of 10 gives one unit above. For the concept asked here, Each ionizable group contributes a pKa and a buffering region.","This explains Buffer choice. Both protonated and deprotonated forms must be available to absorb added base or acid. A useful working range is roughly pKa plus or minus one. Choose the closest suitable pKa rather than merely the widest listed range. For the concept asked here, Each ionizable group contributes a pKa and a buffering region.","This explains Isoelectric point. Below pI, an amino acid tends to be more positively charged; above pI, more negatively charged. Zero net charge does not mean there are no charged groups. For simple neutral side chains, use the two pKa values flanking the neutral species. For the concept asked here, Each ionizable group contributes a pKa and a buffering region."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":9},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":232}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-5"},{"id":"expanded-application-9-4","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-1","question":"On a simple monoprotic acid titration curve, where are acid and conjugate base equal?","options":["At the half-equivalence point where pH=pKa","Only when pH=0","Only after all acid is completely exhausted","At every point on the titration curve"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At half-equivalence, half the original acid has become conjugate base, giving a 1:1 ratio and a zero logarithm term. This is the center of the useful buffering region.","reasons":["At half-equivalence, half the original acid has become conjugate base, giving a 1:1 ratio and a zero logarithm term. This is the center of the useful buffering region.","Equal proportions depend on the acid’s pKa, not a universal pH of zero.","Complete conversion leaves little acid and is not a 1:1 mixture.","The ratio changes continuously as titrant is added."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":7}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-5"}],"MOL-9:guide-6":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-1","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-1","question":"A glycine titration curve has plateaus near pH 2.3 and pH 9.6. At pH 8, which ionizable group is approaching its buffering region?","options":["The alpha-ammonium group","A glycine side-chain imidazole group","The alpha-carboxyl group with pKa near 2.3","A glycine side-chain carboxyl group"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Glycine has two ionizable groups. Use the plateau nearest the pH rather than relying on a missing graph’s phase labels.","reasons":["Its pKa near 9.6 is much closer to pH 8 than the carboxyl pKa.","Imidazole is a histidine feature, not a glycine feature.","At pH 8 it is almost completely deprotonated and buffers poorly.","Glycine has hydrogen as its side chain and no extra carboxyl group."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":2}],"originalQuestion":"At pH 8.0, which of the following phases of the titration curve for glycine is working best as a buffer? A. Phase 1 B. Phase 2 C. Phase 3 D. Phase 4 E. Phase 5 Answer: D","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed the graph-dependent phase-label question using explicit pKa values; pH 8 is not glycine’s optimal buffering pH.","sourceExcerpt":"At pH 8.0, which of the following phases of the titration curve for glycine is working best as a buffer? A. Phase 1 B. Phase 2 C. Phase 3 D. Phase 4 E. Phase 5 Answer: D","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":2,"number":1,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-1"}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-6"},{"id":"expanded-application-9-5","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:2","question":"Glycine has illustrative pKas of 2.3 and 9.6 and no ionizable side chain. What is its approximate pI?","options":["5.95","11.9","2.3","9.6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The net-zero form lies between loss of the carboxyl proton and loss of the ammonium proton, so pI=(2.3+9.6)/2=5.95. For ionizable side chains, a different relevant pKa pair may be required.","reasons":["The net-zero form lies between loss of the carboxyl proton and loss of the ammonium proton, so pI=(2.3+9.6)/2=5.95. For ionizable side chains, a different relevant pKa pair may be required.","11.9 is the sum of the two pKas before division by two; the pI for this neutral-side-chain amino acid requires their average.","The first pKa is one dissociation midpoint rather than the net-zero midpoint.","The second pKa is the other dissociation midpoint, not the average defining this pI."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":10}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-6"}],"MOL-9:guide-7":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-9:slides-2026-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-2","question":"Which statement best explains peptide ionization?","options":["A peptide’s free termini and ionizable side chains contribute to its charge","pH equals pKa plus log10 of the base-to-acid ratio","A buffer works best near its pKa","The pI is the pH where the molecule has zero average net charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Amino and carboxyl groups already used in ordinary internal peptide bonds do not behave like free terminal groups. Count the available groups rather than simply assigning two freely titratable groups to every residue in the chain.","reasons":["Correct. Amino and carboxyl groups already used in ordinary internal peptide bonds do not behave like free terminal groups. Count the available groups rather than simply assigning two freely titratable groups to every residue in the chain.","This explains Henderson–Hasselbalch. When base equals acid, the logarithmic term is zero and pH equals pKa. A base-to-acid ratio of 0.1 gives pH one unit below pKa; a ratio of 10 gives one unit above. For the concept asked here, A peptide’s free termini and ionizable side chains contribute to its charge.","This explains Buffer choice. Both protonated and deprotonated forms must be available to absorb added base or acid. A useful working range is roughly pKa plus or minus one. Choose the closest suitable pKa rather than merely the widest listed range. For the concept asked here, A peptide’s free termini and ionizable side chains contribute to its charge.","This explains Isoelectric point. Below pI, an amino acid tends to be more positively charged; above pI, more negatively charged. Zero net charge does not mean there are no charged groups. For simple neutral side chains, use the two pKa values flanking the neutral species. For the concept asked here, A peptide’s free termini and ionizable side chains contribute to its charge."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":11},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":235}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-7"},{"id":"expanded-application-9-6","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-2","question":"When estimating a peptide’s charge, which ordinary backbone groups should be counted as free termini?","options":["One N terminus and one C terminus, plus relevant ionizable side chains","One free amino and carboxyl group for every internal residue","Only the peptide-bond nitrogen atoms, each as +1","No termini because peptides cannot ionize"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Internal alpha-amino and carboxyl groups are engaged in peptide bonds. Count the free chain ends and ionizable side chains, then use the pH relative to their pKas to assign charges.","reasons":["Internal alpha-amino and carboxyl groups are engaged in peptide bonds. Count the free chain ends and ionizable side chains, then use the pH relative to their pKas to assign charges.","This wrongly treats amidated internal groups as separate free amino acids.","Peptide-bond nitrogens are not all free ammonium groups carrying +1.","Peptides retain terminal and side-chain groups that can ionize."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":11}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-7"}],"MOL-9:guide-8":[{"id":"topic-check-MOL-9:guide-8-1","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-5","question":"What happens when soluble insulin glargine in its acidic formulation is neutralized after subcutaneous administration in the taught example?","options":["Microprecipitates form and release insulin gradually","The molecule becomes permanently incapable of forming any precipitate","Every peptide bond must be cleaved before the formulation can act","Its sequence immediately changes back into the human insulin sequence"],"answer":0,"reasons":["The coherent mechanism is precipitation after neutralization followed by gradual release.","This contradicts the microprecipitate mechanism described in the topic.","The example concerns physicochemical solubility behavior rather than required peptide-backbone destruction.","Neutralizing the formulation does not reverse the engineered amino-acid sequence."],"explanation":"The coherent mechanism is precipitation after neutralization followed by gradual release. Sequence changes can shift pI and solubility behavior. Glargine forms microprecipitates after neutralization.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":12}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-9:guide-8"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-9:guide-8-2","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-5","question":"Why can a change in protein sequence change its solubility near a particular pH?","options":["All sequences have exactly the same charge at every pH","Solubility is determined only by the protein’s name","Sequence changes can affect only DNA, never the protein’s physicochemical properties","Sequence changes can alter charge distribution and isoelectric behavior"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Ionizable groups and sequence differences can produce different charge behavior.","A name does not explain the underlying molecular solubility mechanism.","An amino-acid sequence change can directly alter a protein’s properties.","Changing residues can alter net-charge behavior and the electrostatic interactions that influence solubility."],"explanation":"Changing residues can alter net-charge behavior and the electrostatic interactions that influence solubility. Sequence changes can shift pI and solubility behavior. Glargine forms microprecipitates after neutralization.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":12}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-9:guide-8"}],"MOL-9:guide-9":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-9:slides-2026-4-0","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-4","question":"Which statement best explains physiological buffer systems?","options":["Bicarbonate is central extracellularly; proteins and phosphate contribute importantly inside cells","pH equals pKa plus log10 of the base-to-acid ratio","A buffer works best near its pKa","The pI is the pH where the molecule has zero average net charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Buffer effectiveness depends on concentration, pKa and whether components are regulated. The bicarbonate system is coupled to respiratory CO2 handling rather than behaving as a sealed beaker containing a fixed amount of acid.","reasons":["Correct. Buffer effectiveness depends on concentration, pKa and whether components are regulated. The bicarbonate system is coupled to respiratory CO2 handling rather than behaving as a sealed beaker containing a fixed amount of acid.","This explains Henderson–Hasselbalch. When base equals acid, the logarithmic term is zero and pH equals pKa. A base-to-acid ratio of 0.1 gives pH one unit below pKa; a ratio of 10 gives one unit above. For the concept asked here, Bicarbonate is central extracellularly; proteins and phosphate contribute importantly inside cells.","This explains Buffer choice. Both protonated and deprotonated forms must be available to absorb added base or acid. A useful working range is roughly pKa plus or minus one. Choose the closest suitable pKa rather than merely the widest listed range. For the concept asked here, Bicarbonate is central extracellularly; proteins and phosphate contribute importantly inside cells.","This explains Isoelectric point. Below pI, an amino acid tends to be more positively charged; above pI, more negatively charged. Zero net charge does not mean there are no charged groups. For simple neutral side chains, use the two pKa values flanking the neutral species. For the concept asked here, Bicarbonate is central extracellularly; proteins and phosphate contribute importantly inside cells."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":17},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":241}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-9"},{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-9:slides-2026-3-0","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-3","question":"Which statement best explains volatile versus fixed acid?","options":["CO2-related acid is handled through respiration, while fixed acid also requires renal handling","pH equals pKa plus log10 of the base-to-acid ratio","A buffer works best near its pKa","The pI is the pH where the molecule has zero average net charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Buffers limit the immediate pH change but do not by themselves remove the acid load from the body. Distinguish chemical buffering from actual elimination through lungs and kidneys.","reasons":["Correct. Buffers limit the immediate pH change but do not by themselves remove the acid load from the body. Distinguish chemical buffering from actual elimination through lungs and kidneys.","This explains Henderson–Hasselbalch. When base equals acid, the logarithmic term is zero and pH equals pKa. A base-to-acid ratio of 0.1 gives pH one unit below pKa; a ratio of 10 gives one unit above. For the concept asked here, CO2-related acid is handled through respiration, while fixed acid also requires renal handling.","This explains Buffer choice. Both protonated and deprotonated forms must be available to absorb added base or acid. A useful working range is roughly pKa plus or minus one. Choose the closest suitable pKa rather than merely the widest listed range. For the concept asked here, CO2-related acid is handled through respiration, while fixed acid also requires renal handling.","This explains Isoelectric point. Below pI, an amino acid tends to be more positively charged; above pI, more negatively charged. Zero net charge does not mean there are no charged groups. For simple neutral side chains, use the two pKa values flanking the neutral species. For the concept asked here, CO2-related acid is handled through respiration, while fixed acid also requires renal handling."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":13},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":229}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-9"}],"MOL-9:guide-10":[{"id":"slides-drill-MOL-9:slides-2026-5-0","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-5","question":"Which statement best explains alkalosis and ionized calcium?","options":["Hyperventilation-related alkalosis can increase calcium binding to albumin and reduce ionized calcium","pH equals pKa plus log10 of the base-to-acid ratio","A buffer works best near its pKa","The pI is the pH where the molecule has zero average net charge"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reduced ionized calcium helps explain neuromuscular excitability in the lecture’s high-altitude scenario. Total calcium and the free ionized fraction are different measurements. This question tests binding chemistry, not a treatment decision.","reasons":["Correct. Reduced ionized calcium helps explain neuromuscular excitability in the lecture’s high-altitude scenario. Total calcium and the free ionized fraction are different measurements. This question tests binding chemistry, not a treatment decision.","This explains Henderson–Hasselbalch. When base equals acid, the logarithmic term is zero and pH equals pKa. A base-to-acid ratio of 0.1 gives pH one unit below pKa; a ratio of 10 gives one unit above. For the concept asked here, Hyperventilation-related alkalosis can increase calcium binding to albumin and reduce ionized calcium.","This explains Buffer choice. Both protonated and deprotonated forms must be available to absorb added base or acid. A useful working range is roughly pKa plus or minus one. Choose the closest suitable pKa rather than merely the widest listed range. For the concept asked here, Hyperventilation-related alkalosis can increase calcium binding to albumin and reduce ionized calcium.","This explains Isoelectric point. Below pI, an amino acid tends to be more positively charged; above pI, more negatively charged. Zero net charge does not mean there are no charged groups. For simple neutral side chains, use the two pKa values flanking the neutral species. For the concept asked here, Hyperventilation-related alkalosis can increase calcium binding to albumin and reduce ionized calcium."],"kind":"Concept check · slide-grounded study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":20},{"source":"mol-transcripts","page":229}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-10"},{"id":"mol-slides-9-application-3","lectureIds":["MOL-9"],"concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-5","question":"In the lecture’s high-altitude hyperventilation scenario, why can alkalosis contribute to tetany?","options":["It necessarily reduces all plasma protein concentrations","It increases calcium binding to protein and lowers ionized calcium","It proves every total-calcium measurement must fall equally","It blocks all calcium binding to albumin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The question distinguishes free ionized calcium from total calcium. Increased protein binding can lower the free fraction during alkalosis.","reasons":["A fall in all plasma proteins is not the stated mechanism.","This is the binding-based explanation.","Total and ionized calcium do not have to change in the same way.","This is the opposite of the described binding effect."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":20}],"topicId":"MOL-9:guide-10"}],"MOL-10:guide-1":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-1","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-0","question":"Two polypeptides contain the same number of each amino acid but a different order. Which statement is best?","options":["Their primary structures differ and their folding or function may differ","Their primary structures are identical because composition is identical","They must have the same active site","Neither can have peptide bonds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Primary structure concerns sequence order, not just composition. Changing residue order changes the possible intrachain contacts, active-site geometry and targeting information, so equivalent composition does not guarantee equivalent function.","reasons":["Primary structure concerns sequence order, not just composition. Changing residue order changes the possible intrachain contacts, active-site geometry and targeting information, so equivalent composition does not guarantee equivalent function.","Composition omits the order that defines primary structure.","An active site depends on a specific three-dimensional arrangement that need not be preserved.","Both can remain ordinary peptide-bonded polypeptides despite different sequences."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":7}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-1"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-10:guide-1-1","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-0","question":"A single amino-acid substitution changes a protein’s surface interaction but much of its normal fold remains. Is this compatible with a change in function?","options":["No; only total amino-acid counts matter, not their order","Only if the protein’s sequence is converted into RNA","Yes; a specific local interaction can change without complete unfolding","No; function changes only after every peptide bond is cleaved"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Primary structure includes residue order, which influences interactions and function.","A protein does not have to become RNA for a sequence change to affect function.","The taught relationship includes local surfaces, targeting signals and catalytic features, not only global folding.","Complete backbone cleavage is not required for every functional change."],"explanation":"The taught relationship includes local surfaces, targeting signals and catalytic features, not only global folding. Sequence order matters, not only composition. A single substitution can alter a specific protein behavior.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":7}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-10:guide-1"}],"MOL-10:guide-2":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-2","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-1","question":"Why is rotation about the peptide C–N bond more restricted than rotation about some neighboring backbone bonds?","options":["Resonance gives the peptide bond partial double-bond character","The peptide bond is an ionic attraction with no electrons","Each peptide bond is a disulfide bridge","There are no atoms between successive amino acids"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Electron delocalization gives the peptide linkage partial double-bond character and a relatively planar peptide unit. Backbone conformation still changes through adjacent bonds within steric constraints.","reasons":["Electron delocalization gives the peptide linkage partial double-bond character and a relatively planar peptide unit. Backbone conformation still changes through adjacent bonds within steric constraints.","The linkage is a covalent amide, not an electron-free ionic attraction.","Disulfides join cysteine side chains and are chemically distinct.","The repeating backbone contains N, Cα and carbonyl carbon between residue connections."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":4}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-2"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-94","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:1","question":"What is a peptide bond?","options":["A covalent amide linkage between a residue’s carboxyl carbon and the next residue’s amino nitrogen","An ionic attraction between side chains","A disulfide between two methionines","A hydrogen bond between DNA bases"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The C(=O)–N bond forms the protein backbone and has partial double-bond character.","reasons":["The C(=O)–N bond forms the protein backbone and has partial double-bond character.","That is a salt bridge, not the covalent peptide bond.","Disulfides ordinarily form from cysteine thiols, not methionine.","That concerns nucleic-acid base pairing."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":100}],"originalQuestion":"The Peptide Bond: A) Is formed between Carboxyl group of one amino acid and a hydrogen of the other amino acid B) Ionic bond C) The area of the bond is highly charged D) Denaturation can’t break the bond Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Reconstructed the source’s partially incorrect peptide-bond descriptions.","sourceExcerpt":"The Peptide Bond: A) Is formed between Carboxyl group of one amino acid and a hydrogen of the other amino acid B) Ionic bond C) The area of the bond is highly charged D) Denaturation can’t break the bond Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-10:guide-2"}],"MOL-10:guide-3":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-3","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-2","question":"A single-chain globular protein has several helices packed into a complete fold but no other subunits. Which structural level is absent in the usual classification?","options":["Quaternary structure","Primary structure","Secondary structure","Tertiary structure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Quaternary structure describes association of separate polypeptide subunits. A one-chain protein can still have sequence, local secondary structures and an overall tertiary fold.","reasons":["Quaternary structure describes association of separate polypeptide subunits. A one-chain protein can still have sequence, local secondary structures and an overall tertiary fold.","The described chain necessarily has an amino-acid sequence.","The stem explicitly states that helices are present.","The complete fold of the one chain is its tertiary structure."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":5}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-3"},{"id":"drill-MOL-10-0-0","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:0","question":"Which statement best explains protein structure hierarchy?","options":["Primary is sequence, secondary is local folding, tertiary is one-chain folding and quaternary is subunit assembly","Peptide bonds are covalent links between amino acids","Two cysteine side chains can form a covalent disulfide bond"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A dimer introduces interactions between separate polypeptides, so it has quaternary organization. Not every protein has multiple subunits. An alpha helix is secondary rather than quaternary structure.","reasons":["Correct. A dimer introduces interactions between separate polypeptides, so it has quaternary organization. Not every protein has multiple subunits. An alpha helix is secondary rather than quaternary structure.","This explains Peptide backbone. Backbone hydrogen bonds stabilize alpha helices and beta sheets. Peptide bonds have partial double-bond character and limited rotation. Do not confuse the covalent sequence with noncovalent stabilization of its folded shape. For protein structure hierarchy, use the rule: Primary is sequence, secondary is local folding, tertiary is one-chain folding and quaternary is subunit assembly.","This explains Disulfide bonds. Oxidation links two sulfhydryl groups. Methionine contains sulfur but does not have the same thiol chemistry. Disulfides can stabilize one folded chain or link separate chains. For protein structure hierarchy, use the rule: Primary is sequence, secondary is local folding, tertiary is one-chain folding and quaternary is subunit assembly."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":92},{"source":"mol-past","page":93},{"source":"mol-past","page":94}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-3"}],"MOL-10:guide-4":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-4","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-3","question":"Which hydrogen-bond pattern characterizes an ordinary alpha helix?","options":["Backbone C=O of residue i to backbone N–H of residue i+4","Covalent disulfide from every residue i to i+4","Hydrogen bonds only between side chains of adjacent alanines","Peptide hydrolysis at every fourth residue"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The repeated i→i+4 backbone hydrogen bonds stabilize the alpha-helical arrangement. The usual helix is right-handed with about 3.6 residues per turn and outward-facing side chains.","reasons":["The repeated i→i+4 backbone hydrogen bonds stabilize the alpha-helical arrangement. The usual helix is right-handed with about 3.6 residues per turn and outward-facing side chains.","Only cysteine thiols can form disulfides, and those are not the defining helix pattern.","The characteristic bonds use backbone groups rather than only alanine side chains.","Helix formation retains the peptide backbone rather than cleaving it periodically."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":9}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-4"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-48","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:0","question":"Which interactions directly stabilize an alpha helix in myoglobin?","options":["Hydrogen bonds between backbone carbonyl oxygen and amide hydrogen","Only hydrogen bonds between side chains","Disulfide bonds between every pair of neighboring residues","Phosphodiester bonds between amino acids"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In an alpha helix, a backbone C=O commonly hydrogen-bonds to the N–H four residues ahead.","reasons":["Regular backbone hydrogen bonding supports the alpha-helical pattern.","The characteristic alpha-helix bonds involve the peptide backbone.","Disulfide bonds require cysteine pairs and do not define the alpha helix.","Phosphodiester bonds form nucleic-acid backbones; proteins have peptide bonds."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":11}],"originalQuestion":"What primarily stabilizes the α- helical structure in myoglobin? A) Ionic interactions between R-groups B) Hydrogen bonds between side chains C) Hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl oxygen and the amide hydrogen D) Disulfide bond formation Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"What primarily stabilizes the α- helical structure in myoglobin? A) Ionic interactions between R-groups B) Hydrogen bonds between side chains C) Hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl oxygen and the amide hydrogen D) Disulfide bond formation Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":11,"number":48,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-48"}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-4"}],"MOL-10:guide-5":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-92","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:0","question":"Which comparison between alpha helices and beta sheets is correct?","options":["Only alpha helices can occur in globular proteins","Only beta sheets are secondary structure","Beta sheets are always between different polypeptide chains","Both use backbone hydrogen bonds, but beta sheets pair extended strand segments"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Beta sheets hydrogen-bond between neighboring extended strands; alpha helices have a regular intrachain helical pattern.","reasons":["Both can occur in globular proteins.","Both are examples of secondary structure.","A sheet can contain segments from one chain or from different chains.","Beta sheets hydrogen-bond between neighboring extended strands; alpha helices have a regular intrachain helical pattern."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":99}],"originalQuestion":"In comparing the a-helix to b-sheet, which statement is correct ONLY for the b-sheet? A) Extensive hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl oxygen (C=O) and the amide hydrogen (N- H) of the peptide bond are formed B) It may be found in typical globular proteins. C) It is stabilized by intrachain hydrogen bonds. D) It is an example of secondary structure. E) It may be found in supersecondary structures Answer:C","adaptationNote":"Repaired the source question whose claimed beta-only property intrachain bonds was not unique or universally applicable.","sourceExcerpt":"In comparing the a-helix to b-sheet, which statement is correct ONLY for the b-sheet? A) Extensive hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl oxygen (C=O) and the amide hydrogen (N- H) of the peptide bond are formed B) It may be found in typical globular proteins. C) It is stabilized by intrachain hydrogen bonds. D) It is an example of secondary structure. E) It may be found in supersecondary structures Answer:C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-10:guide-5"},{"id":"expanded-application-10-5","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-4","question":"Two beta strands run in opposite N→C directions and form backbone hydrogen bonds. How are they described?","options":["An antiparallel beta sheet","A mandatory two-chain quaternary complex","An alpha helix with no hydrogen bonds","A sequence of phosphodiester-linked amino acids"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Opposite strand directions define an antiparallel sheet. The strands may belong to distant parts of one chain, so a sheet does not by itself establish a multichain quaternary structure.","reasons":["Opposite strand directions define an antiparallel sheet. The strands may belong to distant parts of one chain, so a sheet does not by itself establish a multichain quaternary structure.","The strands can be regions of one polypeptide; separate chains are not required.","The described extended paired strands are not an alpha helix and explicitly do use hydrogen bonds.","Amino acids are joined by peptide bonds, not nucleic-acid phosphodiesters."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":10}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-5"}],"MOL-10:guide-6":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-6","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-5","question":"A reagent disrupts side-chain hydrophobic packing in a monomeric protein while leaving its peptide bonds and local backbone hydrogen bonds intact. Which level is most directly destabilized?","options":["Tertiary structure","Primary sequence","The genetic code","Quaternary assembly that the monomer does not possess"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hydrophobic packing helps organize the overall fold of one chain, which is tertiary structure. The stated preservation of peptide bonds and local hydrogen bonds narrows the most direct effect.","reasons":["Hydrophobic packing helps organize the overall fold of one chain, which is tertiary structure. The stated preservation of peptide bonds and local hydrogen bonds narrows the most direct effect.","Primary sequence is retained when peptide bonds are not hydrolyzed.","The codon table is unrelated to directly disrupting an already synthesized fold.","A monomer lacks the separate-subunit assembly required for quaternary structure."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":11}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-6"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-91","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:0","question":"What primarily organizes the tertiary structure of a single globular protein chain?","options":["Only the linear count of amino acids","Peptide bonds joining different ribosomal subunits","Only base pairing between nucleotides","The combined interactions and packing of its side chains and backbone"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Hydrophobic packing, hydrogen bonding, electrostatic contacts and sometimes disulfides stabilize the overall fold.","reasons":["Sequence informs folding, but tertiary structure is a three-dimensional arrangement.","This confuses protein folding with a different structure and interaction type.","Proteins are not stabilized by DNA-style complementary base pairs.","Hydrophobic packing, hydrogen bonding, electrostatic contacts and sometimes disulfides stabilize the overall fold."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":98}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following interactions is primarily responsible for stabilizing the tertiary structure of a protein? A) Interactions among the amino acid R groups B) Hydrogen bonds between backbone carbonyl and amide groups C) Peptide bonds linking adjacent amino acids D) Base pairing between complementary nucleotides E) Hydrogen bonds between adjacent polypeptide chains Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Qualified the source’s side-chain-only shorthand: backbone interactions also contribute.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following interactions is primarily responsible for stabilizing the tertiary structure of a protein? A) Interactions among the amino acid R groups B) Hydrogen bonds between backbone carbonyl and amide groups C) Peptide bonds linking adjacent amino acids D) Base pairing between complementary nucleotides E) Hydrogen bonds between adjacent polypeptide chains Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-10:guide-6"}],"MOL-10:guide-7":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-7","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-6","question":"A reducing agent cleaves a bond between two cysteine side chains without cutting the backbone. Which bond was altered?","options":["A disulfide bond","A peptide bond","An N-glycosidic bond","An mRNA cap linkage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Oxidation of two cysteine thiols forms a covalent disulfide. Reduction reverses that crosslink, which is distinct from the backbone amide bonds retaining the protein sequence.","reasons":["Oxidation of two cysteine thiols forms a covalent disulfide. Reduction reverses that crosslink, which is distinct from the backbone amide bonds retaining the protein sequence.","Peptide bonds join backbone amino and carboxyl groups, and the stem says they remain intact.","N-glycosidic bonds connect a nucleic-acid base to sugar in the relevant comparison.","An mRNA cap is an RNA feature rather than a cysteine side-chain crosslink."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":11}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-7"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-75","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:2","question":"Which residue can form disulfide cross-links in keratin?","options":["Phenylalanine","Cysteine","Arginine","Methionine"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Oxidation of two cysteine thiols produces a disulfide bond that can contribute to keratin cross-linking.","reasons":["Its aromatic side chain contains no sulfur.","Oxidation of two cysteine thiols produces a disulfide bond that can contribute to keratin cross-linking.","Its basic guanidinium group does not form a cystine disulfide.","Its thioether is not the thiol pair used in ordinary protein disulfide formation."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":81}],"originalQuestion":"Keratin is found on your hair, it has disulfide bonds. Which amino acid is responsible for curly hair? A) Cysteine B) Methiionine C) Phenylalanine D) Arginine Answer:A ","adaptationNote":"Avoided claiming a single residue alone determines hair curvature.","sourceExcerpt":"Keratin is found on your hair, it has disulfide bonds. Which amino acid is responsible for curly hair? A) Cysteine B) Methiionine C) Phenylalanine D) Arginine Answer:A ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-10:guide-7"}],"MOL-10:guide-8":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-3","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-2","question":"A single-chain globular protein has several helices packed into a complete fold but no other subunits. Which structural level is absent in the usual classification?","options":["Quaternary structure","Primary structure","Secondary structure","Tertiary structure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Quaternary structure describes association of separate polypeptide subunits. A one-chain protein can still have sequence, local secondary structures and an overall tertiary fold.","reasons":["Quaternary structure describes association of separate polypeptide subunits. A one-chain protein can still have sequence, local secondary structures and an overall tertiary fold.","The described chain necessarily has an amino-acid sequence.","The stem explicitly states that helices are present.","The complete fold of the one chain is its tertiary structure."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":5}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-8"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-85","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:0","question":"Two independently folded polypeptides associate into a functional dimer. Which additional structural level is formed?","options":["Quaternary structure","Primary structure only","Secondary structure only","A new DNA duplex"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Quaternary structure describes association of multiple polypeptide subunits.","reasons":["Quaternary structure describes association of multiple polypeptide subunits.","Primary structure is the amino-acid sequence within a chain.","Secondary structure concerns local helix/sheet organization.","Protein-subunit assembly does not create a DNA molecule."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":92}],"originalQuestion":"Binding to the cell surface membrane receptor, caused a change in protein structure, the monomers of the protein joined together to form dimer, what is the change in the highest level of protein structure? A) Primary to Secondary B) Secondary to Tertiary C) Secondary to quaternary D) Tertiary to Quaternary E) Primary to Tertiary Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Binding to the cell surface membrane receptor, caused a change in protein structure, the monomers of the protein joined together to form dimer, what is the change in the highest level of protein structure? A) Primary to Secondary B) Secondary to Tertiary C) Secondary to quaternary D) Tertiary to Quaternary E) Primary to Tertiary Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-10:guide-8"}],"MOL-10:guide-9":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-9","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-8","question":"A chaperone binds exposed hydrophobic regions of a newly synthesized protein. What is its most direct benefit?","options":["Reducing inappropriate aggregation while productive folding occurs","Changing the protein’s amino-acid sequence to a new genetic code","Replacing all missing amino acids permanently","Converting the chain into DNA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Chaperones shield vulnerable regions and assist productive folding or maintenance of an import-competent state. They support the sequence’s folding process rather than rewrite its primary structure.","reasons":["Chaperones shield vulnerable regions and assist productive folding or maintenance of an import-competent state. They support the sequence’s folding process rather than rewrite its primary structure.","Chaperones do not alter the genetic code or normally edit the client sequence.","They are not permanent replacements for amino acids missing from the chain.","Protein-folding assistance does not convert a polypeptide into DNA."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":14}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-9"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-10:guide-9-1","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-8","question":"Which statement best describes a molecular chaperone’s assistance to a client protein?","options":["It must become a permanent subunit of every client it assists","It makes aggregation impossible under every condition","It helps avoid inappropriate interactions while the client folds","It supplies a replacement amino-acid sequence to every client"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Chaperone assistance does not require permanent incorporation into every final protein.","Assistance reduces risk but is not an absolute guarantee that aggregation can never occur.","Chaperones assist productive folding and reduce nonproductive aggregation of vulnerable regions.","The client’s sequence is not supplied anew by the chaperone."],"explanation":"Chaperones assist productive folding and reduce nonproductive aggregation of vulnerable regions. Chaperones limit inappropriate aggregation. Stress can increase demand for protein-folding assistance.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":13}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-10:guide-9"}],"MOL-10:guide-10":[{"id":"expanded-application-10-10","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-9","question":"Protein A has a higher Tm than protein B in the same buffer. What is the most justified conclusion?","options":["A reaches half-unfolding at a higher temperature under those conditions","A must have a longer primary sequence","A can never denature","B must already have had every peptide bond hydrolyzed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tm is the midpoint of thermal unfolding. A higher Tm indicates greater thermal stability in that comparable experiment, not unlimited stability or a required difference in chain length.","reasons":["Tm is the midpoint of thermal unfolding. A higher Tm indicates greater thermal stability in that comparable experiment, not unlimited stability or a required difference in chain length.","Length can influence stability but is not determined solely by Tm.","Even a high-Tm protein can unfold at sufficiently disruptive conditions.","Thermal unfolding and peptide-bond hydrolysis are different events."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":16}],"topicId":"MOL-10:guide-10"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-10:guide-10-1","lectureIds":["MOL-10"],"concept":"MOL-10:expanded-9","question":"A protein loses its native fold and function after an ordinary denaturing treatment, but its peptide backbone is intact. Which interpretation is correct?","options":["Denaturation means the protein has simply melted into a liquid","Higher-order structure can be disrupted while primary sequence remains intact","Loss of function proves that every peptide bond was hydrolyzed","An intact backbone proves the protein cannot be denatured"],"answer":1,"reasons":["Tm refers to fractional unfolding under stated conditions, not an ordinary solid-to-liquid melting event.","Denaturation concerns the native higher-order conformation; ordinary unfolding need not hydrolyze the sequence.","Functional loss does not establish complete peptide-bond hydrolysis.","Backbone integrity is compatible with loss of secondary, tertiary or quaternary organization."],"explanation":"Denaturation concerns the native higher-order conformation; ordinary unfolding need not hydrolyze the sequence. Sequence can remain intact while function is lost. Tm refers to fractional unfolding, not a protein turning into liquid.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":15}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-10:guide-10"}],"MOL-11:guide-1":[{"id":"expanded-application-11-1","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-0","question":"A disease protein has a normal amino-acid sequence but adopts an abnormal aggregation-prone conformation. Which conclusion is supported?","options":["Pathological misfolding can occur without a coding mutation","The protein cannot cause disease unless every residue changes","It must be a frameshift product","The genetic code has disappeared"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Protein conformation can become pathological even with a normal sequence. Sequence variants can increase risk, but not every misfolding or aggregation event requires a coding mutation.","reasons":["Protein conformation can become pathological even with a normal sequence. Sequence variants can increase risk, but not every misfolding or aggregation event requires a coding mutation.","A harmful conformation does not require changing every residue.","A frameshift changes the translation frame and is not implied by the stated normal sequence.","The code remains functional; the defect concerns the protein’s conformational state."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":17}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-1"},{"id":"expanded-application-11-10","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-9","question":"Which comparison separates the molecular examples correctly?","options":["CFTR F508del: folding/trafficking loss; HbS: abnormal intermolecular polymerization","CFTR F508del: an extra tRNA; HbS: M6P deficiency","Both require identical amyloid precursor proteins","Both are caused only by loss of the 5′ mRNA cap"],"answer":0,"explanation":"F508del often reduces delivery of functional CFTR after misfolding, whereas HbS gains an abnormal deoxygenation-dependent interaction. The shared sequence–structure theme has different downstream mechanisms.","reasons":["F508del often reduces delivery of functional CFTR after misfolding, whereas HbS gains an abnormal deoxygenation-dependent interaction. The shared sequence–structure theme has different downstream mechanisms.","Neither an extra tRNA nor M6P deficiency describes these canonical examples.","CFTR and hemoglobin are different proteins with different pathological mechanisms.","The primary examples are coding changes rather than a shared cap-removal defect."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":27}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-1"}],"MOL-11:guide-2":[{"id":"expanded-application-11-2","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-1","question":"Why does the β-globin Glu→Val change favor abnormal HbS interactions?","options":["It introduces a nonpolar surface feature that promotes polymerization, especially on deoxygenation","It adds an extra negative carboxylate at that site","It turns hemoglobin into an RNA polymerase","It removes the entire protein sequence"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Valine is nonpolar whereas glutamate is normally negatively charged. The altered surface permits abnormal interactions that favor deoxy-HbS polymerization and red-cell distortion.","reasons":["Valine is nonpolar whereas glutamate is normally negatively charged. The altered surface permits abnormal interactions that favor deoxy-HbS polymerization and red-cell distortion.","The substitution removes the usual glutamate carboxylate rather than adding one.","A surface substitution does not convert hemoglobin’s protein identity into an RNA polymerase.","This is a single-residue substitution, not deletion of the entire sequence."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":17}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-2"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-24","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:1","question":"In sickle hemoglobin, replacement of beta-globin glutamate by valine promotes which change?","options":["A new negative charge that increases water solubility","Loss of the entire beta-globin chain","A frameshift affecting every downstream codon","A hydrophobic surface patch that favors abnormal aggregation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Glu-to-Val missense mutation creates a nonpolar patch that promotes polymerization of deoxygenated HbS.","reasons":["Valine removes rather than adds the glutamate negative charge.","HbS beta chains are produced but have altered surface properties.","This is a single missense substitution, not a reading-frame shift.","Valine is nonpolar while glutamate is normally charged, enabling contacts between deoxygenated HbS molecules."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":5},{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":3}],"originalQuestion":"A patient with sickle cell anemia has had a hydrophilic amino acid replaced with a hydrophobic one. What does this change cause? A) Increased hydrophobic interactions leading to abnormal hemoglobin aggregation. B) Increased protein folding speed with normal hemoglobin behavior. C) Enhanced solubility of hemoglobin in the blood. D) No effect on hemoglobin structure or function. Answer: A","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"A patient with sickle cell anemia has had a hydrophilic amino acid replaced with a hydrophobic one. What does this change cause? A) Increased hydrophobic interactions leading to abnormal hemoglobin aggregation. B) Increased protein folding speed with normal hemoglobin behavior. C) Enhanced solubility of hemoglobin in the blood. D) No effect on hemoglobin structure or function. Answer: A","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":3,"number":5,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-5"},{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":5,"number":24,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-24"}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-2"}],"MOL-11:guide-3":[{"id":"expanded-application-11-3","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-2","question":"CFTR F508del RNA is produced, but little functional protein reaches the membrane. Which step can explain the discrepancy?","options":["Misfolding causes ER retention and degradation after translation","The absence of a stop codon from every mRNA","The RNA necessarily becomes a GAG","A complete absence of ribosomes from the cell"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gene expression at the RNA level does not guarantee successful folding and trafficking. F508del commonly triggers quality-control retention and degradation, lowering functional membrane channel abundance.","reasons":["Gene expression at the RNA level does not guarantee successful folding and trafficking. F508del commonly triggers quality-control retention and degradation, lowering functional membrane channel abundance.","A stop-codon problem is not the defining in-frame F508del folding mechanism.","RNA is not converted into a glycosaminoglycan in this pathway.","The specific trafficking phenotype does not require total loss of cellular translation machinery."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":17}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-3"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-27","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:0","question":"A misfolded ΔF508 CFTR protein is retained during ER quality control. Which route commonly disposes of it?","options":["Direct release as functional chloride channels","Conversion to mitochondrial DNA","Import into peroxisomes for normal secretion","ER-associated degradation followed by the proteasome"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ER quality control limits delivery of misfolded CFTR to the plasma membrane. ER-associated degradation can direct it to the proteasome.","reasons":["Retention reflects failure to meet folding quality control, so normal surface delivery is reduced.","A membrane protein is not converted to DNA for disposal.","Peroxisomes do not provide the usual CFTR quality-control disposal pathway.","The misfolded protein can be retrotranslocated, ubiquitinated and degraded by the proteasome."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":6}],"originalQuestion":"What is the final destination of a mutated cystic fibrosis transmembrane receptor channel protein? A) Proteasome B) Peroxisome C) Mitochondria D) Lysosome Answer: A","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"What is the final destination of a mutated cystic fibrosis transmembrane receptor channel protein? A) Proteasome B) Peroxisome C) Mitochondria D) Lysosome Answer: A","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":6,"number":27,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-27"}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-3"}],"MOL-11:guide-4":[{"id":"expanded-application-11-4","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-3","question":"Which sequence best represents layered protein quality control?","options":["Chaperone-assisted rescue, selected ubiquitin–proteasome degradation, and autophagic removal of larger material","DNA replication, glycogen branching and urine filtration","Universal secretion of every misfolded protein","Immediate conversion of all aggregates into healthy neurons"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cells can attempt refolding, remove selected damaged proteins and degrade larger material through autophagy/lysosomes. Finite capacity and impaired turnover help explain why aggregates can still accumulate.","reasons":["Cells can attempt refolding, remove selected damaged proteins and degrade larger material through autophagy/lysosomes. Finite capacity and impaired turnover help explain why aggregates can still accumulate.","These processes do not describe the central protein quality-control layers in the lesson.","Misfolded proteins are often retained or degraded rather than universally secreted.","Aggregate removal does not automatically replace neurons already lost to injury."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":18}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-4"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-99","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:0","question":"What can happen to a misfolded cellular protein that fails refolding and quality control?","options":["It may be ubiquitinated and degraded by the proteasome or routed to other degradation pathways","It must always accumulate without any clearance","The nucleus must ingest it","Every misfolded protein functions normally"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Protein quality control can refold, retain, degrade or sequester abnormal proteins depending on context.","reasons":["Protein quality control can refold, retain, degrade or sequester abnormal proteins depending on context.","Cells have degradation and quality-control systems, although they can be overwhelmed.","The nucleus is not a general protein-disposal organelle.","Misfolding can impair function and expose abnormal interactions."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":108}],"originalQuestion":"If a protein is misfolded, what is its fate? A) It is tagged for degradation B) It functions normally C) It is devoured by the nucleus D) It builds up in the cell Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Qualified the original one-fate answer: misfolded-protein outcomes vary.","sourceExcerpt":"If a protein is misfolded, what is its fate? A) It is tagged for degradation B) It functions normally C) It is devoured by the nucleus D) It builds up in the cell Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-11:guide-4"}],"MOL-11:guide-5":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-100","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:2","question":"Amyloid fibrils often share which structural organization despite being formed from different proteins?","options":["A beta-sheet-rich cross-beta architecture","A phospholipid bilayer identical to the plasma membrane","A universally identical amino-acid sequence","Only random-coil structures without order"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Many unrelated proteins can form ordered beta-sheet-rich fibrils.","reasons":["Many unrelated proteins can form ordered beta-sheet-rich fibrils.","Amyloid is a protein aggregate rather than a lipid bilayer.","Different amyloids have different precursor proteins and sequences.","Amyloid fibrils have ordered structural features."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":109}],"originalQuestion":"What is the amyloid protein associated with Alzheimer’s disease composed of? A) Beta pleated sheet. B) Alpha-helical random coil C) Phosphorylated microtubule protein D) Lipid-protein membrane complex Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"What is the amyloid protein associated with Alzheimer’s disease composed of? A) Beta pleated sheet. B) Alpha-helical random coil C) Phosphorylated microtubule protein D) Lipid-protein membrane complex Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-11:guide-5"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-98","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:2","question":"Why can unfolding make some soluble proteins aggregate and precipitate?","options":["Every exposed polar group becomes nonpolar","Denaturation cannot affect solubility","Previously buried hydrophobic regions become exposed and associate","The primary sequence must always be destroyed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exposed nonpolar surfaces can interact with one another, promoting aggregation.","reasons":["Unfolding changes exposure rather than automatically changing chemical identity.","Loss of native packing can substantially change solubility.","Exposed nonpolar surfaces can interact with one another, promoting aggregation.","Aggregation can occur without peptide-backbone cleavage."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":108}],"originalQuestion":"After denaturation, why does a protein precipitate out of solution? A) Exposure of hydrophobic interactions B) Exposure of hydrophilic regions C) Damage to the primary structure D) Denaturation does not affect protein precipitation Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Changed the absolute source claim to some proteins because denaturation need not always cause precipitation.","sourceExcerpt":"After denaturation, why does a protein precipitate out of solution? A) Exposure of hydrophobic interactions B) Exposure of hydrophilic regions C) Damage to the primary structure D) Denaturation does not affect protein precipitation Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-11:guide-5"}],"MOL-11:guide-6":[{"id":"expanded-application-11-6","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-5","question":"Which pairing correctly identifies the classic Alzheimer aggregates and locations?","options":["Aβ plaques outside neurons; tau tangles inside neurons","Tau plaques outside neurons; Aβ tangles inside neurons","Alpha-synuclein plaques outside neurons; glycogen tangles inside","M6P plaques inside lysosomes; DNA tangles outside"],"answer":0,"explanation":"APP-derived Aβ contributes to extracellular plaques, while abnormal tau forms intracellular neurofibrillary tangles. Their distinct proteins and locations are essential to the comparison.","reasons":["APP-derived Aβ contributes to extracellular plaques, while abnormal tau forms intracellular neurofibrillary tangles. Their distinct proteins and locations are essential to the comparison.","This reverses the characteristic plaque and tangle assignments.","Alpha-synuclein is associated with Lewy bodies, and glycogen is not tau.","M6P targeting and extracellular DNA do not describe the two classic Alzheimer lesions."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":21}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-6"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-16-8","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:2","question":"Neurofibrillary tangles in Alzheimer disease predominantly involve which protein?","options":["Transthyretin","Alpha-synuclein","Hyperphosphorylated tau","CFTR"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Distinguish intracellular tau tangles from extracellular amyloid-beta deposits. Both are associated with Alzheimer pathology.","reasons":["Transthyretin can form systemic or cardiac amyloid; it is not the principal tangle protein.","Alpha-synuclein is associated with Lewy bodies rather than the defining tau tangles.","Abnormally modified tau contributes to intracellular neurofibrillary tangles.","CFTR is a membrane chloride channel involved in cystic fibrosis."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":3}],"originalQuestion":"What are Alzheimer’s disease tangles made from? Ans: Tau protein","adaptationNote":"Supplied missing alternatives and specified the abnormal tau form.","sourceExcerpt":"What are Alzheimer’s disease tangles made from? Ans: Tau protein","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":3,"number":8,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-8"}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-6"}],"MOL-11:guide-7":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-16-9","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:2","question":"Lewy bodies in Parkinson disease are strongly associated with aggregation of which protein?","options":["Tau as the universal defining Parkinson aggregate","Amyloid-beta as the principal Lewy-body protein","Transthyretin","Alpha-synuclein"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Disease-associated aggregates involve characteristic proteins. Alpha-synuclein is central to Lewy-body pathology.","reasons":["Tau pathology occurs in other disorders and is not the characteristic Lewy-body component.","Amyloid-beta is associated with Alzheimer-type plaques.","Transthyretin amyloid is associated with a different disease group.","Misfolded alpha-synuclein is a major Lewy-body component."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":3}],"originalQuestion":"Parkinson disease is caused by the misfolding of? Ans: Alpha synuclein","adaptationNote":"Changed caused by to associated with because Parkinson disease is multifactorial; supplied complete alternatives.","sourceExcerpt":"Parkinson disease is caused by the misfolding of? Ans: Alpha synuclein","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-16","page":3,"number":9,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-16-source-9"}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-7"},{"id":"expanded-application-11-7","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-6","question":"A question describes Lewy bodies containing an aggregated neuronal protein. Which protein is the best match?","options":["Alpha-synuclein","Transthyretin","Insulin","Histone H1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alpha-synuclein is a major component of Lewy bodies and is central to the lecture’s Parkinson disease proteinopathy example. This must be distinguished from Aβ, tau and TTR aggregates.","reasons":["Alpha-synuclein is a major component of Lewy bodies and is central to the lecture’s Parkinson disease proteinopathy example. This must be distinguished from Aβ, tau and TTR aggregates.","TTR is a plasma transport protein involved in ATTR amyloid, not the defining Lewy-body precursor.","Insulin is a hormone and is not the named Lewy-body component in this lesson.","H1 is a linker histone rather than the neuronal aggregate protein described."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":23}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-7"}],"MOL-11:guide-8":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-14","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:2","question":"Wild-type transthyretin amyloid deposits are identified in an older patient’s heart. Which protein is forming these deposits?","options":["Alpha-synuclein","Immunoglobulin light chain","Amyloid-beta","Transthyretin"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both transthyretin and immunoglobulin light chains can cause cardiac amyloidosis. The subtype must be specified to give one unambiguous answer.","reasons":["This is associated with Lewy-body disorders.","Light chains can also cause cardiac amyloidosis, but define AL rather than ATTR amyloid.","This is associated with Alzheimer-type brain plaques rather than the stated ATTR subtype.","The stated ATTR subtype identifies transthyretin as the fibril precursor."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":6}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following proteins will cause cardiac amyloidosis? A. ɑ-synuclein. B. Accumulation of β- amyloid C. Transthyretin D. Immunoglobulin light chains E. Fibrinogen Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Added the ATTR/wild-type qualifier because the original cardiac-amyloidosis question had more than one plausible answer.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following proteins will cause cardiac amyloidosis? A. ɑ-synuclein. B. Accumulation of β- amyloid C. Transthyretin D. Immunoglobulin light chains E. Fibrinogen Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":6,"number":14,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-14"}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-8"},{"id":"expanded-application-11-8","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-7","question":"Which event is central to transthyretin amyloid formation in the lecture model?","options":["Tetramer dissociation permits monomer misfolding and aggregation","Conversion of all TTR into a DNA template","Removal of every normal TTR amino acid","Mandatory absence of all liver protein synthesis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"TTR normally forms a tetramer. Destabilization and dissociation can create monomers susceptible to misfolding and amyloid formation, whether due to a variant or wild-type age-associated disease.","reasons":["TTR normally forms a tetramer. Destabilization and dissociation can create monomers susceptible to misfolding and amyloid formation, whether due to a variant or wild-type age-associated disease.","The amyloid pathway is protein conformational change rather than reverse transcription of TTR.","Aggregation does not require removal of the whole sequence.","The process can occur despite continued hepatic production of TTR."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":24}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-8"}],"MOL-11:guide-9":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-97","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:2","question":"What conformational change is characteristic of pathogenic prion conversion?","options":["Direct removal of every peptide bond","An increase in beta-sheet-rich aggregation-prone structure","A required change in the protein’s DNA base sequence at every conversion","Conversion of every beta sheet to alpha helix"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The abnormal conformer can template conversion of normal host protein to a disease-associated conformation.","reasons":["Prion conversion changes folding rather than requiring complete proteolysis.","The abnormal conformer can template conversion of normal host protein to a disease-associated conformation.","Conformational templating can occur without changing the host sequence.","The characteristic pathogenic form is more beta-sheet rich."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":106}],"originalQuestion":"Which protein structure is affected in spongiform encephalopathies? A) Primary structure B) Secondary structure C) Tertiary structure D) Quaternary structure Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Replaced the broad which level is affected question with the defining conformational change.","sourceExcerpt":"Which protein structure is affected in spongiform encephalopathies? A) Primary structure B) Secondary structure C) Tertiary structure D) Quaternary structure Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-11:guide-9"},{"id":"expanded-application-11-9","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-8","question":"What makes prion propagation distinct from ordinary replication of a viral genome?","options":["A disease-associated conformer promotes misfolding of other protein molecules","Every new prion must copy a DNA genome inside itself","Prions are small ribosomes that translate themselves","Prions are always harmless if their sequence is normal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Prion propagation involves templated conformational conversion of host protein. Infectivity in this example concerns a self-propagating protein state rather than replication of a nucleic-acid genome contained in each prion.","reasons":["Prion propagation involves templated conformational conversion of host protein. Infectivity in this example concerns a self-propagating protein state rather than replication of a nucleic-acid genome contained in each prion.","A conventional packaged DNA genome is not the defining prion mechanism.","Prions are not autonomous ribosomes and cannot translate themselves.","A normal sequence can still adopt a harmful conformation."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":25}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-9"}],"MOL-11:guide-10":[{"id":"expanded-application-11-10","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:expanded-9","question":"Which comparison separates the molecular examples correctly?","options":["CFTR F508del: folding/trafficking loss; HbS: abnormal intermolecular polymerization","CFTR F508del: an extra tRNA; HbS: M6P deficiency","Both require identical amyloid precursor proteins","Both are caused only by loss of the 5′ mRNA cap"],"answer":0,"explanation":"F508del often reduces delivery of functional CFTR after misfolding, whereas HbS gains an abnormal deoxygenation-dependent interaction. The shared sequence–structure theme has different downstream mechanisms.","reasons":["F508del often reduces delivery of functional CFTR after misfolding, whereas HbS gains an abnormal deoxygenation-dependent interaction. The shared sequence–structure theme has different downstream mechanisms.","Neither an extra tRNA nor M6P deficiency describes these canonical examples.","CFTR and hemoglobin are different proteins with different pathological mechanisms.","The primary examples are coding changes rather than a shared cap-removal defect."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":27}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-10"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-31","lectureIds":["MOL-11"],"concept":"MOL-11:2","question":"A transmissible spongiform encephalopathy is associated with which protein-misfolding process?","options":["An abnormal prion conformer promotes conversion of normal host prion protein","All neurodegenerative diseases require amyloid-beta","Alpha-synuclein DNA aggregates into Lewy bodies","Tau phosphorylation directly changes the prion amino-acid sequence"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Prion disorders involve templated misfolding of host prion protein into an aggregation-prone conformation.","reasons":["Prions propagate a misfolded conformation without requiring their own nucleic-acid genome.","Different conditions involve different proteins; prion disease is not defined by amyloid-beta.","Lewy bodies contain alpha-synuclein protein, not alpha-synuclein DNA, and are associated with a different disease group.","Phosphorylation does not rewrite the protein sequence, and tau tangles are a different pathology."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":7}],"originalQuestion":"A 60-year-old anatomist starts experiencing rapidly progressive dementia after performing an autopsy. Which of the following best explains the condition? A) Normal prion proteins are misfolded by abnormal prion proteins B) Misfolding of alpha synuclein DNA and aggregation of lewy bodies C) Aggregation of Amyloid beta extracellularly D) Hyperphosphorylation of Tau proteins, forming tangles Answer: A","adaptationNote":"Replaced a clinical diagnosis assumption with a precise biochemical mechanism question.","sourceExcerpt":"A 60-year-old anatomist starts experiencing rapidly progressive dementia after performing an autopsy. Which of the following best explains the condition? A) Normal prion proteins are misfolded by abnormal prion proteins B) Misfolding of alpha synuclein DNA and aggregation of lewy bodies C) Aggregation of Amyloid beta extracellularly D) Hyperphosphorylation of Tau proteins, forming tangles Answer: A","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":7,"number":31,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-31"}],"topicId":"MOL-11:guide-10"}],"MOL-12:guide-1":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-1","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-0","question":"A nuclear enzyme is synthesized fully on free ribosomes and imported afterward. Is this compatible with normal protein targeting?","options":["Yes; many nuclear proteins follow a post-translational import route","No; every nuclear protein must enter rough ER first","No; free ribosomes make only extracellular proteins","Only if the protein is first converted into a lipid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Free ribosomes synthesize cytosolic and many nuclear, mitochondrial and peroxisomal proteins. Nuclear localization is then directed by appropriate signals and import machinery.","reasons":["Free ribosomes synthesize cytosolic and many nuclear, mitochondrial and peroxisomal proteins. Nuclear localization is then directed by appropriate signals and import machinery.","ER entry is not a required intermediate for the usual nuclear import pathway.","Free ribosomes are not restricted to extracellular cargo.","Proteins can be imported as proteins without being converted to lipids."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":4}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-1"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-101","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:1","question":"Which protein is least likely to need an ER-entry signal for its normal destination?","options":["A plasma-membrane receptor in the secretory pathway","A nuclear histone","A secreted extracellular matrix protein","A soluble lysosomal hydrolase"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Histones are synthesized on cytosolic ribosomes and imported through nuclear pores rather than the ER lumen.","reasons":["Many membrane receptors enter the ER membrane during synthesis.","Histones are synthesized on cytosolic ribosomes and imported through nuclear pores rather than the ER lumen.","Many secreted matrix proteins enter the ER.","Lysosomal hydrolases generally enter the ER before Golgi sorting."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":113}],"originalQuestion":"During translation, proteins destined for which organelle are unlikely to have a signal sequence that labels them for translocation into the lumen of the ER. A) Lysosome B) Plasma membrane C) Extracellular matrix D) Nucleus E) Secretory vesicles Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"During translation, proteins destined for which organelle are unlikely to have a signal sequence that labels them for translocation into the lumen of the ER. A) Lysosome B) Plasma membrane C) Extracellular matrix D) Nucleus E) Secretory vesicles Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-1"}],"MOL-12:guide-2":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-2","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-1","question":"Which sequence most closely describes entry of a typical soluble secretory protein into ER?","options":["Nascent signal recognized by SRP, docking at SRP receptor, transfer to translocon, resumed translation","Completed protein binds a nuclear pore, then becomes mRNA","M6P receptor first attaches amino acids to tRNA","A peroxisomal PTS1 is cleaved by DNA ligase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SRP recognizes an emerging ER signal and helps bring the translating complex to the ER receptor/translocon. Translation resumes as the chain enters the ER, and a cleavable signal may be removed by signal peptidase.","reasons":["SRP recognizes an emerging ER signal and helps bring the translating complex to the ER receptor/translocon. Translation resumes as the chain enters the ER, and a cleavable signal may be removed by signal peptidase.","Nuclear import is a different pathway and does not convert protein to mRNA.","M6P receptor recognizes a carbohydrate sorting tag rather than charging tRNA.","PTS1 is peroxisomal, and DNA ligase does not cleave protein signals."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":7}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-2"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-26","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:0","question":"Which sequence correctly describes SRP-mediated cotranslational targeting to rough ER?","options":["SRP binds before any translation → the protein is completed in cytosol → the ribosome then docks","The protein is fully glycosylated in cytosol → SRP enters nucleus → translation starts","Translation starts → SRP binds emerging signal and pauses elongation → docking at ER receptor → transfer to translocon and SRP release → translation resumes","The Golgi makes a signal peptide → DNA exits nucleus → a ribosome is assembled in ER lumen"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SRP recognizes an emerging ER signal, temporarily slows or pauses elongation, and docks the complex at ER for translocation as synthesis continues.","reasons":["The emerging signal is recognized during synthesis, before a typical secretory protein finishes folding in the cytosol.","Conventional N-linked glycosylation occurs in the ER lumen, not before ER targeting in cytosol.","The signal must emerge from a translating ribosome before SRP can recognize it.","Signals are encoded in the protein sequence and ribosomes remain on the cytosolic ER face."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":6},{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":10}],"originalQuestion":"Which order shows the process of cotranslational targeting of proteins in RER. I) Binding of SRP to signal sequence II) Translation begins III) Translation stops IV) SRP disassociates, GTP is hydrolysed V) SRP binds to the ER receptor Which of the following is the correct sequence? A) V-II-III-IV-I B) I-III-V-II-IV C) I-II-III-IV-V D) I-IV–III-II-V E) I-III-V-IV-II Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Corrected the source’s inconsistent order and replaced all sequence options with a complete unambiguous pathway.","sourceExcerpt":"Which order shows the process of cotranslational targeting of proteins in RER. I) Binding of SRP to signal sequence II) Translation begins III) Translation stops IV) SRP disassociates, GTP is hydrolysed V) SRP binds to the ER receptor Which of the following is the correct sequence? A) V-II-III-IV-I B) I-III-V-II-IV C) I-II-III-IV-V D) I-IV–III-II-V E) I-III-V-IV-II Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":10,"number":23,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-23"},{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":6,"number":26,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-26"}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-2"}],"MOL-12:guide-3":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-3","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-2","question":"An ER cargo fails quality control and is directed toward cytosolic proteasomal destruction. Which pathway fits?","options":["ER-associated degradation","Normal DNA semiconservative replication","Kinetoplastid RNA editing","Constitutive secretion of an accepted folded cargo"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ERAD links recognition of defective ER proteins with delivery to cytosolic degradation machinery. It prevents indiscriminate forward traffic of every newly synthesized chain.","reasons":["ERAD links recognition of defective ER proteins with delivery to cytosolic degradation machinery. It prevents indiscriminate forward traffic of every newly synthesized chain.","DNA replication does not select and degrade ER cargo.","RNA editing changes RNA sequence and is not a protein-disposal route.","The stem specifies failed quality control and destruction rather than accepted export cargo."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":9}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-3"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-12:guide-3-1","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-2","question":"A secretory protein is made but fails ER folding quality control. Which immediate trafficking response is appropriate to the taught pathway?","options":["Retention rather than indiscriminate onward export","Guaranteed immediate delivery to the plasma membrane despite misfolding","Automatic conversion into a DNA molecule","Removal of the need for all chaperone machinery"],"answer":0,"reasons":["ER quality control can retain unsuitable cargo and direct selected defective proteins toward ER-associated degradation.","Correct synthesis alone does not guarantee export when cargo fails conformational quality control.","The cargo remains protein; failed folding does not convert it into DNA.","Chaperones and quality control are relevant to the response rather than made unnecessary by failure."],"explanation":"ER quality control can retain unsuitable cargo and direct selected defective proteins toward ER-associated degradation. Correct folding helps cargo progress. ERAD connects ER quality control with cytosolic degradation.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":9}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-3"}],"MOL-12:guide-4":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-4","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-3","question":"A sugar is attached to the side-chain amide nitrogen of asparagine during ER processing. What modification is described?","options":["N-linked glycosylation","O-linked glycosylation of serine","Nonenzymatic hemoglobin glycation","Tyrosine phosphorylation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"N-linked glycosylation attaches a glycan to Asn through nitrogen, commonly in an Asn-X-Ser/Thr context. It is an enzyme-directed secretory-pathway modification.","reasons":["N-linked glycosylation attaches a glycan to Asn through nitrogen, commonly in an Asn-X-Ser/Thr context. It is an enzyme-directed secretory-pathway modification.","O-linked glycosylation uses an oxygen-containing group, commonly Ser/Thr hydroxyls.","HbA1c glycation is nonenzymatic and does not describe this defined Asn modification.","Phosphorylation attaches phosphate rather than a sugar chain."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":9}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-4"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-118","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:1","question":"Conventional O-linked glycans are commonly attached through which side-chain atoms?","options":["The amide nitrogen of asparagine as the definition of O-linked","The terminal phosphate of a DNA strand","A methionine thioether sulfur","The hydroxyl oxygens of serine or threonine"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The O in O-linked refers to the oxygen used in the glycosidic attachment.","reasons":["That describes conventional N-linked glycosylation.","This is not protein O-glycosylation.","That is not the common O-linked acceptor.","The O in O-linked refers to the oxygen used in the glycosidic attachment."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":124}],"originalQuestion":"O likened glysocylation to N- acetyl glucosamine and N- acetyl galactosamine is through? A) Serine and Threonine B) Asparagine and Glutamine Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Separated the merged protein-core/glycosylation source question into its recoverable glycosylation concept.","sourceExcerpt":"O likened glysocylation to N- acetyl glucosamine and N- acetyl galactosamine is through? A) Serine and Threonine B) Asparagine and Glutamine Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-4"}],"MOL-12:guide-5":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-5","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-4","question":"A soluble ER chaperone escapes to the Golgi. Which signal can support retrieval to the ER?","options":["C-terminal KDEL","A free anomeric carbon","A bacterial Shine–Dalgarno sequence","An mRNA stop codon"],"answer":0,"explanation":"KDEL is a retrieval signal for many soluble ER-resident proteins. Recognition after escape supports retrograde return rather than ordinary delivery to a final secretory destination.","reasons":["KDEL is a retrieval signal for many soluble ER-resident proteins. Recognition after escape supports retrograde return rather than ordinary delivery to a final secretory destination.","An anomeric carbon is a sugar structural feature, not the ER-resident retrieval sequence.","Shine–Dalgarno acts in bacterial translation initiation.","A stop codon terminates translation and is not a protein retrieval signal."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":9}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-5"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-39","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:1","question":"A soluble protein enters the ER lumen and has no retention signal or specialized destination signal. Which route is a common default?","options":["ER lumen → mitochondrial matrix by free diffusion","ER lumen → free cytosol through an always-open nuclear pore","ER lumen → DNA replication fork","ER → Golgi → secretion outside the cell"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Entering the ER places a protein in the secretory pathway. Other destinations or retention can require additional signals.","reasons":["Mitochondrial import uses distinct signals and machinery.","The ER lumen is topologically separate from cytosol; nuclear pores do not connect these compartments.","A soluble secreted protein does not normally become part of the DNA fork.","Soluble secretory cargo proceeds through the endomembrane system."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":15}],"originalQuestion":"Proteins that are packed in the lumen of the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) will most likely be transported to which of the following locations? A. Cytosol B. Peroxisome C. Extracellular environment D. Mitochondria E. Nucleus Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Added absence of retention/specialized sorting signals to make the destination unambiguous.","sourceExcerpt":"Proteins that are packed in the lumen of the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) will most likely be transported to which of the following locations? A. Cytosol B. Peroxisome C. Extracellular environment D. Mitochondria E. Nucleus Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":15,"number":39,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-39"}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-5"}],"MOL-12:guide-6":[{"id":"drill-MOL-12-2-0","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:2","question":"Which statement best explains lysosomal targeting?","options":["Mannose-6-phosphate helps target soluble lysosomal enzymes","SRP recognizes an ER signal sequence on a nascent protein","Many secreted proteins pass from rough ER to Golgi to secretory carriers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sorting receptors recognize the carbohydrate tag and route cargo toward endosomes and lysosomes. Clathrin can coat relevant transport carriers. A nuclear localization signal is a different address label.","reasons":["Correct. Sorting receptors recognize the carbohydrate tag and route cargo toward endosomes and lysosomes. Clathrin can coat relevant transport carriers. A nuclear localization signal is a different address label.","This explains ER targeting. SRP directs the translating ribosome to the ER translocation machinery. Without proper targeting, a secretory protein may be made but fail to enter the ER. SRP is not the enzyme that cleaves the signal peptide. For lysosomal targeting, use the rule: Mannose-6-phosphate helps target soluble lysosomal enzymes.","This explains Secretory pathway. The ER supports folding and early modifications; the Golgi modifies and sorts cargo. Nuclear and mitochondrial targeting use distinct signals. N-glycosylation does not mean every protein must be secreted. For lysosomal targeting, use the rule: Mannose-6-phosphate helps target soluble lysosomal enzymes."],"kind":"Concept check · authored study aid","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":113},{"source":"mol-past","page":114},{"source":"mol-past","page":115}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-6"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-12:guide-6-1","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-5","question":"A lysosomal hydrolase has a normal catalytic active site but lacks the sorting information needed for its destination. What can still fail?","options":["The hydrolase must lose all amino-acid side chains","Delivery to the lysosome despite preserved catalytic ability","Its chemical identity must immediately become a ribosomal RNA","Catalysis alone guarantees correct location in every case"],"answer":1,"reasons":["The sorting problem does not require destruction of all amino-acid side chains.","The topic separates cargo catalysis from the tagging and recognition needed for intracellular location.","A targeting defect does not transform protein into RNA.","Having catalytic activity does not guarantee correct sorting or delivery."],"explanation":"The topic separates cargo catalysis from the tagging and recognition needed for intracellular location. M6P sorts many soluble lysosomal hydrolases. Tagging and catalysis are separate properties of the enzyme cargo.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":10}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-6"}],"MOL-12:guide-7":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-7","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-6","question":"Many lysosomal hydrolases are secreted and accumulate outside cells while lysosomes store undegraded substrates. Which lesion best fits I-cell disease?","options":["Failure of the phosphotransferase machinery required for M6P tagging","Absence of sigma factor from bacterial polymerase","Failure of the ribosome to recognize any AUG","A mutation that affects only one unrelated GAG-degrading enzyme"],"answer":0,"explanation":"I-cell disease is a broad targeting problem: deficient M6P-tag generation misroutes many enzymes. Their extracellular abundance can rise while their lysosomal availability falls.","reasons":["I-cell disease is a broad targeting problem: deficient M6P-tag generation misroutes many enzymes. Their extracellular abundance can rise while their lysosomal availability falls.","Bacterial transcription initiation does not explain the human lysosomal sorting defect.","A universal translation-initiation failure would not specifically produce active secreted hydrolases.","A single catalytic deficiency is conceptually different from misdelivery of multiple lysosomal enzymes."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":12}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-7"},{"id":"expanded-application-12-6","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-5","question":"A lysosomal hydrolase is active but lacks its M6P tag. What is the most direct risk?","options":["It may be misrouted and fail to reach the lysosome efficiently","It must lose every peptide bond immediately","It becomes a transcription factor by default","Its DNA necessarily acquires a frameshift"],"answer":0,"explanation":"M6P provides sorting information for many soluble lysosomal hydrolases. A correctly synthesized catalytic protein can still fail its cellular role if the location signal is absent.","reasons":["M6P provides sorting information for many soluble lysosomal hydrolases. A correctly synthesized catalytic protein can still fail its cellular role if the location signal is absent.","Missing a carbohydrate tag does not inherently hydrolyze the protein backbone.","Loss of lysosomal targeting does not create a nuclear DNA-binding domain.","A post-translational tagging defect need not alter the DNA reading frame."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":10}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-7"}],"MOL-12:guide-8":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-110","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:1","question":"Which order correctly describes classical importin-mediated nuclear protein import?","options":["Cargo first enters ER lumen, then crosses the nuclear pore from the lumen","NLS binds importin → complex traverses nuclear pore → nuclear Ran-GTP promotes cargo release → importin recycles","Ran-GDP permanently binds cargo in the nucleus and prevents release","The NLS must be converted to a peptide hormone before entry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Ran nucleotide-state gradient provides directionality to the import cycle.","reasons":["Classical nuclear import begins in cytosol and crosses the nuclear-pore channel.","The Ran nucleotide-state gradient provides directionality to the import cycle.","Nuclear Ran-GTP, not an irreversible Ran-GDP lock, promotes cargo release from importin.","An NLS is a protein sequence signal, not a required hormone precursor."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":121}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following correctly describes the order of nuclear import of a protein? A) Ran-GDP binds NLS → Importin binds Ran-GTP → Protein enters nucleus B) Importin binds protein → Protein diffuses through nuclear pore → Ran-GTP hydrolysis releases protein C) NLS binds importin → Importin–cargo complex enters nuclear pore → Ran-GTP binds importin → Cargo released → Importin exits nucleus → Ran-GTP hydrolyzed to Ran-GDP D) Protein binds Ran-GTP in cytoplasm → Importin releases protein in nucleus E) NLS is cleaved to allow nuclear entry Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following correctly describes the order of nuclear import of a protein? A) Ran-GDP binds NLS → Importin binds Ran-GTP → Protein enters nucleus B) Importin binds protein → Protein diffuses through nuclear pore → Ran-GTP hydrolysis releases protein C) NLS binds importin → Importin–cargo complex enters nuclear pore → Ran-GTP binds importin → Cargo released → Importin exits nucleus → Ran-GTP hydrolyzed to Ran-GDP D) Protein binds Ran-GTP in cytoplasm → Importin releases protein in nucleus E) NLS is cleaved to allow nuclear entry Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-8"},{"id":"expanded-application-12-8","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-7","question":"Which property distinguishes a typical nuclear localization signal from a cleavable ER signal peptide?","options":["An NLS is often basic and is generally retained after import","An NLS must be a long hydrophobic N-terminal segment cleaved by SRP","An NLS is a carbohydrate phosphate tag","An NLS always requires unfolding through TOM/TIM"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Typical NLS sequences are rich in basic residues and are recognized by import receptors. They generally remain in the imported protein, unlike a usual cleavable signal peptide on soluble ER cargo.","reasons":["Typical NLS sequences are rich in basic residues and are recognized by import receptors. They generally remain in the imported protein, unlike a usual cleavable signal peptide on soluble ER cargo.","SRP recognizes an ER signal but is not the signal-cleaving protease; this is not the typical NLS description.","A carbohydrate phosphate tag describes M6P, not an amino-acid NLS.","TOM/TIM belong to mitochondrial import, not nuclear pores."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":13}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-8"}],"MOL-12:guide-9":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-9","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-8","question":"A nuclear-encoded mitochondrial matrix precursor is imported through which major membrane machinery?","options":["TOM and TIM complexes","The spliceosome and U1 snRNP","The M6P receptor and clathrin alone","A bacterial 70S ribosome pore"],"answer":0,"explanation":"TOM and TIM participate in import across mitochondrial outer and inner membranes. Appropriate targeting information, chaperones, energy and membrane potential support import and subsequent folding.","reasons":["TOM and TIM participate in import across mitochondrial outer and inner membranes. Appropriate targeting information, chaperones, energy and membrane potential support import and subsequent folding.","The spliceosome processes nuclear pre-mRNA rather than transporting proteins through mitochondrial membranes.","M6P/clathrin sorting delivers lysosomal cargo along a different pathway.","A ribosome is not the mitochondrial translocase channel."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":14}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-9"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-103","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:1","question":"Why can cytosolic HSP70 assist import of a newly made protein into the mitochondrial matrix?","options":["It can help maintain an import-competent unfolded state","It converts the protein into a lipid vesicle","It adds a5′ cap to the protein","It replaces all import channels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Most nuclear-encoded matrix proteins must remain sufficiently unfolded for passage through translocases.","reasons":["Most nuclear-encoded matrix proteins must remain sufficiently unfolded for passage through translocases.","Mitochondrial protein import is not simple vesicular entry from the Golgi.","Capping modifies RNA, not a protein import substrate.","Chaperones cooperate with targeting and translocation machinery rather than abolish the need for it."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":114}],"originalQuestion":"Which protein binds to mitochondrial-targeted proteins to prevent them from folding? A) TIM 23 B) TIM 44 C) TOM 40 D) MPP E) HSP 70 Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Which protein binds to mitochondrial-targeted proteins to prevent them from folding? A) TIM 23 B) TIM 44 C) TOM 40 D) MPP E) HSP 70 Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-9"}],"MOL-12:guide-10":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-10","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-9","question":"A cargo loses a necessary C-terminal Ser-Lys-Leu signal. Which destination is most directly affected in the classic example?","options":["Peroxisome","Nucleus through an NLS","ER through SRP recognition of an N-terminal signal","Bacterial nucleoid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SKL is the common PTS1 example recognized by peroxisomal targeting machinery. It differs in sequence position and recognition from nuclear, ER and mitochondrial signals.","reasons":["SKL is the common PTS1 example recognized by peroxisomal targeting machinery. It differs in sequence position and recognition from nuclear, ER and mitochondrial signals.","An NLS uses a different basic sequence pattern and import route.","ER targeting typically recognizes an emerging hydrophobic segment rather than the C-terminal PTS1 example.","Bacterial nucleoids are not the destination of the eukaryotic peroxisomal targeting route."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":16}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-10"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-12:guide-10-1","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-9","question":"A defect affects peroxisome biogenesis machinery rather than the signal on one cargo. Which outcome is most consistent?","options":["The defect cannot influence organelle-related metabolism","Import or assembly involving many peroxisomal proteins can be disturbed","Only one mitochondrial TOM complex must be involved","Every protein is automatically secreted normally"],"answer":1,"reasons":["The taught consequences include disturbed very-long-chain fatty-acid handling and plasmalogen synthesis.","Biogenesis machinery can affect delivery of many proteins, as illustrated by Zellweger spectrum disorders.","TOM/TIM are mitochondrial import machinery, distinct from the peroxisomal route described.","A peroxisome-biogenesis defect is not a guarantee of normal secretion of every protein."],"explanation":"Biogenesis machinery can affect delivery of many proteins, as illustrated by Zellweger spectrum disorders. PTS1 is commonly C-terminal. Peroxisomal import is distinct from TOM/TIM mitochondrial import.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":16}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-10"}],"MOL-12:guide-11":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-11","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-10","question":"A beta cell stores insulin in granules and releases it after stimulation. Which secretion pattern is illustrated?","options":["Regulated secretion","Constitutive secretion with no storage requirement","Passive diffusion of all cytosolic proteins","Bacterial polycistronic transcription"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Regulated secretion stores cargo for stimulus-triggered release. This permits rapid release from pre-existing granules, unlike a purely continuous no-storage model.","reasons":["Regulated secretion stores cargo for stimulus-triggered release. This permits rapid release from pre-existing granules, unlike a purely continuous no-storage model.","Constitutive secretion does not require the same stimulus-dependent granule storage.","A protein hormone does not simply cross the membrane by unrestricted cytosolic diffusion.","Polycistronic transcription concerns RNA organization rather than granule exocytosis."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":18}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-11"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-12:guide-11-1","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-10","question":"A cell continuously exports a protein without first storing it in stimulus-responsive granules. Which pattern is described?","options":["Constitutive secretion","Regulated secretion requiring prior granule storage and stimulation","A default route for every cytosolic protein lacking an ER signal","Nuclear import through a pore"],"answer":0,"reasons":["Constitutive secretion sends cargo outward without obligatory storage in stimulus-responsive granules.","The stem omits the defining regulated storage-and-release pattern.","Many proteins without ER-entry targeting remain cytosolic; they do not all default to secretion.","Nuclear import is a different destination and trafficking mechanism."],"explanation":"Constitutive secretion sends cargo outward without obligatory storage in stimulus-responsive granules. Regulated cargo is stored before signal-triggered release. Cytosolic residence is not the same as constitutive secretion.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":18}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-11"}],"MOL-12:guide-12":[{"id":"expanded-application-12-12","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-11","question":"The common HFE trafficking example concerns defective association with which partner?","options":["Beta-2 microglobulin","Beta-2 macroglobulin as printed in the erroneous wording","DNA ligase","A bacterial sigma factor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HFE associates with beta-2 microglobulin. The C282Y example impairs normal processing and cell-surface expression; the lecture’s “macroglobulin” wording is corrected using the original HFE trafficking literature.","reasons":["HFE associates with beta-2 microglobulin. The C282Y example impairs normal processing and cell-surface expression; the lecture’s “macroglobulin” wording is corrected using the original HFE trafficking literature.","This reproduces the source wording error rather than the correct HFE partner.","DNA ligase repairs nucleic-acid backbone links and is not the HFE assembly partner.","Sigma factor participates in bacterial transcription rather than HFE trafficking."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":22}],"topicId":"MOL-12:guide-12"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-12:guide-12-1","lectureIds":["MOL-12"],"concept":"MOL-12:expanded-11","question":"A misfolded secreted protein accumulates inside its source cell and is deficient at its usual destination. Which conclusion is supported?","options":["Intracellular accumulation proves that secretion to the destination is increased","A protein can cause disease only if its DNA is entirely absent","Retention necessarily improves delivery of the same protein to distant tissues","Toxic accumulation at the source and loss of protection at the destination can coexist"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Retention inside the source cell does not establish increased delivery outside it.","Trafficking and folding defects can cause disease despite an expressed gene.","Retention can reduce secretion rather than improve it.","The alpha-1 antitrypsin example illustrates both source-cell accumulation and reduced distal antiprotease availability."],"explanation":"The alpha-1 antitrypsin example illustrates both source-cell accumulation and reduced distal antiprotease availability. A retained protein can cause toxicity at its source and deficiency at its destination. HFE’s partner is beta-2 microglobulin.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":19}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-12:guide-12"}],"MOL-13:guide-1":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-1","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-0","question":"Which classification correctly compares glucose and fructose in their open-chain forms?","options":["Glucose is an aldose and fructose is a ketose","Both must be amino acids because they have six carbons","Glucose has no hydroxyl groups","Fructose is a nucleotide because it forms a ring"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Glucose and fructose are hexose sugars with different carbonyl arrangements: an aldehyde-derived group for glucose and a ketone-derived group for fructose. Carbon count and carbonyl type are separate classification axes.","reasons":["Glucose and fructose are hexose sugars with different carbonyl arrangements: an aldehyde-derived group for glucose and a ketone-derived group for fructose. Carbon count and carbonyl type are separate classification axes.","Six carbons do not make a molecule an amino acid.","Glucose contains several hydroxyl groups that contribute to polarity.","Ring formation alone does not supply a nucleobase and phosphate required for a nucleotide."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":5}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-1"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-13:guide-1-1","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-0","question":"Which statement captures a carbohydrate role beyond immediate energy supply?","options":["Every carbohydrate is a single glucose molecule","The same carbon count forces glucose and fructose to have identical connectivity","Carbohydrate structures can participate in cell-surface recognition","Carbohydrates can never contribute to structural materials"],"answer":2,"reasons":["Oligosaccharides and polysaccharides contain linked sugar units rather than necessarily one glucose.","Glucose and fructose illustrate different carbonyl/connectivity chemistry despite the same molecular formula.","The taught functions include recognition, fuel storage and structural roles as well as energy supply.","Carbohydrates contribute to structural materials and extracellular organization."],"explanation":"The taught functions include recognition, fuel storage and structural roles as well as energy supply. Carbon number and aldose/ketose classification are independent features. Carbohydrate functions extend beyond ATP production.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":2}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-1"}],"MOL-13:guide-2":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-2","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-1","question":"When open-chain glucose forms a cyclic hemiacetal, which carbon becomes the anomeric center?","options":["The former carbonyl carbon, C1","The final carbon, C6, regardless of structure","Every carbon simultaneously","Only the carbon of an attached phosphate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The anomeric carbon derives from the carbonyl carbon. For glucose it is C1, while a ketose such as fructose has a different anomeric position.","reasons":["The anomeric carbon derives from the carbonyl carbon. For glucose it is C1, while a ketose such as fructose has a different anomeric position.","C6 does not originate from the glucose carbonyl group.","Cyclization creates a specific new stereochemical center rather than making every carbon anomeric.","A phosphate is not required for glucose ring formation and does not define the anomeric carbon."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":4}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-2"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-130","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:2","question":"The carbonyl carbon of an open-chain sugar becomes what when it forms a cyclic hemiacetal or hemiketal?","options":["A peptide carbonyl","Always the terminal nonchiral carbon","The anomeric carbon","A sulfate group"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ring closure makes a new anomeric center whose configuration distinguishes alpha and beta anomers.","reasons":["Cyclization is carbohydrate chemistry, not peptide synthesis.","The carbonyl carbon becomes the anomeric center; its original location differs in aldoses and ketoses.","Ring closure makes a new anomeric center whose configuration distinguishes alpha and beta anomers.","No sulfate is created by ordinary sugar ring closure."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":134}],"originalQuestion":"The aldehyde and ketone moieties of carbohydrates with 5 and 6 carbons will spontaneously react with neighboring alcohol molecules to produce ring structures. The rings can open and close allowing for different configurations of the atoms of the rings. Which of the following represents the carbon about which this rotation occurs? A) Glycosidic B) Terminal C) Hydroxy D) Anomeric E) Chiral Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Corrected the source’s rotation wording: ring opening/closure allows anomer interconversion.","sourceExcerpt":"The aldehyde and ketone moieties of carbohydrates with 5 and 6 carbons will spontaneously react with neighboring alcohol molecules to produce ring structures. The rings can open and close allowing for different configurations of the atoms of the rings. Which of the following represents the carbon about which this rotation occurs? A) Glycosidic B) Terminal C) Hydroxy D) Anomeric E) Chiral Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-2"}],"MOL-13:guide-3":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-3","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-2","question":"D-glucose and D-galactose differ in configuration at C4. How are they related?","options":["C4 epimers","Mirror-image D/L enantiomers","Alpha and beta anomers differing only at C1","Different polymers with no shared molecular formula"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Epimers are stereoisomers differing at one stereogenic center. The specified C4 difference identifies the glucose/galactose relationship and differs from anomerism at the anomeric carbon.","reasons":["Epimers are stereoisomers differing at one stereogenic center. The specified C4 difference identifies the glucose/galactose relationship and differs from anomerism at the anomeric carbon.","They are both D sugars and are not full mirror-image enantiomers.","The difference stated is C4, not the anomeric C1.","Both are monosaccharide hexoses rather than unrelated polymers."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":5}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-3"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-128","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:2","question":"What are enantiomers?","options":["Molecules with identical three-dimensional configuration","Molecules that differ only in total electrical charge","Molecules that must have no asymmetric centers","Non-superimposable mirror-image stereoisomers"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Enantiomers have mirror-related configurations and can interact differently with chiral biological systems.","reasons":["Identical configurations are the same stereoisomer.","Charge difference is not the definition of mirror-image stereoisomerism.","Many enantiomer pairs arise from chiral centers; chirality is the key property.","Enantiomers have mirror-related configurations and can interact differently with chiral biological systems."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":133}],"originalQuestion":"Thalidomide and L-dopa are examples of pharmaceutical drugs that occur as enantiomers, or molecules that: A) Have identical three-dimensional shapes B) Are mirror images of one another C) Lack a asymmetric carbon D) Differ in location of their double bonds E) Differ in electrical charge Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Used the stereochemistry definition rather than a drug-safety inference.","sourceExcerpt":"Thalidomide and L-dopa are examples of pharmaceutical drugs that occur as enantiomers, or molecules that: A) Have identical three-dimensional shapes B) Are mirror images of one another C) Lack a asymmetric carbon D) Differ in location of their double bonds E) Differ in electrical charge Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-3"}],"MOL-13:guide-4":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-2-24","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:2","question":"Which disaccharide is nonreducing because both anomeric carbons participate in its glycosidic bond?","options":["Maltose","Cellobiose","Sucrose","Lactose"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A free anomeric carbon can equilibrate with an open-chain form and support reducing-sugar behavior.","reasons":["Maltose has a free anomeric carbon and is reducing.","Cellobiose also has a free anomeric carbon.","Its glucose and fructose anomeric carbons are linked, leaving no free reducing anomeric group.","Lactose retains a free anomeric carbon and is a reducing sugar."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":10}],"originalQuestion":"Which of the following is not a reducing sugar? A. Glucose B. Fructose C. Sucrose D. Erythrose Answer: C","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Which of the following is not a reducing sugar? A. Glucose B. Fructose C. Sucrose D. Erythrose Answer: C","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-2","page":10,"number":24,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-2-source-24"}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-4"},{"id":"expanded-application-13-4","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-3","question":"Why is sucrose nonreducing in the usual comparison with lactose?","options":["Its glycosidic link involves both relevant anomeric centers","It contains no oxygen atoms","It is a protein rather than a carbohydrate","It has no covalent bonds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sucrose locks both the glucose and fructose anomeric centers into its glycosidic linkage, preventing the ordinary free reducing-end behavior. Lactose retains a free anomeric center.","reasons":["Sucrose locks both the glucose and fructose anomeric centers into its glycosidic linkage, preventing the ordinary free reducing-end behavior. Lactose retains a free anomeric center.","Sucrose contains many oxygen atoms; lack of oxygen is not the explanation.","Sucrose is a disaccharide, not a protein.","Its sugar units and linkage are covalent structures."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":7}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-4"}],"MOL-13:guide-5":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-5","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-4","question":"Which statement best distinguishes HbA1c formation from ordinary N-linked glycosylation?","options":["HbA1c involves nonenzymatic glycation, while N-linked glycosylation is enzyme-directed","Both are identical attachment of M6P to every protein","HbA1c requires a bacterial ribosome","Glycation always removes all sugars from hemoglobin"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HbA1c forms through nonenzymatic glucose-related glycation of hemoglobin and integrates glycemic exposure over time. N-linked glycosylation adds defined glycans enzymatically to selected Asn residues.","reasons":["HbA1c forms through nonenzymatic glucose-related glycation of hemoglobin and integrates glycemic exposure over time. N-linked glycosylation adds defined glycans enzymatically to selected Asn residues.","M6P sorting is a specific glycan modification, not a synonym for either process in all proteins.","Glycation of circulating hemoglobin does not require bacterial translation machinery.","Glycation adds a glucose-derived modification rather than removing every sugar."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":7}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-5"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-21","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:2","question":"Which glycated protein is routinely measured to estimate average blood glucose exposure over recent months?","options":["Any glycoprotein without specifying one","Collagen alone in a routine HbA1c assay","Ferritin","Hemoglobin A1c"],"answer":3,"explanation":"HbA1c reflects nonenzymatic glycation of hemoglobin. Interpretation can be affected by conditions that alter red cell turnover.","reasons":["Enzymatic glycosylation and nonenzymatic glycation are different processes; the clinical marker is specific.","The HbA1c assay measures glycated hemoglobin rather than collagen.","Ferritin mainly reflects iron storage; it is not the standard glycated marker of average glucose.","Nonenzymatic glycation of hemoglobin accumulates over the red cell lifespan."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":5}],"originalQuestion":"Patient with untreated diabetes, which glycated protein would be used to measure the amount of time glucose has spent in the blood A) Hemoglobin B) Glycoproteins C) Glycans D) Ferritin Answer: َ","adaptationNote":"Supplied the missing answer key and clarified glycation versus enzymatic glycosylation.","sourceExcerpt":"Patient with untreated diabetes, which glycated protein would be used to measure the amount of time glucose has spent in the blood A) Hemoglobin B) Glycoproteins C) Glycans D) Ferritin Answer: َ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":5,"number":21,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-21"}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-5"}],"MOL-13:guide-6":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-6","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-5","question":"A lectin recognizes a particular sugar pattern on a cell-surface glycoprotein. Which role of carbohydrate is illustrated?","options":["Molecular recognition and targeting information","Only long-term energy storage","Amino-acid decoding at the ribosome","DNA proofreading"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cell-surface glycans can be recognized selectively by lectins. Sugar identity, linkage and branching can provide information for adhesion, targeting or pathogen attachment beyond serving as metabolic fuel.","reasons":["Cell-surface glycans can be recognized selectively by lectins. Sugar identity, linkage and branching can provide information for adhesion, targeting or pathogen attachment beyond serving as metabolic fuel.","Carbohydrate functions extend beyond storage, and the stem specifically describes recognition.","Codon decoding involves mRNA/tRNA interactions rather than a surface lectin.","Proofreading concerns nucleotide-copying fidelity, not glycan recognition."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":3}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-6"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-exam-1-36","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:0","question":"Some viruses attach to glycans or glycoproteins on airway epithelial cells. What role do these surface structures play?","options":["A guarantee that every virus infects every cell","Intracellular ribosomes exposed on the outer membrane","A replacement for all viral entry processes","Specific recognition and attachment sites"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Surface glycans and glycoproteins can be recognized by pathogens. The exact receptor and entry mechanism depend on the virus.","reasons":["Receptor availability contributes to selectivity, not universal infection.","Ribosomes are intracellular and do not form the described surface glycan receptor.","Attachment is an early step; fusion, uptake and other events may still be required.","Cell-surface glycans can contribute to receptor recognition and tropism."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":8}],"originalQuestion":"A patient is exposed to an unfamiliar virus through a respiratory droplet. The virus approaches the surface of the patient’s airway epithelial cells and must locate a specific structure on the cell membrane before entry can occur. Which mechanism allows the virus to infect the host cell? A) By binding directly to intracellular ribosomes B) By attaching to specific membrane glycoproteins on the host cell surface C) By passively diffusing through the phospholipid bilayer D) By mechanically rupturing the host membrane to enter Answer: B","adaptationNote":"Qualified the original universal wording: not every virus uses the same receptor class.","sourceExcerpt":"A patient is exposed to an unfamiliar virus through a respiratory droplet. The virus approaches the surface of the patient’s airway epithelial cells and must locate a specific structure on the cell membrane before entry can occur. Which mechanism allows the virus to infect the host cell? A) By binding directly to intracellular ribosomes B) By attaching to specific membrane glycoproteins on the host cell surface C) By passively diffusing through the phospholipid bilayer D) By mechanically rupturing the host membrane to enter Answer: B","originalQuestionLabel":"Original recalled question","sourceOccurrences":[{"source":"mol-exam-1","page":8,"number":36,"sourceQuestionId":"mol-exam-1-source-36"}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-6"}],"MOL-13:guide-7":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-7","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-6","question":"Which polymer has alpha(1→4) chains and more frequent alpha(1→6) branches than amylopectin?","options":["Glycogen","Amylose","An unbranched GAG repeat","A DNA double helix"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Glycogen is a highly branched animal glucose-storage polymer. Amylopectin is branched plant starch, while amylose is mainly linear alpha(1→4) glucose.","reasons":["Glycogen is a highly branched animal glucose-storage polymer. Amylopectin is branched plant starch, while amylose is mainly linear alpha(1→4) glucose.","Amylose lacks the frequent alpha(1→6) branch pattern specified.","GAGs are generally unbranched heteropolysaccharides and are not this glucose-storage structure.","DNA uses nucleotide phosphodiester bonds rather than glucose glycosidic branching."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":9}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-7"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-midterm-amylose","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:2","question":"How does amylose differ from amylopectin?","options":["Amylopectin is made from fructose only","Amylose contains only beta(1→4) bonds like cellulose","Amylose is mostly unbranched alpha(1→4)-linked glucose, while amylopectin also has alpha(1→6) branches","Amylose is a protein with no peptide branches"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Branching and linkage orientation change polysaccharide structure and digestion properties.","reasons":["Amylopectin is a branched glucose polymer; it is not made exclusively from fructose.","Amylose has alpha(1→4) linkages, whereas cellulose has beta(1→4) linkages.","Both are starch components, but their branching patterns differ.","Amylose is a carbohydrate polymer, not a protein."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-midterm","page":43}],"originalQuestion":"Amylose differs from amylopectin in that amylose… Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Corrected the source phrase no branch proteins and stated the glycosidic bonds.","sourceExcerpt":"Amylose differs from amylopectin in that amylose… Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-7"}],"MOL-13:guide-8":[{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-136","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:2","question":"How can soluble, viscous dietary fiber influence the rise in glucose after a meal?","options":["Every nondigestible substance necessarily speeds absorption","It converts glucose into insulin in the intestine","It makes all carbohydrate absorption impossible","It can slow carbohydrate digestion and absorption"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Viscosity and altered intestinal transit/mixing can reduce the speed of glucose appearance; effects depend on fiber and meal.","reasons":["Fiber types have different effects, and this is opposite the typical viscous-fiber effect.","Insulin is a peptide hormone, not a glucose conversion product.","Fiber can slow absorption without abolishing it.","Viscosity and altered intestinal transit/mixing can reduce the speed of glucose appearance; effects depend on fiber and meal."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":138}],"originalQuestion":"How can eating a non-digestible diet affect the absorption of carbohydrates A) Slows the absorption of carbohydrates B) Increase the absorption of carbohydrates Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Replaced non-digestible diet with soluble viscous fiber and qualified the magnitude.","sourceExcerpt":"How can eating a non-digestible diet affect the absorption of carbohydrates A) Slows the absorption of carbohydrates B) Increase the absorption of carbohydrates Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-8"},{"id":"expanded-application-13-8","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-7","question":"Two meals contain similar carbohydrate amounts but have different starch organization and food matrices. Which interpretation is most defensible?","options":["Digestion and absorption rates may differ despite equal carbohydrate mass","Equal mass guarantees an identical glucose curve in every person","Any branch point proves the food causes diabetes","Dietary structure can never affect enzyme access"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Enzyme access, linkage, physical organization and meal context influence digestion. Equal carbohydrate mass therefore does not determine an identical physiological time course, and simple structural comparisons should not become universal disease claims.","reasons":["Enzyme access, linkage, physical organization and meal context influence digestion. Equal carbohydrate mass therefore does not determine an identical physiological time course, and simple structural comparisons should not become universal disease claims.","This ignores structure, processing, meal components and individual physiology.","A molecular branch does not by itself establish a clinical diagnosis or inevitable outcome.","Substrate organization is one factor affecting digestive access."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":8}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-8"}],"MOL-13:guide-9":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-9","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-8","question":"Which GAG exception is correctly stated?","options":["Keratan sulfate contains galactose rather than a uronic acid in its repeat","Every GAG is completely unsulfated","Hyaluronan must contain more sulfate than heparin","All GAGs are branched glycogen molecules"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Keratan sulfate uses galactose and an amino sugar rather than the common uronic-acid pattern. Hyaluronan is another important exception because it lacks sulfate groups.","reasons":["Keratan sulfate uses galactose and an amino sugar rather than the common uronic-acid pattern. Hyaluronan is another important exception because it lacks sulfate groups.","Many GAGs are sulfated; hyaluronan is the major unsulfated example.","Hyaluronan is unsulfated, so it cannot exceed highly sulfated heparin in sulfate content.","GAGs are generally unbranched heteropolysaccharides, unlike branched glycogen."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":15}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-9"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-133","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:0","question":"Why are many GAGs highly negatively charged?","options":["Every sugar carbon has a positive charge","They contain many lysine side chains","They contain carboxylate and often sulfate groups","Only their peptide bonds are negatively charged"],"answer":2,"explanation":"These ionized groups attract counterions and water and contribute to matrix hydration.","reasons":["Ordinary GAG carbons are not uniformly positively charged.","GAGs are carbohydrates, not lysine-rich polypeptides.","These ionized groups attract counterions and water and contribute to matrix hydration.","GAG chains do not have peptide backbones."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":135}],"originalQuestion":"Sulphate groups contribute to the negative charge commonly associated with: A) Glycosaminoglycans B) Proteoglycans C) Glycoproteins D) N-acetylgalactosamines Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Sulphate groups contribute to the negative charge commonly associated with: A) Glycosaminoglycans B) Proteoglycans C) Glycoproteins D) N-acetylgalactosamines Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-9"}],"MOL-13:guide-10":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-10","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-9","question":"Why do GAG-rich matrices resist compression and retain water?","options":["Negative groups promote hydration and expanded water-rich structures","They are entirely nonpolar and exclude all water","Every GAG is a rigid mineral crystal","Their positive DNA bases consume every water molecule"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Carboxylate and sulfate groups contribute negative charge, counterion interactions and hydration. The resulting expanded water-rich matrix supports resilience under compression and re-expansion when pressure is removed.","reasons":["Carboxylate and sulfate groups contribute negative charge, counterion interactions and hydration. The resulting expanded water-rich matrix supports resilience under compression and re-expansion when pressure is removed.","GAGs are highly hydrophilic rather than completely nonpolar.","Their mechanical role does not require a mineral-crystal structure.","GAGs are carbohydrates, not DNA polymers, and they retain rather than consume all water."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":14}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-10"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-125","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:0","question":"How do GAGs help synovial fluid and cartilage resist compression?","options":["Their fixed negative charges attract counterions and water","They exclude all water from the joint","They must be rapidly destroyed before any lubrication occurs","They consist of hydrophobic amino acids"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hydrated GAG-rich matrices generate swelling pressure and support lubrication and shock absorption.","reasons":["Hydrated GAG-rich matrices generate swelling pressure and support lubrication and shock absorption.","Their function depends strongly on hydration.","Matrix hydration, not obligatory rapid destruction, explains the mechanical role.","GAGs are carbohydrate polymers, not polypeptides."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":130}],"originalQuestion":"Why can glycosaminoglycans provide lubrication at synovial joints? A) Because they are highly negative charges B) Because they are rich in hydrophobic amino acids C) Because they form covalent cross-links with collagen fibers D) Because they are degraded rapidly by synovial enzymes E) Because they contain aromatic side chains that reduce friction Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Why can glycosaminoglycans provide lubrication at synovial joints? A) Because they are highly negative charges B) Because they are rich in hydrophobic amino acids C) Because they form covalent cross-links with collagen fibers D) Because they are degraded rapidly by synovial enzymes E) Because they contain aromatic side chains that reduce friction Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-10"}],"MOL-13:guide-11":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-11","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-10","question":"Which pairing is correct?","options":["Hyaluronan: unsulfated matrix/lubrication role; heparin: anticoagulant role","Hyaluronan: a highly sulfated protein; heparin: a DNA polymerase","Both are identical to glycogen","Heparin: the universal protein core of all proteoglycans"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hyaluronan is a large unsulfated GAG important in hydrated matrices, whereas heparin is a highly sulfated GAG with anticoagulant activity. Their shared family does not make their functions identical.","reasons":["Hyaluronan is a large unsulfated GAG important in hydrated matrices, whereas heparin is a highly sulfated GAG with anticoagulant activity. Their shared family does not make their functions identical.","Hyaluronan is not a sulfated protein and heparin is not an enzyme.","Glycogen is a glucose-storage polymer with different composition and architecture.","Heparin is a GAG chain rather than the universal protein component of proteoglycans."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":16}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-11"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-13:guide-11-1","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-10","question":"Which comparison correctly avoids treating heparin and heparan sulfate as interchangeable?","options":["Heparin has a prominent anticoagulant role; heparan sulfate has broad cell-surface and ECM signaling roles","Heparin and heparan sulfate are merely two names for an identical molecule","Both are nonsulfated forms of hyaluronan","Neither belongs to the glycosaminoglycan family"],"answer":0,"reasons":["The related GAGs differ in sulfation patterns and common biological roles as described in the topic.","Related names do not establish identical structure or interchangeable function.","Heparin is highly sulfated, whereas hyaluronan is the nonsulfated example.","Both belong to the GAG family despite differences in structure and function."],"explanation":"The related GAGs differ in sulfation patterns and common biological roles as described in the topic. Hyaluronan: unsulfated hydration/lubrication example. Heparin: anticoagulant example.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":16}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-11"}],"MOL-13:guide-12":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-12","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-11","question":"Which description defines a proteoglycan most directly?","options":["A core protein bearing covalently attached GAG chains","A lipid containing no carbohydrate","A free monosaccharide with no polymer","A nucleosome made only of histones"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Proteoglycans contain a protein core and GAG chains. This distinguishes them from glycolipids, small free sugars and nucleic-acid packaging complexes.","reasons":["Proteoglycans contain a protein core and GAG chains. This distinguishes them from glycolipids, small free sugars and nucleic-acid packaging complexes.","Glycolipids link carbohydrate to lipid, whereas the described proteoglycan carrier is protein.","A proteoglycan is a macromolecular assembly, not just one unlinked sugar.","Nucleosomes package DNA with histones and are not protein–GAG structures."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":19}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-12"},{"id":"topic-check-MOL-13:guide-12-1","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-11","question":"How does a typical glycoprotein differ from a proteoglycan in the comparison taught here?","options":["A glycoprotein must contain carbohydrate attached only to lipid","A proteoglycan contains no core protein","Hyaluronan must be covalently attached to every proteoglycan core","A glycoprotein commonly has shorter, often branched oligosaccharides rather than long GAG chains on a core protein"],"answer":3,"reasons":["Sugar attached to lipid describes a glycolipid, not the defining glycoprotein arrangement.","A core protein is central to the proteoglycan definition.","Hyaluronan is an exception that can support noncovalent aggregates rather than being covalently attached to a core protein.","The comparison distinguishes common glycoprotein oligosaccharides from the GAG-rich core-protein arrangement of proteoglycans."],"explanation":"The comparison distinguishes common glycoprotein oligosaccharides from the GAG-rich core-protein arrangement of proteoglycans. Proteoglycan means core protein plus GAG chains. Hyaluronan can support noncovalent proteoglycan aggregates.","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":19}],"kind":"Topic check · authored study aid","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-12"}],"MOL-13:guide-13":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-13","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-12","question":"A lysosomal enzyme cannot remove one required group during GAG degradation. What is the expected biochemical tendency?","options":["Partially degraded substrates accumulate upstream of the blocked step","All GAG synthesis immediately becomes impossible","The missing activity is automatically supplied by DNA polymerase","Every affected cell must secrete normal GAG-free lysosomes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sequential degradation requires each relevant hydrolase or sulfatase. A deficient step causes incomplete breakdown and accumulation, explaining the storage principle of mucopolysaccharidoses.","reasons":["Sequential degradation requires each relevant hydrolase or sulfatase. A deficient step causes incomplete breakdown and accumulation, explaining the storage principle of mucopolysaccharidoses.","A degradation defect does not inherently abolish synthesis of all GAGs.","DNA polymerase copies DNA and cannot replace a missing GAG hydrolase.","The expected lesion is intracellular storage, not obligatory secretion of normal empty organelles."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":21}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-13"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-134","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:0","question":"Hunter and Hurler syndromes are primarily disorders of which process?","options":["Lysosomal degradation of GAGs","Only secretion of hyaluronan","Universal absence of GAG synthesis","Only formation of glycogen branches"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Specific lysosomal enzyme deficiencies lead to incompletely degraded GAG accumulation.","reasons":["Specific lysosomal enzyme deficiencies lead to incompletely degraded GAG accumulation.","These disorders involve lysosomal degradation of specific GAG substrates.","The problem is degradation, not complete failure to synthesize GAGs.","Glycogen branching is not the defining GAG-storage defect."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":135}],"originalQuestion":"Hunter and Hurler syndromes are examples of mucopolysaccharidosis that is caused by: A) Defective secretion of hyaluronic acid B) Deficiency of chondroitin sulphate C) Defective synthesis of GAGs D) Lysosomal hydrolase deficiency Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Edited from the supplied source into a clear single-best-answer study item. Wording and options were standardized or completed; explanations and distractor rationales are authored. The source excerpt is retained.","sourceExcerpt":"Hunter and Hurler syndromes are examples of mucopolysaccharidosis that is caused by: A) Defective secretion of hyaluronic acid B) Deficiency of chondroitin sulphate C) Defective synthesis of GAGs D) Lysosomal hydrolase deficiency Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-13"}],"MOL-13:guide-14":[{"id":"expanded-application-13-14","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:expanded-13","question":"Which enzyme deficiency correctly identifies Hunter syndrome in the classic MPS comparison?","options":["Iduronate-2-sulfatase","Alpha-L-iduronidase","RNA polymerase II","Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hunter syndrome is MPS II, associated with iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency and X-linked inheritance. Alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency instead defines MPS I, including the Hurler/Scheie spectrum.","reasons":["Hunter syndrome is MPS II, associated with iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency and X-linked inheritance. Alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency instead defines MPS I, including the Hurler/Scheie spectrum.","Alpha-L-iduronidase is the MPS I enzyme rather than the MPS II enzyme.","RNA polymerase II transcribes nuclear genes and is not the GAG-degrading sulfatase.","tRNA synthetases support translation and do not define this lysosomal degradation disorder."],"kind":"Lecture application · authored explanation","references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":24}],"topicId":"MOL-13:guide-14"},{"id":"reviewed-mol-past-book-123","lectureIds":["MOL-13"],"concept":"MOL-13:0","question":"Alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency in Hurler syndrome causes accumulation of which GAGs?","options":["Only hyaluronan because it has no sulfate","Only lactose","Only glycogen","Heparan sulfate and dermatan sulfate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"These GAGs require alpha-L-iduronidase during lysosomal degradation.","reasons":["This is not the characteristic accumulated GAG pair in Hurler syndrome.","Lactose is a dietary disaccharide, not the stored GAG pair.","Glycogen is not a GAG and belongs to different storage disorders.","These GAGs require alpha-L-iduronidase during lysosomal degradation."],"kind":"Past paper · adapted","references":[{"source":"mol-past","page":129}],"originalQuestion":"8. Which of the following carbons determine L enantiomer configuration and ketose sugar classification? A) 1 and 5 B) 2 and 5 C) 1 and 2 D) 2 and 4 Answer: ","adaptationNote":"Completed the source’s partly missing options and included both principal accumulated GAGs.","sourceExcerpt":"8. Which of the following carbons determine L enantiomer configuration and ketose sugar classification? A) 1 and 5 B) 2 and 5 C) 1 and 2 D) 2 and 4 Answer: ","originalQuestionLabel":"Original source excerpt (may include adjacent items)","topicId":"MOL-13:guide-14"}]};
